      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NI:LLIE, I notice that in evening dress the softness of lace and chiffon is now preferred to the hard glitter of the sequin, and now that the latter has begun to riot in colour, we may look for its speedy disappear- ance from the toilettes of the well-dressed. Three evening blousen have just Peen are all suitable for New Year parties. The first is in tucked silk. pale green in colour. The fronts a:e bordered with lace, which is continued round the shoulders like a collar. A vest of finest, silkiest white muslin is laced across with nairow black velvet. The etc and blouse is made of white chiffon, and takes tho form of an ever and aide bodice, the former bor- dered with a cascade of coffee-coloured lace. A band made of very tiny pangs and tucks finishes the bodice front and back, erossing the shoulders, where it forme the upper band of the little puffed sleeves. There is also a lower band of the same trimming, finished with a frill of laoo. The two rosettes are made of lace. The waistband is folded black satin. The third blouvte is made of figured black silk, and can be worn in mouruirg.</p>
        <p>There arc a number of pretty little coats for little girls to b 3 noticed in the shops and worn by little children jast now. They should be warm for present wear and wherever possible the collars should be made to turn up to a reasonable height so as to protect the wearer from cold. One I saw recently was of velvet of a blue shade. The edge of the collar had a row of warm fur around it. It was made in such a simple fashicn that anyone could not help but admire The little girl had a tam-o-shanter pattern velvet hat with a feather stuck in the side and fastened with an imitation jewelled buckle.</p>
        <p>Everywhere fur toques are to be met with, and It seems that they are having a good run this win- ter. Many of them are extremely pretty and be- coming as well as comfortable. Those of very cheap quality should be avoided, however, as a toque made of a skin that obviouely once belonged to a yellow cat is anything hub stylish or beauti- ful. Some of the most charming of these furry oats are of chinehilla. They consist of just a low soft crown and a crumpled brim caught up in front or at the side with either a cluoter of flowers —an incongruous mixture considered chic at the moment—or a jaunty arrangement of ribbon loops. Violets or mauve primulaa look charming with chinchilla, and so do pale yellow wallflowers or owslips. Sable toques are most effectively trimmed with knots of bright cerise of tomato-red ribbon, while those of grebe may be successfully adorned with either chestnut-brown or turquoise-blue. Toques composed entirely of sealskin are not at all general this winter, but some with crowns of velvet, panne, or stitched cloth and seal brims are to be seen, and are both serviceable and smart. The far brim ie sat and folded loosely, and there is not infrequently a e,ookade of the crown mate- rial at the eide with a cluster ef fliwers or a twist of bright rcd or cerise satin beneee.h the brim. These under trimmings, however,- rnuat be quite small to be in accordance with the latest edict of fashion.</p>
        <p>There is no end to the variety and baauty of evening capes and wraps. One I have is of Oriental velvet, that artistic variety with amber ground and delightfully harmonising colours of many shades, forming the pattern. This is lined with white satin, and is daintily trimmed with a foamy ruff collar and frou-frou of cream chiffon and full flounce of the same edging the bottom of the cape, while half-way up an insertion of white satin about five indica deep i covered with cream lace. The magpie combination continues as popular as ever.</p>
        <p>It is very evident that long loosely-fitting three- quarter coats are becoming more and more worn, but they will doubtless give way to their old rival, the short coatee, as warmer weather comes. These latter prevail in f-ar or velvet. Cloth capes are shelved in favour of their fur brethren ; a short clott cape is absolutely &amp;mode; now. Black or dark brown pelisses and coats are far more worn than light brown shades.</p>
        <p>Your loving JIINNIIII.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRiTIOUS.</p>
