        <p>":WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Duke of Connaught'a c.euntry residence, Be gehot Park,near Windsor, which will be closed during the rest- deuce of his Royal Highnes; and the Duchees in Irelend, is not at all imposing, or euggestive of a Royal dwellino„. It is a long, low, comfortable, homely place, almost covered in summer, with trailing roecs and creepers. Bagehot belongs to the Crown, and was granted by the Queen to the Duke for his life when he merried bonny Princess Margaret, twenty-one years ago. Claremont House, &amp;her. is yet another Royal resi- dence rebieh will be closed for wen() time to come. Clare- mont was settled upon the Duchess of Albany by her Majesty some sixteen years ago, and she will in all pro- bability return there again when the Duke attains his majority. There are already rumours of a brilliant marriage for Princess Alice, who will be seventeen next month, but at preset* these need confirmation. Princess Alice is a very faseinsainsr, clever girl. She can scarcely be termed beautiful. bait like her cousin, Princess Mar- garet or Connaught, she possesses a very sweet and in- telligent face. The war has given point to the protest that there ought to be no death duties on the estates of soldiers an milers who are killed while on active service. The late General Symons was, for instance, by no means wealthy and it seems hard that the Treasury should corns down and take tithe of the estate of one who has so heroi- cally died in the service of his country. The late Sir William Symons, like so many other generals in the field had no heir to follow him. Like the gallant General Wauchope, ho was childless, though bath leave widows lecrd Roberts has also now no son, and General Buller, like Lord Wellesley, has but one child—a dau .later— while Lord Kitchener is unmarried.</p>
        <p>Sir William MacCormac rereads v.ry favourably upon the hospital arrangements for our wounded soldiers in Semite Africa. It is at least one feature of the war which e° erne to have been moot carefully thought out, and thanks to the best medical treatment and attention, an the more humane character of the injuries inflietool by theiMauser guns used by the Boers, the p-oportien of complete recoveries is exceedingly high. These in fact include !nee shot through the lungs, or other parts, to which injury used to always prove fatal, but which are now considered anything but hopeless, and are very often successfully treated. The region of the heart iv the part which remaies mortally vulnerable, but the majority of the wourads its war occur in the extremities, sod it apre ars tlsat aftor these about half or two-thirds of the patients are back again in the ranks within a fortnight or three weke. If the heart were protected it would add vary largely to the soldiers' security, and this has revived the eugees- don that it might be possible to contrive 1-mall breast- plate which could be worn without undue eneumbrance and answer this purpose. The war has created considerable interest in South African geography which has also become a very popular subject in the schools. There is nothing like a war to teach people geography, and that, as the la:e Mr Join Bright 'aid with truth, is the enly useful purpost. eshith war promotes. Another result of the war, as it eftfente chit:teen, has been, it seems, to cause a great run on the leaden soldiers in the toy shops. These, like most other toys, are nearly all " made in Germany " but so great has been the demand that the sources of supply has, given out. A similar scarcity occurred at the time at the Franco-Prussian War. Toy dealers, lik the military authorities at the Cape, are anxiously looking out for reinforcements, which will no &amp;Allot soon notne al .eg. The Bishop of London's remarks at the Imperial Institute the other day, were net vette flittering to the British boy whom be desc-ihed as despising knowleelge, determined net to bare, and hating his teacher. A girl on the other hand se.erned to him to be alweys desirous of learning, and to have eornething like entluei,,sm for her teacher. The Bishop has boys of his own, so he ought to know, but the majorito of people would say that the description is rather tura upon boys in general. Perhaps the Bishop was joking, a thing net at ether foreign to his nature, and his cleelng remarks (-ma t show that everything he said should, not he taken an pied de in latre. If however, his lordshie's description is exact, it is evident that there must be a great nember of exceptions to the rule. There are, no doubt, a -eery large number of laelsto whom the acquisition of knowledge from beoks is pain- ful labour, who cannot learn as ether boys do, hut the average bey takes his lessons very much as a matter of course, in the same way thot he puts on his boots end his stockings, without thinking very much whether he likes the lessens or not. Of course it dente (Is a good deal upon the teacher, but it would net be a very rash</p>
      </div>
