        <p>thing to say tnat there is mare play at the Universities, than at the secondary schools. Every oman must wish, just now, to Drove her patriosism, and there is one way in which she ce.n do it. by purchasing w.ly British or Colonial goods. If it be- came the fashion to a.k the shopkeeper concerning every Article, "Is it British ?" we should find a more civil tone adopted by 011ie Continental nations, who, if all the women in these Isles of ours were unanimous in refusing their goads, would soon become as obseqnious as they are now insolent. We owe it not only to ourselves to resent the dastardly breaches of neutrality, but to these eallant fellows in South Africa,who are fighting against the rascality of half the capitals of Europe. " Picture " hats are not much en evidence this winter, and the few that are to he seen are very funereal ot aspect, ,-eing as a rule, built entirely in black,with huge waving plumes. But as spring advances they will probably regain their old place in our affections, being far too becoming to the majority to go the way of most modes.</p>
        <p>The bolero, far from disappearing, ig in greater favour th..n efer. Almost cvery smart bodice hts a bolero,real or simulated. For wearing with those, is aa infinite variety of fronts laid out on counters, and in shop windr)ws, to tempt the m9vey mut of our pockets. Some are in striped silks, some is lace, while others, and perhaps the most fascioating, arc, built of 'chiffon loosely urawa. and held in position by small crosswey bands of fur. Trawso cLri all be easily manufactured at home, by the nimble fingered. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A case which was before the London Police Courts recently tarnishes an example of the expedients which are tried by people who seek to obtain a livelihood by dishonest means. Two men were convicted at the Guildhall of picking pockets,and it was stated that both telong,ed to a ang of thicvas wh dressed frequently as clergymen ! If the men do not proclaim their cccupa- tion by their tacos, and are careful to hold their tongues, it is easy to see that the disgui:ie must be very service- able to them. In an omnibus, or train, for example, seated next to a man in clerical drP4s the last thing that would occar to the majority of us would be, that he was desirous of picking our pocket. " MADGE."</p>
