      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</p>
        <p>• BY ESME STUART, Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable," "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CHAPTER XX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XX.</p>
        <p>When he left her he went to his own study, and from the depth of a case he took out some old letters.</p>
        <p>• "How strange," he thought, "if she should be the daughter of my old chum. I would gladly repay him all his kindness. But he was always most strange. It would be like him to shut himself up in an old house and to see no one, but I cannot understand this persecution of such a child. Ruby is very like him; I could not re- member where I had seen that face. I have been the means of saving her from terrible misery. A marriage with this Felton could not have been worse, poor child." He took out a letter and read part of it. "I am determined to go where no white man has been. I find that man, in his civilised state, is a being not to be endured. There are regions in this continent where only the savage can be found, and you, Arthur, being a much younger man, will come some day to think as I do. We find greed, envy, evil-speaking among our own set, what can we find worse among savages? You may not hear from me for years, but remember that I made a friend of you. You were always grateful, and you were above all meanness. YOU never stooped to deceit. Good-bye, Arthur. You and I met by chance, we part by chance, but I shall think of you as the man who nearest came up to my ideal of an English gentleman." , "I should like to send this to Ruby's father, and see if he is, indeed, my old friend. It may be another of the same name, but it is strange that this child came'from Australia."</p>
        <p>So thinking, D\r. Murray put away the letter and went out to see the old lady he had mentioned to Ruby, to see if he could persuade her to take the girl, at least for a little time. She was a distant connection of his. She never went out and never received visitors, so that Ruby would be out of the way of any lovers; but she would certainly be very dull and the old lady was very eccentric.</p>
        <p>When Mrs. Murray returned she found Ruby much more cheerful. The girl told her, with a smile on her face, that Dr. Murray said she might be called Miss Halkett.</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. Murray smiled. "Well, dear, it is certainly more convenient to be called something. Miss Ruby Halkett is a very nice name. What has Arthur been saying to you to make you look more cheerful ? " "He says that he is going to ask an old lady if I may read to her." Mrs. Murray and her daughter smiled at each other when Ruby left the room. "Arthur thinks he can get over anyone." "He usually can, mother," said Jessie. "Then he never , asks anything for himself. He is strangely interested in this poor girl. I sometimes wonder if her story is true, or if we are being taken in." Jessie Murray was forty-five and had often been taken in. "I am sure that it is true. She does not look like a designing girl," said Mrs. Murray. "If old Mrs. Vernon will take her we can go away as we intended, and I think she will be so dull there that she will want to return home." "If Mrs. Verdon gets fond of her she will not let her go. But we can but try, and if she is not happy when we return we must try something else." "Arthur will never consent to losing sight of her." That evening Dr. Murray came in to tell Ruby that he had succeeded in his mission, and that Mrs. Vernon would like to see her. She was a very rich and very eccentric lady who had had many companions, and had found some fault with them all. She was just now in want of a young lady to read to her, but she wished to see her first." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Miss Murray offered to conduct Ruby to her, and the next day they went to 99, Eaton-square. Ruby had never been in a house so full of beautiful things before, but when she saw Mrs. Vernon she saw before her a very simply-dressed old lady. "So you are the young lady in question, eh ? " "Yes," said Ruby, "I want to earn my living and I know nothing, but I can read aloud." "Oh, you know nothing! The young people I have had always• tell me they know everything. How old are you and how much money do you want ? "</p>
        <p>Ruby looked at Miss Murray. "I am only seventeen, and I don't want any money but just enough to buy my clothes, please, and pay a few debts." "Well, you are not grasping, at all events. I don't want to give much, eh, Jessie Murray? How little can a girl dress on ? " "Miss Halkett has had very little experience, so we must leave it to you to see if she suits you." "Very well, that will be best; then I shan't be robbed as I have been so often. Come quite close, child, I am not quite blind, though people say so." Ruby knelt down by her side. "Oh, you are rather too pretty. You will wane excitement." _</p>
        <p>"Oh, no no," said Ruby, quickly, "I don't want to amuse myself, only just to earn my living." "What is your father ? ' "Papa wanted me to marry someone that I did not like, so I left home," said Ruby, simply. "But I shall be so grateful if you will keep me." "References, Jessie? ' "I have none," said Ruby, quickly. "Nobody knows me but Mrs. and Miss Murray, who have been very kind to me. I don't know whet I shall do.</p>
        <p>Ruby spoke so simply that tho old lady looked up. "Eh, no references! But they usually tell lies. As well have the lies alone as to have them certified, eh, Jessie ? " "If you will try Miss Halkett till we return, then-" _</p>
        <p>"Yes, I'll try her, and if she is unkind to the dogs or forgets the cats she must go at a minute's notice." _</p>
        <p>"Oh, thank you," exclaimed Ruby. "You may leave her, I want someone at once. But where is her luggage ? " "I have none," said Ruby. "I will arrange to send her some things," said Miss Murray, quickly. "Queer! Well, I want someone now, so I may as well try her, but I warn you, miss--" "Oh, call me Ruby, please, and I will do any- thing you like." "Well, that's settled, and I consider that I have got the article cheap." Miss Murray said good-bye to Ruby, who could not help looking sad at losing her one friend. "I will tell my mother and brother that you had to stay and we will come and sec you as soon as we can. We are all going on a visit to an old friend in the country. Good-bye. Ruby. Don't say thank you till we come back." Poor Ruby did indeed feel sad as the door closed on Miss Murray, but the old lady left her no time to brood. She made her go round the room and learn all the things she might want, and read the names of the books. "I expect you to read a great deal. lam very fond of books; they do not disappoint one as people do. Now come back and let me talk to you. Ruby obeyed, feeling very much afraid of the sharp old lady. "You have quarrelled with your father. That's not pretty. I daresay you are wrong. I dare- say he wanted you to marry a proper young man, and you had another lover. Probably you are very much to blame, but it's no business of mine. I have got you cheap, and cheap things are always nasty." "I don't think I can be very nice, or papa would not have wanted to get rid of me. I thought he loved me," said Ruby, not seeing the smile on the old lady's face. "Love is all nonsense! You disobeyed your father." "Never before. He has been very good to me, but except about this, and he said he could not help it." "It was your fault, lam sure. Now see that Pompey has his tea, and let Bella out of the door. Ring the bell and tell the man-servant that he is to let Bella have some cream to-day in your honour." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Ruby obeyed. Then she.,was told to pour out tea and to read a boot 410ud. Ruby did all she was bid very patiently. The book was a history book. She knew nothing about it, but it interested her. _</p>
        <p>"So _you have never read the history of the Thirty Years' War? You are very ignorant. I shall have you taught. I don't want you in the morning till eleven o'clock. You shall have -an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CHAPTER XXI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>Whilst Ruby was safe with the Good Samaritant; who had not waited to ask who was their neigh- bour, Alec was under the care of Bob. The food and the brandy had wonderfully restored him, hut it would have been impossible for him to have escaped in the condition he then was. He lay on the bed wrapped up in blankets, and Bob, who had to leave him, promised to return as late as he could so as not to be heard and to sleep in the MOM.</p>
        <p>Bob could hardly believe his own senses now, for he expected Mr. Alec to be spirited away from under his very eyes. On his second return he found the/patient better. "There is nothing to do but to cut and run, Bob, as you once suggested to me." "Yes, ]!iir. Alec, if you can't see the enemy's face better ahew him your back." "If only I could see him or catch him! " "Slippery as eels, Mr. Alec; father's like that! It's as much as I can do to give him the slip when he has me, and then as likely as not [leave summat in his hands."</p>
        <p>"Bob, you were born honest and cute. Very uncommon! Look here, can I get to the station to-morrow by the early train?" "Well do our best, Mr. Alec, but it's not easy. If the squire is coming back Rudd will be all agog, And he'll be wanting me, but never fear, I'll manage somehow." Alec smiled.</p>
        <p>"Where there's a will! The one thing which comforts me is the thought that Miss Ruby got that telegram." "She cut and run there, I'm sure she did," said Bob. "I feel another man now that I think that she has escaped from the Feltons." "She is a rare one, but she thought everybody good till she got to know Mr. James!" Alec did •not say more to Bob-, but his whole mind was filled with gratitude. At least the girl he loved had not meekly given in. His words had borne fruit, and she had been able to realise that no man has a right to sell his daughter as her father had wished to do.</p>
        <p>"Look here, Mr. Alec, we must not Le seen leaving this place together. I'll go early and just tell mother. She is a one .for to help anybody. If you puts your watch right, then it will be better for you to get up and start on the sly. I'll leave the garden door open, and you nkust try to lock this door so that Nancy can't get in. You must make it look sort of tidy. But lor, there's Jane; she be a stupid as . ever was. I'll try to bring the cart by the lower road. • There aren't many people as goes by there early." The nest morning, before there was anyone stirring, Alec Beauworth might have been seen stealing out of the house which Sir Harry had told him was to be his in the future., ;It was, indeed, a poor specimen of the well-grown English- man who went slowly and painfully across the park. The key of his room was in his pocket, so that he felt that even if he were discovered it would not be before he had given much trouble in causing the lock to be orced. He thought over his wonderful escape, and took courage. Surely God had not forsaken him now, when he had been preserved from what looked like certain death. He seemed years older, and Ruby would hardly have recognised her cousin and the man who had taught her what love may mean, in the feeble individual who slunk away from the sight of the early ploughman. He felt sure that he could not walk all the way, and only prayed that Bob might be able to keep his word. Otherwise, he ran great risks of being discovered. It was cold, and a slight mist was over all the country; but there were signs that ii was going to be a fine day. It was a good omen, he thought.., Why had he not taken Bob's advice and disappeared long ago? But in that case he would not have been able to save Ruby. Was she safe, would he find her with Mrs. Scott when he reached town? At last he heard wheels behind him. He turned aside into the copse wood, foz fear it should be someone who would stare at,him, as his toilet had been anything but satisfactory. He must look a very strange character, dragging himself painfully along. "Mr. Alec," cried Bob, laughing, for his sharp eyes had discovered his friend, "you are a DOOr one at hiding!"</p>
        <p>Alec smiled too. "I fear I should shew little fight at this moment if )11r. James Felton_were.to come by."</p>
        <p>"Getup, quick, Mr. Alec. I lizi•Ve had to dab it on thick for to be let to go. I said—well, that don't matter; but I can only set you down oiltside the station. Sir Harry may come any moment; if so, there is a lot of danger in my being seen with you.,, "Look here, Bob, sooner or later they will find you out. I. want you to cut the whole affair, and to come and live with me. I shall manage to earn enough for two, and I owe you my life." "Earn for me, Mr. Alec! Well, that is a good 'un. But give me your address, and I'll promise to come to you when they make it too hot for me here. It will be safer now, too, if they don't find me out. But they'll think you mighty clever to have got out alone. I wonder if the squire knew. I don't think he did. He'd have been afraid to leave 11- place • and Mr. James, he hoped that you Iv—A die Le a rat—that's what he hoped." "What have I done to them to bring all this on me? That's what I should like to know." "The devil hates folk for the pleasure of it, mother says." At last they got as far as it was safe for Bob to come. Alec got down with difficulty. He took Bob's hand.</p>
        <p>"I didn't make a friend for nothing, Bob. Re- member, I expect you at this address as soon as you can leave safely." "But there's Miss Ruby. You'll tell her that I did my best for you," said Bob, rather shyly. "If happier days ever come, Bob, I shall-" "Hist 1 Mr. Alec. Go now; 'tain't the time for talk. You'll hear from me somehow." They parted, and Alec walked up to the station that Ruby had come to. Alec had happily not lost his purse, and though it was not well filled, still he had but to get to town. He had offered Bob a gold piece before starting, in case they did not meet• again; but Bob's face fell visibly he shook his head.</p>
        <p>Ly ; . "You're my friend, Mr. Alec—you said so." "The bargain is a bad one for you, Bob. God blQss you." Just as Alec entered the station he saw a man coming out of it who looked at him intently. Casting a glance back, he fancied he recognised him as man he had once seen talking to James Felton.</p>
        <p>There was nothing to do but to avoid a meeting, so he entered the station, got his ticket, and stayed in the waiting-room till the train should come in. He was glad to sit down, as the walk he had taken had tired him. "And this is all I have got by coming to view what might be my inheritance," thought Alec. "This is all!" But the next moment he smiled. "No, it is not all. I have seen Ruby, and I love her better than life. It has all been worth it. If only I can protect her." The train came up, Alec hurried into it, then he saw the man he had met come out and watch him into his carriage. Now they were off he was safe, at least he was safe for the present; but if this man had anything to do with the squire, it might be the worse for Ruby. Of course, Sir Harry would come to his lodgings when his daughter's disappearance was discovered. Had Sir Harry anything to do with the shameful treatment he had met with i Alec clenched his hand, feeling very sore at the bare idea that it had all been a plant to lure him to his destruction. Still, do what he might, he could not unravel all the tangled threads. He had been living in lodgings, interfering with no one, devoted to his profession, and entertaining no false hopes of inheriting an estate belonging to a man who was not old and who any day might marry. If ha had been wanted to fall in love with Ruby, certainly he had easily fallen into that trap; but if the result of this had been very advantageous to the squire, Alec might have understood the temptation to put him quietly out of the way. The papers he had signed were waste paper if the squire married again, which he might do any day. If, on the other hand, Sir Harry had been for some reason indebted to the Feltons, why did he run the risk, which proved a real one, of throwing him into the company of Ruby, when it would have been for the benefit of t 2,11 concerned that nay</p>
        <p>•should• not see anyone except the man to whom her father wished to sacrifice her. _</p>
        <p>Again and again Alec went over all these reason- ings, but be could find no clue, could think of no light strong enough to pierce the mystery. WhY had his death been, as he might almost say, decided upon when neither his life nor his death could materially benefit Sir Harry himself? When he reached the terminus he stepped into a hansom, having no luggage to trouble him, though he thought regretfully of his best suits. He would certainly not claim anything, for his safest course would now be to efface himself. He gave the address to the cabman, and felt as if he had come out of a terrible dream. _</p>
        <p>"Shall I ring, sir ? " said the cabman, but Alec, putting his hand into his pocket, touched the old familiar key, and shook his head. He paid his fare and dismissed the man. When he entered, he was surprised to see his landlady running up the back stairs, looking as if she had seen a ghost. "Lawk a mercy, Mr. Beauworth, whstever have you been doing? You here! Why, I had a tele- gram from you saying you was called away to foreign parts, and that you would not come back yet awhile. " "From me!"</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, I had three of 'em, and I said to Mr. Smith, says I, it's the queerest thing for Mr. Beauworth to go off to foreign parts, and never to come and say good-bye; and your things was to be sent to the station, and the rest to wait till you wrote."</p>
        <p>"Let me see the telegram," said Alec, so afraid of sinking deeper into the pit that had been pre- pared, that he determined to be very cautious and to say nothing. Mrs. Smith brought the yellow papers. "I see here that the bill was asked for. Was it paid ? " "Yes, sir; I had a postal order for all the amount—but, of course, it did not pay for all the worry. Very ungrateful I thought it was of you, and if you wish to blame me--" "I don't wish to blame you, Mrs. Smith, only I wish to get to my room and have a good wash." "Lawk a mercy! Mr. Beauworth. Why,, seeing you had been like that to us, we have relet, ana there is a young man and his wife in your room— s good let it is." Already 1 " "Well, yes, sir; you see we was a bit hurt with you, and we thought you must be flying from debt for to be in such a hurry to leave the country; so, says we—" "Never mind, Mrs. Smith, I think you, might have trusted me better, haying known me so long; you ought to have known that I should not treat you badly." _ _ _ "Well, I'm sure, we didn't mean no harm, and we are poor people. But you do look ill, Mr. Beauworth." _ _</p>
        <p>Alec felt as if ho were, indeed, a homeless wanderer now. His place seemed to know him no more. At least, there was one who was waiting for him. He would go and find Ruby. He did not even care to ,read the telegrams, but he cast his .eyes over them. They were as Mrs. Smith had said, except that the last one said: "Keep lodgings till you hear again from me." This it was that had made Mrs. Smith rather afraid to see him! • "You have not even done as I said in these. I have been ill, but even according to these, you onzht not to have let again. Good‘day, Mrs. Smith." Airs. Smith protested, and even wept, for she had already found that the new lady gave a great deal of trouble. [ideed,</p>
        <p>"I'll give them warning, Mr. Beauworth—ii: I will."</p>
        <p>_ "No, thank you; as you have been paid,. I'll find other lodgings." He walked away, followed by Mrs. Smith's lamentations; but in his heart Alec was rather glad that this had happened. He would now be able to go and leave no trace of himself, the Feltons could not follow him—that is, if they had not gone abroad, for no reliance was to be placed on their word. Still, he felt terribly done up, and unable to hunt for lodgings. He called another cab, feeling as if misfortune would follow him, and that he was in a cycle of dangers which must soon overwhelm him. He got into a cab, and told the man to drive to the address where lived his friend, Mrs. Scott. He must get some food and rest before he hunted for lodgings. What would he say? He could not incriminate Ruby's father. It was safer for the present to make no fuss. "Is Mrs. Scott at home ? " Alec felt that he should also find her removed to the Antipodes. The maid, who knew him, stared a little at the strange appearance of Mr. Alec. He looked as if he had come out of the back woods.</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir." He went up, and Mrs. Scott—a bright, kind woman of forty—started up as she heard his name. "Well, Alec, here you are, and where is your young lady, and what do you mean—" "Isn't she here ? " gasped Alec, sinking into a chair. _ -</p>
        <p>"Here! No, I have been expecting her ever so long, and you might have written--" "Indeed, I—l have been ill." "Ill! You look as if you had been buried and dug up again. Pray explain all this mystery." She isn't here? She went from Yewdale, and she had your address. Good heavens! That man has got hold of her again. I must go —I must." "You can't go anywhere, or do anything, till you have had some food. My poor Alec, have you been at death's door, and what is all this mystery about? You should not mix yourself up with these intrigues. I wired that I would have her for your sake; but feel a little relieved that—" "Indeed, it is all terrible. I can only tell you the outlines; but about Ruby I will tell you all. She was going to be immediately married to a scoundrel, a man unworthy to touch her hand, and her wretched father was heartlessly sacrificing her to—"</p>
        <p>"Oh, you are deeply in love, and have lost your head. My dear Alec, I thought you were a man of sense; I have never known you so foolish. I should think you will have brought yourself within the pale of the law, if you help girls to run away," "But where is she ? " "I hope sincerely that she has gone home again. Perhaps she thought that falling in love with a penniless architect was not shewing much worldly wisdom."</p>
        <p>"Indeed, you know nothing about the case. Ruby is an angel surrounded by devils called men." "All young men in love think the same I But where did you leave her, and what has happened to you ? "</p>
        <p>"I have been ijj. I was at death's door, and Ruby went withoM me, and she must have lost her way What can Ido ? " "At death's door; but what with ? " "Oh, it was damp, and cold, and wet, and--" "Rheumatism? Come, did you have a doctor? Your illness must have a name.</p>
        <p>"I did not have a doctor; but, indeed, I shall soon be well. It is Ruby that matters." "My dear boy, that precious stone of yours is at this moment, I daresay, thinking that she has been very foolish, and that she is safer at home with her own father. You told me in a letter that he was devoted to her. A little disagreement between father and daughter about a lover is soon made up. Besides, what are you to do with a young lady on your hands ? " "I thought that you would have taken care of her for me." "And the father would have come and demanded his daughter! A nice mess you would have got into!"</p>
        <p>—Put in this matter-of-fact manner, Alec saw that he could only have saved Ruby for a very short time; but where was she now? What was be to do? He felt terribly weak and unable to think,</p>
        <p>"I must go and look for some lodgings." "Why, you were so happy with Mrs. Smith!" "She has let my rooms." "What extraordinary things have happened to you, my dear Alec." "Yes, I suppose so; but people's lodgings have been let over their heads before now. I don't see why that is extraordinary!" "I see that you must have a good rest. My spare room is empty, and you had better go to bed and have a sound sleep. It is of no use saying at this early hour: Are you hungry? ' " "I think that would do me the most good,* said Alec, smiling to himself. "Oh, I thought that you would say that you could not touch food! Lunch will be ready in half-an-hour, and then, perhaps, you will make yourself respectable. Being an old friend, Alec, I don't mind saying that you look as if-" "Never mind what I look like. Put on your lunch, and I will make myself spic and span after- wards."</p>
        <p>When they were at lunch Mrs. Scott was so much mystified that every now and then she looked at her visitor expecting some signs of madness.</p>
        <p>"You eat an if you had not-- By the way, after 41.2 ilineip,__Adec, you .nhou4l be eiaaxilig.."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>"1 nuns tnat my umess is one that requirea building up,- said Alec, with a faint smile. What was the 11A9 of telling the truth? Mrs. Scott would think him stark crazy, or apply at once to a lawyer, and in that case all hopes of rescuing Ruby would be at an end. Fearful visions of Sit Harry haunted him. He had no one to -help him but an innkeeper's son, whose father was one of the worst characters.</p>
        <p>He was, however, grateful to Mrs. Scott for all her kindness, and, torn with doubts and anxiety., there was nothing to be done but to accept the hospitality and to try and get strong. He dared not telegraph to Bob, and yet he was the only person who could tell him if, by any chance, Ruby had returned home. What should he do, how should he proceed, where Virbfi Sir 'Jerry? Had he returned home to find—if he even knew it—that the prisoner had escaped? He could not think even now of his fearful struggle with death without a shudder. lie was saved, but only by the skin of his teeth, and was all danger over? Those who so desired his death, would they not pursue him till they had accomplished their object? How was he to escape? At all events, for the present he was safe; but Sir Harry knew the firm of architects he worked for, and it would be the easiest thing for him to find out where he lodged. In some way or other, he must bury himself in London, and Five his address to no one.</p>
        <p>(To be antinued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ARE YOU ALL RIGHT?</head>
        <p>ARE YOU ALL RIGHT?</p>
        <p>" How are. you Mb; morning? " sivs your friend 'Oh, I'm all rigbt, thanks," you answer.</p>
        <p>And by the common standard you are all right. Which ans that you are act suffering—or not much—lrom any particular ailment. You are able to be about and to do your work, whatever it may be. Nevertheless we are not quite all right, even when we ray so. There is &amp;in:moiler something amiss. Possibly the chronic trouble to which we are so accustomed that it eceres a part of our nature; the thing we never expect to he rid of; the one endless worry that we seldom talk of—f say it may be a matter of this sort, or it may be some pain or ache which We are sure will pass away in an hour or two. We try to ignore these facts, like the sensible fellows we are, and answer cheerily, " Oh, I'm all right." And how else can we speak'? We met keep all on continually hawking and huckstering our pains and ailments to others. It VYould be a nuisance to one' fritnds ; and besides, We don't want to be re- garded as such unseaworthy craft. The truth is, most of no are more or less wrong all the blessed while, carrying our hidden weaknesses and abiding complaints as we carry a male or a birthmerk—beezuse we can't help it. How merry men and women are really and truly sound aril riabt, inside and our:, from scalp to soles, for one month together? How many ? Sakes alive! Not one in forty thousand. Yet here Whir. Waldron, of Trimpley Green, who says that an occasional dose of Mother Seigel's Syrup keeps him all right. Can it be true? Yes, from his point of view. Remember, th^re is no such thing as an absolutely perfect physical condition. We are obliged to give ourselves odds. Bearing that fact in mind. I say, Mr. Waldron states the case so that we can accept it as a good working bypothc:l-.15. For vnry many years," he sate forth in a letter written last winter, " I was nverely troubled with in- digestion, baving,as you may say, continually, a feeling of lassitude and weakness. My appetite was not very good, and no kind of food actually agreed with me." [No marvel about that. Dc, the best of coals "agree' with a badly made grate, and a chimney that won't draw ? The stomach is a stove, and digestion is cook- ing.] "After meals," goes on Mr. Waldron, " I was-apt to be sick and had a drial of pain at the chest, and a sensation in the stomach as if living vermin were gnaw- ing rue there. No matter how light or nutritious the things I ate, I always felt uacomfortable and uneasy atterwards.</p>
        <p>"1 did not rest well at night, and commonly arose in the merning tired and, unrefreshed. Bad and de- pressing as all this was, I struggled on with my work— yet did a deal of it in pain that by rights ought te hare laid mo up. " This went on, as I have told you, for years. I gat bottles of medicine Train chemists at,d consulted doctors but no good came of any of them ; that is, nothing that lasted and stood by me. I might be a bit better for a few days, but was sure to be bad again. "it was in the winter of 1892 that I got very low and weak. I was fairly frightened with the Idea that I was in danger uf breaking down altogether. I didn't know what te do, and the futuru took on a rather black look.</p>
        <p>" Happily, I received a book relating cases like my own which had beea cured by Mother Seigel's Syrup. I got a bottle from Mr. H. Roberts, Kidderminster, and it did me a lot of good. And, in few words, I continuid using the Syrup until it made me sound and strong. My pain vanished with the indigestion. Since then an ccca- Cone. do:e has kept me all right."— T. Waldron, Holly Line Cottage,Trimpley Green, near Bawdley, Worcester. shire, January 19, 1899. Ideal health may be a rarity for a thousand reasons, but it any remedy can make and keep us " all right," the chances are that the name of that remedy is Mother Seigel's Syrup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Late Mr. John Hartley..—Funerai.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. John Hartley..—Funerai.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. J•ohn Hartley, of Latch ford ,were interred in the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. Shortly after one o'clock the mourners and a large number of the deceased's friends, including the Mayor and Mayorees (Alder- man and Mrs Roberts), Councillor Ellison, and Mr W H J Jenkins (solicitor), attended the service in the Wesleyan Church,of which Mr Hartley had been a member since it was built. The service was of a most impressive character and was conducted, in accordenee with the deceased's wish, by the Rem T. Bishop end H. G. Roberts, assisted by the Rev. J. Guni;ell. The members of the choir were in attend- ance, and the hymns" Rock of Ages " and " A few more years shall roll" were sung. The Rev. J. Gun- nell delivered an address in which he referred to Mr. Hartley's methodical life, he having made his funeral arrangements two or three hours before his death. He thought that it was very appropriate that they should bold such a service is the chapel which he had helped to erect and in which he took so deep an interest. There was nothing complex in his character and there was no mystery about his life. It was simple, genuine,un- ostentatiens, and deeply religious. They all sym- pathised with the bereaved widow and daughter,and they would commend them to God's keeping. At the conclusion of the service Mr. Ford, who pre- sided at the organ, played the Dead March in "Saul." The cortege then left for the Cemetery, where,notwithstanding the extremely cold weather, a large number of the deceased's late friends had assembled. The chief mourners were---Mrs Hartley (widow), Miss Hartley (daughter), Mr George Hart- ley (brother), Mr J B Isaan, Mrs Looker and Mrs LOlna7 r;icars), Messrs Looker, Perkins, Henshell, Taylor, 'Silcock, Bernard, D Jones, and Ridgway. Th-o ,erv.ce at the graveside was conducted by the Eevs 1.1 Bishop and H G Roberts. A large number of floral tributes had been received from the em- plo3 is and staff of Messrs George Artingstall and Co., Limited (of which firm deceased was a partner), Mrs Deacon, Mr and Mrs Henshall, Miss Atkinson, Mr and Mrs D Jones, Mr and Mrs Looker, Mrs Lister Janet, Mr E Jones, and others. MOURNING.—W. T. WILLIAMS makes a Speciality of the Moerning Pepartraent : Choice Selection ef Materials always in S'ock. Smart Dressmaking; Charges moderate. 74. SANKEY-STREET. Warrington.</p>
        <p>Married Ladies. FREE. maumm. Information of Priceless value FREE on receipt of stamped envelope and address. TESTIMONIALS DAILY..</p>
        <p>Mits.F :ii. AELEIGM, (Box 63,) 16t) RAORNRY ROAD. LONDON. N.B</p>
