      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SOLDIER'S FATE.</head>
        <p>A SOLDIER'S FATE.</p>
        <p>Interesting Disclosures.</p>
        <p>Ex-sergeant Harry Co liTes, late of the 9th Lancers, is at Home at 14,Alswitha-torrace,Elyde-street,Winche6ter It was the " South Manchester Chroniole " which ob- tained his deeply interesting story of War and Peace. Collins,who is forty-three years of age, was ensconced in a cosy corner of his nest little howie, surrounded by his children, and looking the very picture of health and happiness' " Some account of my experiences ?" he commenced. "Well, I served for twenty-one years in the 9th Lancer', and left that regiment as a sergeant fit for a good many years, further service. During my service I was for three years in Afebanistan. UNDER LORD ROBERTS, and was awarded three medals. Immediately after leavieg the Army I joined the Corps of Cemmissioneires, acd was employed keeping the door of a large establish- ment. I was on door duty for about three years, and during that time I was contracting one cold on top of another, until I got 53 bad that I had to be removed to work inside the premises. I had begun to spit ohlesen with streaks of blood. I If.ist my appetite, and I got so ' weak that I coeld only walk slowly. I lost a stone in weight, and at Bight I used to awake wet with perenir- ation—a sure sign of consumption. At last I werit to see a very able doctor, who told me that I sv mild have to have a change of air and rest, or I would break up. I went to bed and called in another doctor, who said I was in the first stags of cow,umptien, and if I did not leave Manchester I would not last long. He seemed to under- stand my case so well that I thought he wan right, and I grew so weak that I did not seem to Care whether I died or not." " You tried soine remedy, I nuppoe, ?" asked the re- porter. " Yes," was Mr. Collins's reply. " Whilst I was lying in bed I saw a case mentioned in the papers of a marvellous cure effected by Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. The case in poiat was exactly similar to mine, and the symptome appeared to be the gams. I sent my wife for a box, and after having taken them I already felt slightly better. After I had taken a seeonel box I went to work again. I continued taking the nil's, and after annther eight boxes I was completely cured, and my health at present is very good. I told the elec- tor that Dr. Williams' pink pills had saved my life." Continuing, in a chatty manner, Mr. Collins said : "My father died of asthma,and that complaint seems to run in the family. I used to cough all day, but since I have fortunately taken Dr. Williams' pink pills my cold has been cured. A good many people wondered at tieing me cured so quickly, but I told them I owed it all to Dr. Williams' pink pills, and recommended them to take them." It is to their power of renleniahing the blond sepply that these pills Owe their life-saving properties in cases of consumption and bronchitis. The lungs consist of innumerable small tubas, in which the air we breathe comes in contact with the blood. If the blood is poor or deficient the lungs are weakened, and one of these tiny vessels may be ruptured. That is the beginnicg of consumption. Irritation of the air tubes is bronchitis. Rich abundant blood prevents irritation and enables the system to throw off dieease—not only chest complaints, but languor, headaches, and breathlessness. palpitations of the heart, indigestion (from inability of the blood to I absorb nourishment from food taken), nervausnese, St. Vitus' dance, fits, rheumatism, unhealthy eensitiveoess to cold, and even paralysis. Dr. Williams' pink pills act directly on the blood. Their effect is speedily seen, thin people put on flesh, rheumatism and coughs disappear. . there is new life in all the body. But substitutes will not give new blood, nor do any other good ; the genulne pills, and they alone, possess the property of making new blood with every dose; they always bear the full name—Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. If sub- stitutes are pushed, it is best to Fend direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company. Holborn Viaduct, Londen, enclosing the price—two and ninepence for cue box ; thirteen and nine for six boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Co-operative Society The annual meeting of the Co-operative Society took place on Monday evening. The President . (Mr. T. Birchen), who was in the chair, raid the past year had with them not been remarkable for ' any special development, but had bean a dead level of quiet work and substantial succees. The audi- ence would see by the balance sheet that the last quarter's sales showed an increase over the previous one of £2,953 6s. 34. He sometimes wished the members would attend these meetings in larger number, instead of showing such unbounded confidence in the Committee of Manage- ment as to leave it almost entirely in their hands. Members of the Committee had recently attended the last business meeting of the Co-operative Wholesale Society, whose sales last year amounted to thirteen millions. It was the largest commercial concern in this country,inaugu- rated and carried on by representative working men. The directors reported that the Roden estate of 75 acres, which was purchased some time ago, was very successfully utilized in the growing oi tomatoes and other vegetables much in demand in their societies, and that the large mansion was to be tranetornted into a convalescent home for those of their members who, recovering from sicknegs, needed good country air. Cottages were built for the labourers on the estate,giving three bedrooms to eaeh and good living rooms for 2s 6d per week, the minimum wages of labourers to be 184 per week. They would feel proud that their leading society was setting such an excellent example to the agri- cultural world. At the same meeting a sum of £2,000 was voted for relief to the families of those men who are fighting our battles in South Africa (Applause.) As earnest co-operators they claimed to be in the van of social progress. (Applause.)— The balance-sheet, showing sales for the quarter of £24,394 Os 8d and giving a dividend of 3e in the pound on purchases, was passed. Tbe dividend and interest for the quarter amounted to some £3,900.— The election of president was proceeded evith,when Mr Birchen was unanimously re-elected for the 33rd time in succession. Councillor Shaw Green was unanimously re-elected auditor, and Messrs R Cook, T Critehley, S Dunbo'lbin and W Jones were unanimously re-elected on the committee of:manage- ment. —A resolution of Gympathy with Mr G Molyneux. an absent member of the committee, through illness, brought the proceedings to a; close</p>
      </div>
