      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900.</p>
        <p>1900, prisener, whem they nail Tom can in, kneeked me down hit me in the face and kicked me from the bank. Af'er I was kicked I ran upstairs and prisoner followed me awl said if he hadn't finished me he would de so. I fastened the bedroem door. PHeene tried to push it onen,but I held it. I never left my bedroom till f was taken te the Infirmary where I have lain ever s;n7e. I had had no prr,vious querrel with the prisoner, and showed him no violence. Prisoeer's wife and mine had had a querrel the night previous, and prisoner's wife had brokan some vriodews. Cross-examined be prisoner. Thomas Wale)] : I sent fn• two or threes pints of beer for our wives on the previous nieht. I am not aware that thee was any bother between rue end your wife about a bey named Lennon. Your wife did not kiek me en Wednesday night, hut she dil on Thursday morning aheut the rib. I did not strike you in the face. I was knocked Fawn three or few times. Re.exanained : I know I am very ill and not likely to recover. Superintendent Monaghan said that at seven o'clock on Monley evening, January Bth, he received the prisoner into custody from the Prezcet Police. Prisoner asked him how was the " old men ?" and he replied that he lied not seen him. Prisseor geld, "Mr wife Old me about keying some bother and they would not give her her shawl. I went down to the honam of Swift on Thursday morning for the shawl and he ordered me out of the house. I hit him twice, mice on the nose and once on the jaw, and his wife assaulted me with a @hovel." When arrested he was wearing clogs. He ehere,ol him with causine grevione bodi'y harm to ome Joseph Swift at abent. 8.30 roe the morning of the fourth of January, and he replied "He ordered me nut of the helms. I hit him three times, once abltat the ribs once en the nose, and once on the jaw. I never kicl:ed a man in my life." It was about a mile from 37, Snotteryl-elarl, where the prisoner lodged to Swift's house (2, Ressell-street). Prisoner wee a Duellist and heeed at some of the travellimg, shows By trade he was a forgemen. and worked et Hallam Forge. By the Chief Constable : When arrested the prisoner nil not say enethine about his wife taking any part in</p>
        <p>tKo avilg• upin Swift. Nurcm Tl,lnt, of th in rnlr.gaiei che wig preaent wtpn Jos.nh Swift died at 5.15 on Monflay morning, January 1541. _ _</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NORTH- WICH AND WARRINGTON LIGHT RAILWAY. Public Meeting at Stockton Heath At the National Schools, Stockton Heath, on Thurs lay last week, a public meeting was held to consider the pro- posed light railway between Northwich and Werringtaa, Mr. Pearson (chairman of tie) Parish Council) presided, and amongst those present wore :—Messrs El J West. brook, J Pemberton, Mason. Whitley, dallows, Clarke. England, E J WilleCt, J W Beucuier, Rose, Tinton:ley. Mess, C W Daveapert, Miller, and others with Mr Bead- ens. w (clerk of the Parish Council). rue Chairman at the outset said they had all no doubt learned that it was proposed by a private com- pany to apply for powers to construct a light rail way from Stockton Heath to Northwich and also a railway down Grappenhall-road. The light railway was to be used for passenger and goods traffic and the lines were to run down the centre or the side of the road. The Parish Council had had this scheme before them on several occasions and had given it their careful consider- ation Tney came to the conclusion that it was their bounden duty to give notice of objection in order that the interests of the residents might be safeguarded. Ne body of intelligent business men would approve of a light railway without taking precautions to safeguard their interests: Having regard to the narrow neck of road near the Mulberry Tree it would be unwise to consent to a light railway corning down the road until the difficulty had been satisfactorily removed. A second objection was the great danger to children leaving the school and entering the high road where a railway would practically exist. In the third place no provision was made in the order for loading or unloading except in the public street. Mr Scotter had attended a meeting of the Parish Council ars] they had not been able to get a clear explanation from him as to where the company intended to make a siding. It was certain that there must be a terminus at the end of the line and the Parish Council's anxiety was that it should be in a suitable place. Treffie was already congested at Stockton Heath and it was important that the sidiag or terminus should be in a position where it would not cause obstruction to the public traffic or be dangerous. Then providing a railway was e4eblished there was no regulation as to speed. Mr. Scotter told them that the Board of Trade wouln protect the public in that respect and stated that at Stockton Heath the speed woula perhaps net be more than from one to four miles an hour. The rarish Cauncil also thougnt that the railway would depreciate the value of property and deter people from coming to live in the neighbourhood. It was probable that the rail- way would cut across Victoria square, and as that was the only piece of land the Parish owned,the Council was particularly anxious to retain it. He thought all would aerse with the action of the Parish Council in opposing the order if only for the purpose of getting terms with the new company. (Hear. hear.) 3e thought they ought to ask for at least a 40ft. road be- tween the curbs, in order to leave ample room for the proposed railway. He also objected to all the goods traffic coming through the parish, and it would be far better if the campany could deviate at London Bridge, and have a siding on land near there. (Hear, hear.) There was nothing shown in the plan where the wait- ing rooms would be situated, and it would be for the Parish Council ta see that no obstruction or encroach- ment took place. It had been said that the Parish Council had no locus etandi but he thought that what- ever resolution they came to at a public meeting of rate- payers, would have due weight with the commissioner who ivould be sent down by the B oard of Trade. Mr. CLARicE recounted his objections to the order in detail. He referred to the fact that the company sought to rice " mechanical "power and if they got it there would be nothing to prevent them from using steam. Mr. Scotter declined to bind himself to el ic- tricity. It was pointed out in tee oleic teat, the com- pany's tvaggens would be 6ft. 6ia. wide but they would be in a pcsition to take foreign waggons, and these generally measured Bft. By this order the company became users of the public roads for ever arid they were paying nothing for the privilege. The rail- way lines would be a great danger to all vehicular traffic and especially to cyclists. If a wheel got into the groove of the line which in the case of a railway must be a wide one the cyclist would be „seriously injured. The railway would also interfere with the district sewers and hydrants. At present these were in the centre of the road and if the railway were placed over them there would be a great difficulty experienced in attending to repairs. If the conapany got the powers they sought they would be in a positionto run:all their:waggons from Warrington and Northwich through the public streets and incommode vehicular traffic and foot passengers. With regard to the fares, there was this clause in the order : " The fares for passengers shall be one penny per mile; but the company may charge for any distance exceeding half a mile and not exceeding two miles any sum not exceeding twopence." If this %sere granted the company weuld be in a position to charge twopence for short distances. The charges for goods traffic were also three or four times higher than those of the London and North Western Railway Company. None of the promoters of the scheme had the slightest interest in this part of Cheshire, and there was no doubt that if the promoters got the order they would throw it on the market and float it at a profit. If there was a profit to be made let the County Council take the matter up and work it for the benefit of the county. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion Mr. Clarke said he based his objections on the three main grounds that the rail- way would be a source of discomfort and inconvenience to vehicular traffic ; a source of annoyance not only in the day time but in the night time ; and also a gre it danger. Mr. Bouchier asked the Chairman whether the opposition of the Parish Council was against the prin- ciple of a light railway or only to details? The Chairman said the Council had not objected te the Bill in principle, but on the details which he had given. Mr. England thought if that was the case they ought to pass a resolution approving of the introduction of a light railway. It was possible that the objections which had been raised would damn the scheme. The Chairman remarked that if the Company met their objections the Bill would be acceptable. Mr. Bouchier said he was very glad to hear that ex- planation. Be did not see how the Council could object if they objected to the railway in principle. As to the details they knew very well that they could be thrashed out before the Commissioner who would be sent down to inquire into the order. He would like to refer briefly to a few of the objections which had been raised. Mr. Clarke objected to the wording of the clause relating to " mechanical' power. but that was the usual e phraseology for orders of this kind. The company provided for different modes of traction and when they brought forward the system they had decided to adopt, the objectors would have an opportunity of making their protest against any particular kind of traction. If the new company decided upon electricity they might be certain of this that no other system would be adopted becanse the expense of altering it would be too great for a small company like the one in question. Most of Mr Clarke's objections were based upon the supposition that the line would be laid in the centre of the road. If the railway ran at the side of the road then most of the objections would fall to the ground. With respect to the waiting room, that would have to be erected in accordance with arrangements come to with the District Council, County Council or the owner of the land, and he therefore did not see that any objection could be raised on that point. Then really uo objection could be raised to the paragraph dealing with fares. The company would have to run in opposition to the omnibuses so far as Warrington was concerned and it would not be to their interest to charge a twopenny fare. He was glad to hear that the opposition of the Parish Council was not to the principle of the bill but only to the details. That being so, he maintained that the difficulty which had been raised could be cleared away. They might depend upon it that nothing at all would be done without a Com- missioner being sent down and the objections could then be considered. Mr. Davenport said the object of the Council in oppos- ing the scheme was to get the best terms they could. He did not want anyone to have the idea that they did not want the railway at all. They wanted it an reasonable terms. (Hear, hear). Mr. Pemberton said he would like to know what a light railway was. If ordinary goods waggons were to be run on the line, the metals would have to be of the same depth as ordinary railway lines. Their streets at Stockton Heath were net wide enough for such a rail- way, and they ought to strongly oppose it. Mr. Westbrook moved the following resolution : "That this meeting of ratepayers of Stockton Heath parish consider the scheme for the proposed lightrailway to be objectionable on many grounds' particularly on account of the narrowness of the roads which ought not to be used as a railway track and also that the draft order is defective in many respects and this meeting hereby calls upon the arish Council to represent such objections and Pdefects to the Rural District Council. County Coun- cil and Light Railway Commissioners." Stockton Heath</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ALLEGED WILFUL MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED WILFUL MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>FATAL QUARREL.</p>
        <p>Inquest and Verdict. It is only a short time ago that a Warrington man named Thomas Kinsey was committed for twelve years penal servitude for beating to death James Poole of Wellington-street, and unfortunately, War- rington is again to figure prominently at the next Liverpool Assizes in a tragedy which was enacted in the early days of this year at Russell-street. On Monday morning last there died at the Warrington Infirmary an ironworker named Joseph Swift, 56 years of age, who lived at 2, Russell-street. He was admitted on Sunday, January 7th, suffering from injariee which it is alleged were the result of a kick by Thomas Walsh, an ironworker, who was at one time a professional pugilist travelling with a show to fairgrounds. The quarrel arose as the result of a drinking bout between the wives of the two 1318 H. On the evening of January 3rd they were in the ' Blackburne Arms, imbibing freely of rum and beer. At the invitation of Ars. Swift, the wife of Walsh accompanied her home, and here more drink was consumed. They quarrelled and had a fight, in which the deceased took part. Before departing Mrs. Walsh wha lost her shawl and front in the affray, broke nine panes of glass. On thefollowing morning shortly before eight o'clock Ira Walsh re- turned to the scene of the previous night's disturb- ance in compeny with her husband, who had just finished a night's turn at the ironworks of Messrs' Psarson and Knowles. The quarrel was renewed, Mrs. Walsh confining her attentions to the wife of the deceased, and the SCCU•10(1 commenced to fight the deceased. In the melee it is alleged that Walsh inflicted serious injuries upon the de- ceased by kicking him between the legs with his cos. Deceased immediately went up-tairs, fol- lowed by the accused, who is stated te have ex- claimed" If I have not finished you, I will do so." Deceased barricaded the bedroom door, and pre- vented the accused from entering. Walsh and his wife then departed. Later in the day Or Curtis was called in and advised the removal of Swift to the Infirmary. Here he was operated upon but despite the skill and attention of the hospital ssaff he died after suffering reef., egeny about five o'clock on Monday morning last. The serious condition of the injured man was reported to the Chief Constable a week previously and he thought it wise to arrest the accused as soon as possible. A warrant was issued for his arrest. The prisoner's wife got to know , ,e of this and informed the prisoner,and both of them ' absconded from the town. Telegraphic and telephonic messages were despatched and Walsh was arrested at Prescot where he was going under the name of On Tuesday evening, a Coroner's jury after car&amp; telly investigating the case returned a verdict of "Wilful murder" against Walsh, and he will now have to answer that charge at Liverpool Assizes. The prisoner made a statement on oath in which he stated that he saw his wife kick Swift, but on his wife being called, she absolutely denied that either she er her husband kicked the deceased. The prisoner will be brought before the magistrates on , Wednesday next. He is a strong muscular man, and seems to realise his position keenly. When he heard the verdict of the jury he buried his face in his arms and before being led below he cast despair- ing glances at his wife who was seated behind. The Inquest. Verdict of "Wilful Murder." _ .</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body of Swift was confine:. the Mien Cur. , o 3 Tuesday afternoon, by Mr. S. Brie- heuseeCounty Coroner. Mr. William Roberts was the foreman of the jury. Prisoner, who occupied a place in the (leek, was undefended. Th, Chief Cone:able ('Zr. Luke Talbot) watched the proceeding's on behalf of the police. Tne Coroner, in openiee the ins' eiry; described the C 2013 as being a simple one, which would not take up the time of the jury very long. The first WitileBi was Mary Swift, 2, &amp;well-street, wife of the deceauri who stated that her husband was an iron worker, and was fity SiX years of age last birthity On Januery 3rd, she met Annie Walsh, prisoner's wife, about seven o'elcck, near Sankey-street. The 7 called at the BI ickburne Arms, and than went homes While. there deceased came lute the house, and she and Mrs Walsh had a qeeerel. About eight o'clock next moraine she and her husband were in the bedroom wheel they heard someone knoeking at the door. They both went downsteire and her husternd who wee only weariee trousers eteckinee and shirt opened tile doer d,ua prisoner and his wife Came in. Walsh imitedilee'y ;Tenn her husband a blow on the head, without a weed of ex- p!anation. Mrs. Walsh then got the shovel and the fryingpan and hit her on the head. She was thrown on the filer, and heard her husband say, " My Grol dons:: kill me! I never saw you before." fire sere men were net acquaintnl. When she got up her husleend was 'yin! on the sofa, with Walsh standing over him. As her husband tried to get sway prisoner kicked him from behind between the legs.anci followed him upstairs sheut- ing " You pig I'll kill you." As deceased managed to get into a bedroom prisoner went away, " saying he would fimieh him" when he saw him at his work at night. Deceased lay on the bed all dear, and never came down till the following Seoday night, when lee was removed to the Infirmary. She knew of no previous quarrel be- tween the men. She never saw her husband strike prisoner during the time that the prisoner Was in the house. By the Chief Constable: She could not tell what kind of ehees the prisoner was wearing at the time he kicked Swift. • By the Prisoner : At the " Blackbureos Arms " we had three glasses of rum and two glasses of beer. When we got in the hole,e I sent for ono pint of beer. Your wife did-nat kick the deceaeed.but she broke nine panes oF gloss in my windows. You two men did net have " a fair fieht." ?dueller. I never kicked a man, woman or child in my life. lam innocent as a child. By the Jury: She heard. her husband say "This man (meaning the prisoner) surely has killed me." She was sure the prisoner's wife did not kick the deceased. The Coroner thoireht that was a cowardly defence to be set no by the primer. The Prisoner: It is my wife's confession. If she has committed the offence I don't see that I should suffer— the innocent for the guilty. Further questioned, witness said she was sure it was the prisoner who kicked her husband. She did not see Mrs. Walsh do so. So far as she know there bad been no previous qurrrel between her husband and prisoner. Tnernae Ho leett, a carter residing at 4,Russell-stroet, neat •loor to the deceased,said that on Wednesday night the 3rd inst he was in bed wheu he heard a smashing of windows. The next morning at 8.39 he was in the kitchen and heard a scuffle taking place in Swift's house. Swift shouted "Oh man don't kill me" and all went quiet. The scuffle lasted about ten minutes. Be the Chief Constable : e knew Swift personally and had no diffieulty in recognizing his voice. He was a quarrelsome man when he had drink but was "as quiet as a lamb "at other times. He was a strong man, but not as strong-looking as Walsh. George Mullis, a boy aged 14 years, residing at 1, Russell-street, said that on Thursday morning. the 4th inst., he was passing at the back of Swift's house about ten minutes to nine when he noticed a woman in the back yard, whoha he did net know, but 11091 identified her as the prisoner's wife. He beard Swift shout "Oh, don't kick me." The voice came from the kitchen. He walked round to the front of the house to wait for two lads, and as shey were going down Banana-lane, they saw the prisoner end his wife. By the foremen : He was quite sure thet it was the prisoner whom be saw. Dr. Curtis, 93, liewssy-street. saii that deceased came under his professional care on Thursday eveaiog,the 4th Inst. Fie was suffering from injuries to the lower parts of the body. He was removed to the Infirmary on Sun- day. Since death he had made a post-mortem exernina- tine assisted by Dr. Thorpe. Death was caused by the injuries refereed to. A considerable degree of violence would have to be used to cause the injuries. The clogs produced, which belonged to the prisoner, were quite sufficient to Cause the injuries. The kick would probably he given from behind. By the Chief Censtable : Fcr the first 24 hours he thought the deceased had a chance of recovering. De- ceased was a s'.rong man for his age. Dr, Harold Thorpe, junior bonne surgeon at t'le Infir- mary, said the deceased was admitted on Rundae, the 7th inst, and remained under the care of the staff until his death. Witness assisted Dr. Curtis when the post- mortem examinatien was made, and he agreed that the man would not have died but for the injuries be had received. An erdioary kick would be sufficient to cause the injuriee which had been described. Mr. George F. F, White (the deputy magistrates' clerk) nroduced the depositions of the deceased taken on the 10th January at the Infirmary in the presence of a magistrate and the prisoner. The depositions were as follows :—" On Thursday morning, the 4th January,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Prisoner Gives Evidence.</head>
        <p>The Prisoner Gives Evidence.</p>
        <p>The prisoner elected to give evidence on oath on his n.in behalf. He slid he lodged at 37, Scettand-rold, Wirrington, an Ihe was a forgeman. At 5.30 on the WsdnesdaY evening he wee': to work at Mesees Pearson and Knewlei' neat and iron work, and worked all nieht until six o'clOck on Thur.?.lay morning, when he ollained an advance of Sc from Mr Walc4field. He arrived home aboist a quarter-past seven when he found his wife sitting in front of the fire. He thought it was a very unesual thing for her to be up 49 early, and in con- sequence of the conversation that followed ha acenimpleied her to Swift's house, 2. Russell- street. They knocked at the back door and walked into the front kitchen. They saw the deceased, who asivei them whet thee wanted a,ni he told him that ha hai come ro • his wife's shawl. Swift told him to"elear out," or "he wou'd get the same as she got." He told him he would gri when he found his wife's shawl. and went to the sofa where he naticed a bundle of things. He was looking at the things when Swift went up to him and struck him in the faee with his fist. He retaliated and they were fic!h•ing for two or three minutes. Shortly ef7erwerds •ceif.c's wife struck him on the shoulder with a shovel and he turn-cl round and took it off her. It this jenctnre his (prisoner's) wife and the deceased were "rectline"; he separated them, and the deceased went upsleiirs. Toes' thee eirained the shawl and the front and went out throueh the hank door. Cross-examined : He did not say anything when he was apprehended about Smith heving been assaulted by his wife. He was a professional pugilist, and had earned his livine by boxing. When arrested by the Prescot peg:. he gave the name of Bernard Riley, and his wife was with him. His wife told him about the steps being 'taken by the poles, and they both atm:ended fr ea the town. By the Cerevr : He knew hie wire had been turned eit o' S,vjt's house after a quarrel. She did not tell him abeut the panes of glass till they got to the house. The C ironer : hiving that knowledge in your mind, de you tell the jury that the object of your visit was to get the shawl and front ? The Prig ur : Yes, sir. The C,rener : Do you deny that when the man was wal%ing away from the sofa you kicked him from behind ? The Prisoner : I do, sir. I never kicked a man since I was born —never in my life. The Cerener : Then you lead us to believe that if this man was kicked it was by your wife? Prisoner (hesitating) : It is rather hard for me to say that. The Coroner : You said if it was yonr wife who caused the injuries it was very hori for you to suffer for it. With Chit sentiment we all agree. Are you preresred to say "Yes" er .'No." Did you see your wife kick Swift? Prisoner : I dd. Th- Ceroe : At what part of the proceedings did your wife kick Swift ? . Tne Prisoner : After hes w;f hit one w.elh the shovel. Swift ani my wife were en the floor. Alter further eusestioning. the prisoner added that Swift aryl his wife were wrestling together on the floor. She kickee him while she was on the top of him. The Coroner : You either saw your wife kick Swift or vete did riot. I), you say you saw her kick him ?</p>
        <p>No answer. The ()Ironer ; This is the penalty you incur by offer- ing yourself for oroes,exemination. No man ought to have the privilege of offering himself for evidence unloss he doea undergo that ordeal. I aperoved of the altera- tien of the law long ago before the Legislature approved of it, because I knew what would happen. I knew no matt coati] face the ordeal successfully unless he was tolling the truth. You say your wife kicked Swift ?</p>
        <p>Prisoner : Yes. sir. The Coroner: Ia what position was she ? Prisoner: Leaminz over him. He got up and went upstairs straizhtaway. I pulled her away from him. In answer to membars ot the jary, the prisoner denied fo!lowinF,, Swift upstairs. He did not make uge of the words "If I have not fioished you I will do now." Plis wife was wearing button-up hoots at the time. 119 did not hear Swift say" Oh man, don't kill me." Prisoner's Wife in the Box.</p>
        <p>Prisoner then called his wife, Annie Walsh, to give evidence on his behalf. Her statement, however, was entirely contradictore to his. She dmied that her husband ever kicked the deceased. No one kickld him. Her husband followed Walsh up- stairs but could not got in the room. By the Chief Constable : Swift was not on the floor. She admitted in the Chief Constable's office that she kicked the deceased but she did it baeause she thought she would "get Tommy off." The Coroner : Whet is the value of evidence of that character. A man on one side says deliberately he saw his wife stoop over the man and kick him, and she en the other side deliherately denies it. The Coroner then summed up the evidence and gave certain directions to the jury, and informed them that it was their duty to say whether the circernatances justi- fied the prisoner in being committed for trial. It eeemed to him that this was a case that ought to be very easily disposed of. The question was whether they believed that Walsh went to the house intending or no Intending to c mamit inj cry to the deceased. If the injury upon Swift was greater than be anticipated he was reapaesi- ble for what followed. There could be no middle course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Verdict.</head>
        <p>The Verdict.</p>
        <p>After deliberating in private for a quarter of an ham, tho jury returned a verdict of" Wilful courier "against Walsh The verdict seAlned to come as a ehock to the prisoner who had to be assistel by st:veral officers to the cells below.</p>
        <p>Prisoner before the Magistrates. Further Remand. Walsh was brought bef ore the magistrates on Wednes- day morning, and ilharged with the wilful murder of Joseph Swift on the 4th January. The Chief Constable said Walsh was charged formally, for the purpose of remand, with the wilful murder of Joseph Swift on the 4th of the present month.. He had been remanded from time to time, and the previous day a Coroner's inquest was held and the jury brought in a verdict of wilful murder." In order to properly pre- pare the case for hearing, he had to ask for a remand for a week to Strangeway's Gaol. The remand was granted.</p>
        <p>Lecture at St. James' School, Latchford. A large audience assembled in St. James' school, Latchford. on Monday evening, when Mr. John II Cooke, Ta'cnrtler of Over, ,lelivered a lecture en- titled "A sin en the sunny Mediterranean to Greece and C3natantinopla &amp;c." The Rev. F E Powell (vicar) presided and opened the proceedings with a few well chosen remarks. Mr. Cooke gave a most interesting account of his journey,thelecture being profusely illustrated with limelight views. Ora the taotiPn of Mr. Burgese,seconded by Mr. H Houghton a connal vote of thanks was accorded to the lec- turer and the lanternist (Nir. Dodd). A collection was taken for the reduction of the church debt,and a vote of thanks having been passed te the Vicar for presiding, the proceedings were brought to a close with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
