      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>people wanted 0 know,he said, what benefit they were going to get. The prormtsrs 111.1 not been asked to come there with the railway and before aecepting it they must know what was going tn be given them for the privilege of using the public rod; instead of buying land for a railvray in the usual way. The objections of tbe Parish Council would not be met unless they made them known to the Commissiontr._ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Miller seconded the resolution. Mr. England proposed the following amendment "That this meeting approves of the introduction of a light railway into the district but would leave the ques- tion of details of the scheme in the hands of the. local authorities whose districts are affected." He con tended that Stockton Heath would benefit by the light railway to a substantial degree. A company tbat would have to spend £20,000 or £30,000 in the district must bring some advantages, for the money would have to be turned over many times in order to make a profit. The railway would bring a considerable amount of trade and many residents into the district. They knew very well that between Stockton Heath and Northwich, the district was practically inaccessible owing to the lack of a good means of communication. A. similar scheme to that now proposed had been heartily approved by the people of Southport and Orcnskirk. At the present moment there was a light railway in existence between Ruabon and Wrexham and it was looked upon as a great been. Mr Clsrke might never have eeen a railway judging by the obje.ctiens he had raised. The chief traffic on this railway would be passenger traffic. (" No, no."), Well that was his ooinion. Mr Clarke had not a good word to sty for the scheme. He Could only see objeations,lbut they knew that any means of communication by water or rail were a distinct benefit to any community. Mr ; Clarke's objeetions were of a frivolous nature. The [promoters were going to improve the communications'of Stocktoo Heath, and the residents ought to thank them for it. The Board of Trade would see that the interests of the district were not impaired. Mr. -Mason considered light railways a retrograde movement and he was not ashamed to say that ho objected to th-m in principle. He did not agree with an act of Parliament which enabled a private company to monopelise the public roads without compensating the authorities for them. In a populous district like Stockton Heath there was a greater objection to them than in country districts. It was necessary that their roads should be at least 63 ft before they allowed a light rail m-ey. Mr. Moss seconded idle amendment, and stated that he had not heard any serious objection to the scheme. Mr. Whitley said he would like to ask Mr. England whether the Snip Canal had been an advantage or a nuieanee to the district. (Laughter). Mr. Roan and Mr. Flallosvea having spoken, a vote Was taken. Only four voter/ for the amendment, and on the resolution being put to the meeting it was carried by a large majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>VIRTUE OF CLEANLINESS,</head>
        <p>THE VIRTUE OF CLEANLINESS,</p>
        <p>If "cleanliness is next to godliness" then we may gain a valuable object lesson in its application to manufac- ture by observing a model factory which from a small beginning has grown in'o a mammoth business where cleanliness Is strictly observed. All the articles prepared are edible, and for this cleanlinmea is particu- larly insisted upon. It has teen a delight to many to pay a visit to Histon. near Cambridge, and inspect Chivers' great fruit Farm and Factory. The fruit is chiefly grown by the firm itself in their own plantations, and the district in the neighbourhood of the factory. Plucked early on a summer's morn, the fruit is made in- to jam, or the juice extracted to flavour Ch;vere Gold Medal Jellies. Here is the testimony of Dr. Gordon Stables, R.N., "Messrs. Chivers have come to the front with Jellies "which really add to our health and locum No home "is complete without theee pore and excellent Jellies." A Grocer remarks, "Your Jellies are preferrrd to other "makes. Customers will have Chivers.' I think that "speaks far their excellence." A Q.C. writes "Kindly "send me a sample of yonr Jellies. lam told that they "are marvellously good." A Baptist minister eays,"My "wife considers your Jellies perfection, and I fully en- "dorse her opinion." Sold by Grocers and Stores in packets Half-pint i 21.1 Pints qi, Quirts Bd. A Free Samole will be sent on re- ceiptcf post card. S. craivers and S:ns, Histon, Cam- bridge. Please mention this paper,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>ORDER OF DEDIDS.—LOYAL STONERENGE LODGE, No. 2,086.—A knife-and-folk tea wag held in the lodgeroom on Saturday night, to celebrate the 50th anniversary of the commencement of the lodge. About 50 members pert.* of the meal, which was served in a @Cede which did credit to the catering of the hostess,Mre Cook,of the London Bridge Inn. A meeting was afterward; held, Presided over by the senior trustee. Bro J Alhin.on, who in a vary feeling manner prepored the toast of " The Queen end Revel Family." A vPrne of the National Anthem was slum, followed by A song by Bro J W Isher- wood, entitled " The difference between east and west." The toast of "The Army and Navy" was proposed by Bro W Shaw, jun., referenee being made to the deeds of the navy in the past, and the hardships our soldiers are enduring in the Transvaal. A hope was expressed that the war would soon be ended to the satisfaction of the country. Bro R Isherwood sang " When London sleeps," after which Bro J Hough•:on, proposed "Success to Loyal Stonehenge Lodge," to which the Secretary,Bro F Shaw, replied, giving a sketch of the doings of the lodge in the east, with a statement showing that the suns of £5,762 16s 8i had been paid net for sick allow • anee end funeral claims during the 49 years the lodge had been in existence. The total worth of the lodge at the end of the year 1899 was £976 6s qd, showing a gain on the year's working of £74. Ben F W Grounds sang "The cabin with roses at the door," and lire W Beswick, sen., proposed the toast of the "Board of Management," coupled with the name of Mr W Rough- ten, organising arent,who thanked the members for the honour duo to the Board in including the toast en -the list. Other longs follemed,and a collection was made for the widows and orphans of the soldiers now in the Transvaal.</p>
        <p>Warrington Markets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday wheat(wkite) fetched 3s 11d. (701 b to the bushel) ; red wheat 3s 104 ; oats, 23 441; flour, best seconds per 2401 b., 194; bran, Is per 20b. At the Provision Market prices were as follows:—Fresh butter, Is 3d and is 41. per lb; fresh eggs, seven for is ; fowls 43 6d to 53 61 per couple; English beef, 51 to 01; mutton 7d to 104 ; veal, 7d to 10d; pork, .1 to 81.</p>
        <p>Body Found in the Canal at Bewsey: Mr. Brighouse held an inquest at the Chapel House Inn, Great Sankey on Tuesday afternoon, on the body of Fred Greaves (19), a shoemaker's ap- prentice, who lived at 199, Winwick-road, and whose bedy was found floating in the Sankey and St. Helens Canal, at Bewsey on the 15th inat. De- ceased who had been missing since December 6th had suffered from its for a considerable number of years. On December 4th, he went to Orford Barracks, to enlist, but was rejected. He then went to Liverpool, and returned on the Otis, between five and six p.m. His father spoke to him about him wishing to join the Army. Deceased left the house and was not seen alive again.—A verdict of "Found drowned " was returned.</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T., No. 1 Convention, S.W. Lancashire. The monthly meeting was opened on Saturday afternoon, in the Miner's Hall, TyWesley, by Bro A Francis, CC T (Earlestown). There was a good muster of representatives and visitors from War- rington, Wigan, Ashton, Earlestown, St. Helens, Leigh, Tyldosley, and Atherton. The Convention , Secretary (Bro Newman, of Earlestown) read the minutes of the last session, after which the Press and Credential Committees were appointed. The Secretary presented his monthly report, and said he was glad to see such a good attendance. He intimated that Bro Jacklin, G L Missioner, would be in the district in February, and he hoped that the lodges would endeavour to make the visit a success. The visiting deputies reported that good work was being done in the various parts of the area. The members then sat down to an excellent tea, provided by the Tyldesley friends, after which a public meeting was held in the same room. Bro E Newman, DSJ T, presided. After a hymn had been sung and a prayer offered, a glee was well rendered by W-..e choir. The chairman urged the claims of juvenile templary, and an interesting musical programme was gone through.</p>
        <p>LINUII C&amp;THARTICUM PILLS, dlgestire, corm- the, and agreeably aperient, 91d., 13id. Of all Chem sta. -- Advt.</p>
