      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAIVIIN EV.</head>
        <p>THE EXAIVIIN EV.</p>
        <p>JANUARY 19m, 1900.</p>
        <p>It would be matter for The Effects of Colonial Assistance. general regret in this country if any ill feeling or poli:ical trouble should follow the action taken by the Colonial Government in sending troops to South Africa. There are hints of something of thekied, if they do net count for much at present, and may not develop in any alarming manner. Tne colonie3 feel a special interest in what is a colonial struggle, and they take the sensible view that what ccaceres the Empire as a whole also effects and must concern the colonies themselves. It may be that unscrupulous political opponents of the several Ministries which have sent and are sending troops to South Africa may have seen in the movement a chance of dealing effective blows ; but there is no evidence of any serious reaction, and we should have heard of the discontent if it were wide- .)read or likely to be serious. It is from Canada that we might expect to hear of protests and complaints, because poli- tics there are ?Fleeted by racial and religious sentiments more than in some ot our other colonies. It has been thought that the French Canadians might hold aloof from the Impeaial movement. Happily, they are not doing so, and the remarks of Mr. JETTE, the Lieutenant- Governor of Quebec, in bidding farewell to the Quebec Volunteers, are of a moat reassuring char- acter. "We F aeh people of Canada are devoted to the Empire," he declared ; "our devotion resting on sentiment, founded on the gratitude we owe to England for the political and religious liberty and far treatment we have received during the past half-century." Some Canadian politicians, how- ever,have given utterance to remonstrances. and the "New York Tribune" states that Sir WILFRID LAURIER is ;n no danger because of the part he has taken. It believes that "the overwhelming majority of Canadians, of both polit'eal parties, approve Sir Wieeran's action." It adds, with great significance, that "the British Empire is a w hole, and it parts must stand or fall to gether." A similar idea animates New Zealand and the Australian Coloaies. Every proposal to furnish more aid is heartily welcomed, and the I several Governments do not seem to be the least imperilled by anything they have done in sending troops to South Africa. Money and men are being found, and in a burst of patriotism which is excel- lent, aed untinged by any hope of imrned.ate reward in colonial concerns. The troops equipped have done splendid work in South Africa, and the same compliment mrst be paid to the Canadians. The Beers themselves acknowledge that the scout- ing and attackieg exploits of the colonials deserve the highest praise. If we tarn to India, which is strictly speaking, a dependency and not a colony, we discover the same agreeable willingneas to contribute to the support of the Imperial arms. The native Princes are send- ing horses, and they would send men also if we' would let them. The Volunteer regiment which is being formed will consist, of course, of Anglo-Indians and not of natives, and the forma- tlon of Lumscten's Horse is proceeding with des- patch and enthusirsm. From Ceylon there will also be voluntears, and Rangoon would join in the movement if it had the chance. Even remote Hong Kong wishes to do her part in the defence of British interests. So far, then, no evil effects have follovied anywhere from colonial enthusiasm, and we strongly hope they will not he manifest. The projected Fenian invasion of Canada in the i spring s not at all likely to come off. The United States is now too vigilant to prevent its effective organisation. But even if something in the nature of a raid were really attempted the Canadians would be quite able to protect their own country and to h:ve a warm reception to Irish-Americans.</p>
        <p>POLITICAL Strategy indi- The Inquiry on the cates that we are about to War.</p>
        <p>have an inquiry promised on the war, with a view to anticipate attacks and amendments. We cannot affect to be surprised that this movement is in contemplation, and we shall do well to understand what is meant, and to prepare to make the best of a difficult situation. Mr. BALFOUR'S elation at the successes of War Office administration has been received with Mixed emotions. There has been some admira-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>4:1</head>
        <p>4:1</p>
        <p>tion of his zeal in defence of his colleagues, but it has been mingled with astonishment at his bad memory, his desire to minimise real complaints, and his apparent belief that he can appeal with success to party spirit: against patriotic indigna- tion. We need not now go over again the old ground in reply,or seek to draw any political infer. maces from the consternation and rebuke with which his apologies have been received. Cur object is to anticipate events, and to warn the country against cleverness and plausibility in a new form. It is probable, we may almost say it is certain, that the Government will be compelled to grant an inquiry of some sort, either by having to meet a hostile motion, or by the attitude of its best-informed fol- lowers. The fear is that Parliament will not obtain what the country wants,and that the Government will endeavour to outflank a legitimate position. The country, irrespective of party, desires an in- vestigation into the whole conduct of the war,into the failure of the War Office and the Committee of National Defence and the Cabinet to understand what the Boers had done and were doing, and into the insufficiency of the early efforts that were made to meet the expected developments in Natal,at the Cape, and at home. The Government as a whole, as well as the Colonial Office, the Intelligence De- partment, the Ordnance Department,and the War Office, are under censure and have to be tried. The move is national, not one of par' y politics' Hence the suspicion, based upon the whispers that are heard, that the Government will grant some- thing that is not asked for or fails to comprise with. in the terms of its reference the conduct of some of its members. Probably there will be an offer of an inquiry into the working of the War Office. It will not suffice. The slow transports were not hired by the War Office but by the Admiralty. The Colonial Office has had some share in the laxity about collecting information and placing it before the right officials. The Foreign Office must be held responsible for ignorence of what Dr. LEYDS and others were doing. It is not clear that Mr. CONYNGHAM GREENE can be wholly absolved from blame. It is also important Sir WILLIAM BUT. LER should be heard. All these points—and others might be givee—suffice to demonstrate that no limited inquiry will meet the necessities of the case or disclose the facts upon which the country will have to pronounce judgment at the earliest pos- sible reoment. Lie War Office has confessed to ignorance, and has acted in ignorance. It cannot escape the most searching inquiry. But other matters cannot be left out. Even were the terms of reference made to include the work of reorganisation in the future, they would not be broad enough. The country has a right to know how the Government brought it into a position which imposed so great a strain upon its military resources. When did it know that war was in- evitable, and what steps did it take, if any, when the discovery wes made? Unless we can force an investigation comprehensive enough td reveal everything, the inquiry will be a put-off, a sham, and altogether unsatisractory.</p>
        <p>The Parish Council of Stockton Heath have taken a stEctly correct attitude in deciding not to oppose in principle the coi..:,truction of the pro- posed Northwich and Warrington light railway but to content themselves with objecting to de. tails in connection with it which may be inimical to the best interests of the township. There is one point upon which all are more or less agreed and that is the absolute necessity of widening the road from the Ship Canal swing bridge to Victoria Square. To attempt to work a light railway along this narrow and twisted neck of highway would be a serious menace to the safety of pedestrians and vehicular traffic. A width of 401 t should at least be insisted upon, and if the promoters are prepared to undertake this burden, they will have removed the most serious objection to the scheme so far as Stockton Heath is concerned. Mr. Clarke recited a number of minor objections, some of which were rightly termed frivolous, but nothing was heard on such an important point as to whether the electric railway was to be worked on the overhead or conduit system. The overhead system is gener- ally distinguished by a number of unsightly poles and these may be a source of danger in the coun- try lanes where artificial light is not used. These are matters which can be fully discussed before the Board of Trade Commissioner when he holds an inquiry into the application of the promoters for an order. There seems little doubt but that this improved means of communication will ultimately benefit Stockton Heath and it would be a short- sighted policy to block the scheme for grievances which were not real.</p>
        <p>Whatever may be the popular opinion of lawyers as expressed in proverbs, it is quite evi- dent from proceedings in the courts within the last few days that the Incorporated Society is de- termined to maintain a certain standard in the profession. Some ten solicitors have been struck off the rolls for breach of trust in financial matters, and this by a very simple application before a judge. Whatever may be said about the law's unavoidable delays and unnecessary expense, the society finds none of these difficulties in its own transactions. There is no appearance of dis- tinguished Queen's Counsel; Mr. Hollams, a plain and capable junior, manages these things himself. The suspected solicitor gener- ally has to submit to the excision ; anyway, there is no appeal. What a model for litiga- tion, and how different from its ordinary pro- cess! The plan would also be as useful if it could be applied to other professions. The black shepherds in the folds of the Church of England are not dismissed as easily as this. The fact that a Bishop has to bear the costs of a prosecution makes him cautious to timidity. Neither is the Medical Roll as promptly cleared of offenders against its code. Even when a doctor is deprived of his qualifications there is nothing to prevent him practising so long as he can get some other medical man to give a certificate of death, or he gets out of the case before death occurs. But let an ex-lawyer write a letter on behalf of a former client in the old professional style, and he will soon find himself in the police-court. The removal of a name, if permanent, means the end of his career, professionally. His only chaisce is to get some immature young man, already a solicitor, to let him act as manager, but the living thus obtained is precaricus.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Lacy,who has lived among the Boers for over a dozen years, presents the world with a most unflattering picture of their characteristics in the "North American Review this month. In their manner of life no people are less Dutch than the bucolic farmers of the inner Transvaal. The distinctive feature of Dutch life is cleanliness ; the distinctive feature of Boer life is dirt. Both sexes are unsavoury in their habits and persons. A Boer bedroom is a thing for Outlanders of all lzinds to avoid. Morality,Mr. Lacy says,is non-existent. Marriage is an affair of convenience. Affection for wife or offspring after the first few mouths of union does not seem to be a Boer characteristic,according to this same chronicler, who even demolishes their reputation for hospitality. The Boer may be all these things—Mr. Lacy has lived in close com- munion with him for a considerable number of years, while many admirers of Boer life and char- acter have never so much as set eyes upon a real specimen--but the picture is not, as has been asserted elsewhere. a fair argument in favour of utter destruction. All nations had to emerge from barbarism once. The true first cause of the present war is neither goldfields nor politics, but the back- wardness of civilisation in Beerdom. The'state of things described by Mr. Lacy should excite pity rather than dislike or derision ; and, after all, these unkempt people are brave fighters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>Warrington schoolgirls deserve an extra feather in their hats for the noble and successful effort they have made in aid of the wives and families "Tommy" has loft behind him. The schoolgirls' bazaar held in the Town Hall under Mayoral patronage on Tuesday and Wednesday exceeded all anticipations in its extent, variety of attrac- tions and total proceeds. No less a sum than was realised. Under any circumstances this is a large amount to raise in so short a time, but with the call to "pay, pay, pay," ringing 9on- tinuously in our ears from every side, the merit of our schoolgirls' achievement is greatly enhanced. They have carried the whole thing through from beginning to end; the bazaar was organised by schoolgirls, worked for and stocked by them. Really,they have almost pat their elders to shame. After this we shall have to treat the schoolgirls with respect, notwithstanding their short frocks and home-lessons. They have suddenly become a power in the town—a power for good—and that power no one can afford to despise. Well done schoolgirls!</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Town Council meeting last week a lengthy and spirited discussion took place on the Hospital extension question, several members speaking in favour of taking the Institution out- side the town altogether. Councillor Plumpton was the prime mover in this direction, the "leader of the opposition," so to speak, and we must con- fess to having not a little sympathy with his views. It appeared to us that the Corporation did not act in the best interests of the town in letting the opportunity sl p of selling the Hospital to the Guardians, whose purpose it would have answered admirably. But, as Councillor Ben- nett said, it is no use crying over spilt milk—especially as the Mayor denies the spilling of it. The Health Committee's argument is founded on scientific proof that there is no danger of infection being conveyed aerially from a hospital of this class, and they therefore stand out strongly for maintaining and extending it on its present site. The argument of the opposition is more sentimental, but none the less weighty in our opinion. It is, that the inhabitants clustered so thickly rouna the hospital object to its preximity and fear its extension. Added to this is the contention that it would not be a very expensive operation to flit the Infectious Diseases Hospital to the outskirts of the town, where the benefits of isolation and fresh air are obtainable. Some foolish remarks were made about the next generation looking after itself. We should have thought the legacies from the past would have taught the legislators of to-day greater wisdom than this It is their duty to ever bear in mind the requirements of the future as well as those of the present, and we are convinced that a much sounder policy woulhi have been to free the centre of the town from its fever-house. Science may demonstrate the harmlessness of such an h ospital but it cannot by this means eradicate the rooted objection of people to living near it. With the additional inducement ofiered by the benefit to the inmates, and the comparatively small monetary loss which would ho incurred, we should have expected an enlightened council to jump at the opportunity of taking the hospital into the country. Their failure to do so may ultimately assume the form of another " legacy " to future generations.</p>
        <p>Saturday witnessed another unique and interest- ing event in the history of Warrington for during the forenoon the Mayor attended at the Drill Hall and: superintended the attestation of allegiance to the Queen by the local volunteers who are destined to go to the front within the next few weeks. The spectacle of some fifty men volunteering to leave their homes and quiet pursuits in the "good old town" for the rough and perilous work in South ' Africa is one which speaks well for our local patrio- ' tism. It is a matter for congratulation that War- rington has had the distinguished honour of sending the first volunteers to barracks for service at the seat of war. At noon, the men proceeded to. Orford Barracks and there they will follow military duties until they receive orders to embark. They were joined at the Barracks on Monday by the St. Helens contingent of volunteers and it is safE.) to conclude that a feeling of friendship will spring up between these comrades in arms who are to complete a company to be attached to the First South Lancashire Regiment now forming part of Sir Chqrles Warren's brigade at Chieveley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LOCAL BILLS IN PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL BILLS IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Bridge Across the Mersey and the</p>
        <p>Ship Canal Important- Project.</p>
        <p>The Widnes and Runcorn Bridge Company is promo- rtiedefror the purpose of constructing a bridge across the v Mersey and the Manchester Ship Canal between Widnes and Runcorn, and for that purpose a Bill is being brought to the attention of Parliament during the ensuing session. The first shareholders are Sir J• T. Brunner, M.P., and Messrs. Joseph Bercham, Henry Wade Deacon, Holbrook Gaskell. jun., Duncan WiKeehnie, Henry Seton-Karr, M.P., Henry Sutton Timmis, and John Whittingham Wareing. The preamble of the Bill recites/that it would be a great c)nvenience to the people of both Runcorn and Widnes to have such a bridge giving communication between the two towns. The bridge will be a very irenortant structure if it can be ere:.:ted without doing harm to the Manchester Ship Canal, but it will require to be an immense height above high water level to Why; of the passage of vessels. In the Bill it is described as " a transporter bridge over the iver Mersey and the I‘l,ifich,ster Ship Canal, commenc- ing en the northern side of the river Mersey in the borough and township of Widnes at the junction of the Mersey-road with Terrace-road and terminating en the southern side of the Manchester Ship Canal Irl the township of Runcorn in Waterloo-road at the junction of Waterloo-road with the northern sides of Lord-street and Church-street, such bridge being intended for the purpose of transporting trucks. d wagzons, carriages, passengers, animals, goods, !rchandise over and across the said river and and by means of a platform or car suspended from the britc-fe and worked by suitable machinery." The esti- mated cost of the construction of the proposei bridge is £Bb,Boo.</p>
        <p>, Cheshire Lines Railways. By the Bill of the Cheshire Lines Committee to be introduced during the coming session of Parliament power is sought to construct a snasll junction railway half a mile in length in the parish of Rixton with Glazsbrook from the Liverpool Extension Railway to the Wigan Junction Railway Company's line at Glazebrook Moss signal box. A large number of smaller extensions ore contemplated by the Bill, and for that purpose a Lugo number of plots of land at varions points along the lines of the company are to be acquired, including plots in Warrington, Culcheth, llrmston, Heaton Norris, Chcrlton-cum-Hardy, and Northwich.</p>
        <p>POPITL&amp;R CONCERTS, WID 14 S • The concert On Saturday nis!lit in the Baths Assembly Room, was presided ovor by Mr. T Glover. There was a large audience. Songs were given by Miss darvie, Messrs. Wood and J. Taylor. The Widnes subscription Prize Band gave several selections. and Messrs. Corcoran and Moran contributed a. cornet duet. Messrs Hynes, Clarke, Corcoran and Quinn rendered an instrumental quartette, The programme was completed by a num- ber of selections given on the gramophone.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS Ain) SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, 13RIDGE-STREST.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK You BEST BOOTS LiiT LANCIASHIRIL—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
