      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A BELFAST SAILOR IN TROUBLE. William James Werlin, a Belfast sailor, was brought before Mr. F.J. Nerman,on Wednesday morning charged with bAinz drunk and incapable in Fryer-street at 11.30 the previous night.—Tbe case was proved by Superin- tendent D Paareon.—Fined 2s 6d. INDECENT BEHAVIOUR IN THE STREETS. Sarah He!eby, married woman, of Runcorn, and Augustie Ohlson, a German sailor were each fined 103 and 6s costs for indecent behaviour in the streets.—The case was heard at the Runcorn Police Station before Mr. F. J. Norman, on Thursday morning. NARROW ESCAPE CIF HORSEi AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the horses attached to a mourn- ing coach bolced from near the St. Mary's graveyard, down Waterloo-road where they came to grief through one of ail wh,els of the tae%rS3 colliding with a kerb stone. The axle bar and pole were smashed but the horses escaped injury. The driver escaped having previously left his seat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, Runcorn Harriers journeyed to Sankey, to take part in the invitation run with Manchester and Sankey Harriers. Trail was laid over abut 9 miles of exceedingly heavy country, including SM26I ENOCI jumps over both hedge and water. The race was a hot one throughout, resulting in S rinpkinfi arriving home first for Runcorn, followed by W Thomas 2nd, W Ray 3rd. About 70 runners were entertained to tea at the Sloop Hotel, and subsequently there was a smoking colleen and entertainment, in which the Manchester men took a prominent part.</p>
        <p>Sr. PAUL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN. The Rev. C. W. L. Christian, superintendent of the circuit, preached a special sermon to young men at the above place of worship on Sunday last, which was very attentively listened to by a large congregation. Mr. Christian spoke of the " past as the present,and the pre- sent as the futurre," and urged upon his young hearers the all importance of an early dedication of life to Christ so that there would be no dark shadows thrown upon their future life, and so that by steady purpose of living in the time of youth, they in old age would he frce frona that remorse which to often weighed down the souls of men.</p>
        <p>OLD MEN'S TREAT AT HALTON. The annual dinner to the old men at Hilton was given in the Norton Arms Hotel, on Tuesday ev3ning, when Mr. and Mrs. Birel placed a splendid repast bsfora about 42 of the oldest inhabitants of the ancient village. The Rev. G D Wray, vicar, occupied the chair and proposed the usual loyal and pitriotic toasts. Mr. J. Stokes proposed" the Bishop, clergy and ministers of all denominations," and coupled with it the name of the Vicar who appropriately responded. The donors of the feast, which includei Sir Richard Brooke, Bart., and Sir John Brunner, were toasted on the proposition of Mr. Stokes, and he counted with it the name of Mr. Thomas Wright, whom he said had ever taken an active interest in the welfare of the people of Halton. (Hear, hear.)— Mr. Wright suitably respondel—Musical items were contributed by Mr. Thomas Cooke, Runcorn, Mr. W. H. Walton, Halton, and others which greatly enlivened the proceedings. SERIOUS ACCIDENT TO A DRIVER NEAR RUNCORN. Robert Derbyshire, Okell-street, Runcorn, carter in the employ of Mr. Watkin, grocer and provision dealer, Church-street, on Wednesday morning went- with a shandry in order to bring a quantity of furniture be longing ta his master's brother from Ellesmere Part to Runcorn The shaadry was duly loaded and Darbyshire had reached Nethet ton on his way back waen the bed of the cart gave way with a loud report that startled and caused the horse to belt. Derbyshire jumped from the cart retaining his hold of the reins and was dragged under the vehicle which paesed over and broke his thigh. The horse was eventually brought to a standstill but not before considerable damage was done to the property. Mr. Oakes,fartater. Netherton, kindly caused Darbystdre to be taken into his house,and later Dr.Burten was called in to set the broken leg. Subsequently Derbyshire was placed on a stretcher and conveyed to Runcorn, in vehicle, drevided by Mr. Oakes, and driven by his son. At Runcorn. Dr.Orrasby took charge of the injured man who is now reported as doing as well as expected.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT RUNCORN—CONSTABLE FRY TO THE RESCUE in the early:hours of Sunday moraine" Police Constable FIT was on duty in Church-street, and on approdching the end of Loch-etreet,he heard screams from a cottage. He discovered that the noise was that of a woman in the bedroom of No. 3, Loch-street, and having effectel an entrance by the front doer, he found a table and drawers ablaze. He quickly cleared the table of some crockery, Sta., removed the burning tablecloth, and put the table into the street. His attention was then drawn to the bedroom by the occupier of the house, Frank , Price by name, who called out "Catch thie baby." The lyoungster, some eight months old, was safely deposited into the constable's arms, and tranferred to the care of a neighbour. Fry then entered the house again, amid a volume of suffocating smoke, and succeeded in extin- guishing the fire, which had spread iron the table to the drawers, and was getting a good hold of them and their contents. Price, his wife, and another child were almost overcome by the smoke. It appears that the husband, when apprised of what had occurred, made an attempt to come downstairs,but was driven back by the smoke, and had it not been for the timely presence of the constable, the consequences would have been most disastrous. Price estimates the damage to his furniture at £2los. It appears that some fire was left in the grate,and the outbreak is believed to have been caused by .a spark from the fire igniting the tale!e cloth. DIKE OF DEVONSHIRE LODGE —Mil ODEFELLOWS.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet of this lodge is just issued and a very successful year 1899 has proved. Contributions, interest on capital, arc., have brought in £lBB while sick pay and funeral levies to district have amounted to £132 leaving a geed balance on the right side. The number of members introduced during the year reaches the grand total of 31 and the totni financial gain for the 12 months amounts to £6l. There is a good contingency fund in connection with this lodge from which members out of employment have their contributions paid for them by the lodge and at the present time the lodge is bearing thecontributionsof one of its members who is doing duty in South Africa.—The juvenile lodge connected with this lodge is also in a flourishing con- dition and there have been 19 members propos3d into this branch during 1899 making a total of 65 members. The total woalth of the lodge is £1,020 and the adult membership numbers 172 with the exceedingly low average age rf 29 years for each member.—The lodge is worthy of the notice of all young men who seriously in- tend joining a good and sound institution. SINKING OF A RI:MOGEN FLAT IN THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>At tie Liverpool County Court on Saturday, before his Honour Judge Collier, assisted by Captains Quine and Thompson. nautical assessors, an action was heard in which Richard Able, fiatowner, Runcorn, claimed damages from the owners of the steamship Duke of Leinster for sinking the flat Nellie in the Manchester Ship Canal at Wiggs' works, Runcorn.—Mr Bateman, bar- rister, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. A. G. Steel, barrister was council for the defendants.—The case for the plaintiff was that on the 11th August last, while the Nellie was moored in the canal. the Duke of Leinster came along at tee great a speed, and caused a wash, which resulted in the water rushing into the flat and sinking it.—The defendants denied negligence, and con- tended that their steamer was net proceeding at too great a speed, but slowed down on approaching the flat. They further argued that the plaintiff was to blame in not having the fiat, which was a very narrow boat, pro- tected by side clothe or boards. Had such protection been provided, defendants submitted that the wash set up by the steamer would not have injuriously affected the fiat.—The Court held that the Duke of Leinscer was alone to blame, and that there was no contributory negligence on the part of the flat. Judgment was accordingly given for the plaintiff for the amount of the damage and Costs. THE LATEST AMERICAN NOVELTY.-4 DECIDED SUCCESS. The amusement, and usefulness, derived from American notions Enelishified, was well illustrated at the Mersey Miemion to Seamen, on Wednesday last, when the "lunch-basket carnival came to a successful issue. The organieers of the venture were Cartain and Mrs. .knosburv, and First Officer Ambermal, avid Mrs. Amber- mail of the" Puritan," now lying at the Runcorn Layby. Mr. George Duckett, bad kindly consented to act as auctioneer, and there were six double baskets, and 16 single ones offered for sale, and purchased at prices ranging from 10s. downwards. The proceeds for this sale reached £6 31. The baskets were provided by the following ladies :—Deuble baskets : Mrs. Tonks and Mrs. Cliffs ; Mrs. W. Shaw, junior, and Mrs Richard Shaw; Mrs Outhwaite, and Mrs Callaghan; Mrs Hughes and Miss Lilly Shaw; Mrs and Miss Dyer Miss Florria and Miss Edith Shaw. Single baskets : Mesdames Hughes, Evans, Lockett, W Shaw (Lowlands- road). JohnsonsiClough,Amsbury, Ambermal , Harnpson Misses Prescott, Evans, Temple, M Hill, Bowyer, L Shaw A Whitfield. After the sale the ladies dined with the gentlemen who had purchased their delightfully got-up baskets, and whilst the feasting was in progress a musical pregramme was submitted by Misses Garvie and Gibson, Miss Lilly and Master W Shaw,and Mr J Rutter whom) several items were highly appreciated. Another source of income was a guessing competition as to the weight of a plum pudding mace by the ship's cook of the" Puritan." The actual weight proved to be 31bs. 14 ounces. Miss M. Dyer proved the nearest in her calculation, though no one succeeded in guessing correctly. Captains Lovett and Amsbury further diversified things by obtaining a large basket apparently filled with fruit, the sale of which realised 45., but bar the top layer of fine fruit, the purchaser found nothing in the basket but straw. For those per- sons who attended more as spectators, or who could not secure a basket an excellent refreshment bar was pro- vided presided over by Mrs W Shaw (senior), Mrs Leach, Mrs Bower, and Miss Kate Faragher.— The proceeds, which were towards the sale of work pro- posed to be held at an early date in aid of the renovation and beautification of the mission church, realised over £lO. Mr. W. Shaw before the conclusion,moved hearty votes of thanks to the organisers, the artistes, the ladies who provided the baskets, and to Mr. Duckett for his kind assistance, all of which were accorded with acclam- ation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900.</p>
        <p>moved the adoption of the Parliamentary Committees minutes. Mr. Ili oodward seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>TEIE STEAM ROLLER.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Baxter rep3rted that the Committee having the above subject under consideration had recommended the adjournment of the questim for a short paned, so that the Clerk could accumulate considerable data as to the cost and benefits derived by other Rural District Councils alrcady equipped with a steam roller. Mr. Bradford seconded, and the report was confirmed.</p>
        <p>MR. PILLING AND THE ADMIRAL</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling moved the appointment of Mr. Swinton as engineer to prepare plans, specifications, and estimates, for levelling, paving, metalling, flagging, channell- ing, and making good Bostock-street, Gasksll-street, and Lesnard-street in the parish of Stockton Heath. Admiral Parry objected as the matter was out of order. Mr. Piling thougut the Admiral was a little too quick that time. His motion was not open to argument until seconded. Subsequently on the motion of Mr. S. Woodward the matter was referred to the Highway Committee. Admiral Parry strongly objected to that course,on the grounds that the Council had passed a very wise resolu- tion which would have to b 9 rescinded before that sub- ject could be opened. Their surveyors had described how they worked ni..4ht and day and Sundays as well. (Laughter.) He emphatically objected as in his opinion the whole thing was out of order. This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at the Wo:khoulo, Dutton. on Tuesday. There were present : Messrs J Moseley Williams, A R Norman, T Reynolds, the Rural District Councillors. with Mr G F Ashton (clerk), Mr. WE Hough (deputy clerk), and Mr. Joseph Cunnah (master). ACHNOWLEDHEMSNT OF SYMPATHY. Mr. and Mrs. Knowles and family wrote expressing their sincere thanks to the Guardians for the sympathy shown to them in their sad bereavement.</p>
        <p>BOYS WANTED ON THE INDEFATIGABLE. Captain A. Wellesley Brunner, superintendent of the Training Ship Indefatigable, wrote under date sth January, as follows :—" I will feel obliged if you will kindly send me the 15s for the boy John Ketteringbam's holiday allowance as approved by the Guardians at Mid- summer. My accounts are to be audited next week,and my camp balance sheet made up. We have a great de- mand for boys and good openings, having sent 113 to sea last year. If you can send me a number for 12 months training I feel sure the Guardians will be satisfied with their success. The ship is very healthy and caused and received complimentary remarks from the Local Government Board Inspectoes." The question was raised as to the ages of the boys re- quired, and the Clerk was instructed to inquire. THE OVERSEERS AND VIE ASSESSMENT APPEALS. Mr. C. E. Linaker had given notice of the following resolution :—" That it be a recommendation to the Assessment Committee that they require notices of ob- jection against assessments to be in the bands of their clerk and of the overseers of the parish affected for seven clear days at least before the date upon which such objections are heard." Since the notice Mr. ILinaker wrote stating that he was prevented attending thq meeting of the Guardians, and he had asked Mr. Bax(er to propose the resolution standing in his name. Mr. Baxter in proposing the motion, said that Mr. Linaker felt that recently he had only had a day or two's notice given to him when there had been a case of appeal of assessment in tbe Township of Frodsham. He thought it was therefore only fair that a little more time should be given them so that they could raise any objec- tion to the assessment being altered. He (Mr. Baxter) suggested that in future the overeeers have at least seven days' notice, and proposed the adoption of the , motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bradford seconded. Mr. J. Edwards theinht that Mr. Linaker simply implied a recommendation to the Assessment Committee as being the leoly that were in direct communication with the assistant overseer. Mr. Mclntyre diked what were the instructions sent annually to the assistant overseers. The Clerk observed there was nothing as to time in which notice was to be given. Mr. Baxter intimated that Mr. Linaker wrote to him to the effect that" he (Mr. Linaker) b!lievel there was a resolution on the minutes of the Beard to the effect that 7 clear days notice should be given to the overseers of any appeal of assessment." Mr. Norman tnought there were few, very few, instances in which the overseers did not get that notice or more. Das Clerk: That is so. Mr. Norman: As a rule there was a week or two. I quite agree with Mr. Linaker, however,aod do not think that the overseers shenl4 be rushed. But I understood that the overseers always had, however, to withhold the case until they were prepared to deal with it. Mr. Edwards referred to the ignorance of the general public as to when the meetings of the ABS3SSMIRIS Com- mittee were held. Indeed sometimes the Assessment Committee didn't know themselves when they were to Me3t. The Clerk observed that if the resolution was passed, ,he simply took it as an instruction to the Clerk of the Assessment Committee, and ia the event of notice of appeal being given less than 7 days prior to the Assess- ment Committee it would be held over to the next meet- ing . Mr. J. Stokes thought that some instructions should be eiven to the assistant overseers. Mr. Linaker's motion was then put, and agreed to unanimous'y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ANNUAL TEA.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL TEA.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Visitiug Committee proposal helling the annual tea for the inmetes on Friday (the 19th inst.) and invited the Guardians and their Chairman. The Clerk was instructed to reply to the ladies inti- mating that twelve members of the Board were likely to accept their generous invitation. THE VACCINATION FEES.-A REVISION PENDING. Mr. S. Woodward submitted the financial report which showed outrelief was granted :—Runcorn, £B5 ; Frodsham, £23; Great Budworth, £24. The quarter's account for maintenance of lunatics at the Cheshire County Asylum was £317 8s 4d at 7s 7d per head. The Superioress of Pantasaph Orphanage (Holywell) required £26, being 4s per week each for children mainteinel there. Mr Cooke's registration fees amounted to £l7 lie 101 ;Mr Riley's to £6 13s 31; and Mr De Prez's to £8 6s id. The same officer's vaccination fees were re- spectively £ll 04 41, £4 10i 51 and £51e31. Drßurton'e vaccination fees amounted to £l6 7s 01—twenty-six of which were for 7s 61 tAch. Dr Bower's account for vaccination was for £l6 13s 6cl of which 33 cases were at 7s 6d each. They had also received Messrs Evans and Company's account for coals at 12s 41 per ton ;an item they could appreciate.—Several Guardians Yes. Very much.—The total peyment for the day amounted to £687 6s 11d, which left a balance of £1,331 19s id in hand. He (Mr Woodward) farther wished to intimate that in respect of the negotiations for the loan of £928 from the Public Works Loan Commission, it nad cost them £lO 12e 61. Mr. A. R. Norman asked how the vaccination fees compared with the old state of tbings. Several Guardians . Oh ! dear ! Mr. Piling: They don't compare at all. (Laugh- ter.) Mr. Norman took it that at the end of the twelve mouths the medical officers fees for vaccination were to be remodelled; and, while they agreed to a twelve months arrangement, he did not think they should go a moment beyond. Mr. J. Edwards raised the questions whether 7s. 6d. was the acognised fee, and how it compared with other unions. Mr. Woodward explained that the medical officers were to received is. 61. for each case entered for vacci- nation ; be. for each case attended at the homes of the people within a mile radius, and 7s. 6d. for all cases beyond one mile from their station or residences. The Chairman thought they had based their figures upon the minimum allowed by the Local Government Board, and that they compared favourably with other unions. Mr. Norman, on being informed that the twelve months had expired, said he would move that the sub- ject be reconsidered with a view to revision. Mr. Edwards seconded. Mr. Reynolds asked that the clerk provide a statement showing the cost of the present system of vaccination (in each section) compared with the old system. Mr. Norman further remarked that the feeling of the committee having the matter under consideration was that they did not want the medical men to suffer. Mr. Clerk: At the same time I don't think they want the ratepayers to suffer. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Woodward explained that under the present system there were no vaccination stations in the country, and the medical officer had to visit each home to perform the operation, and if unsuccessful they had to go again and again, so that whea they took that fact into consideration it was not so great on the face of it. Mr, Norman, whilst appreciating' that point, and the fact that the situation had been forced upon them by the new act, remarked that in the old days the people were taken to the station and vaccinated at so much a. dozen, almost. Still the doctors, as well as the commit- tee, perfectly understood, and agreed to the revision of the whole fees at the termination of a twelve month. Subsequently the financial report was adopted. TEE EFFECT OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The Master in submitting his report referi•ed to the great dearth of nurses, at the present time,. The Chairman: It is probably due to the war in the Transvaal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CHAIRMAN DEFENPS THE BOiRD.</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN DEFENPS THE BOiRD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moseley Williams (chairman) drew attention to a paragraph which appeared ia the "Runcorn Examiner" written by "A. Correspondent." He did not know whether that correspondent was the reporter for the dis- trict, nor whether he was the reporter present at the meeting, but there was one point he objected to viz., the extract reading .—"lt was evident that several of the Runcorn Guardians looked upon Mr. Meadowcroft as an obstructionist, and whether he was backed up by reason or argument, seemed to have no consideration with them and in the end he got out-voted, or in his on words "just what he expected," "and also the fol- lowing, subsequent extract, "The little incident, how- ever,illustrated that he who would secure a following on the Runcorn Board of Guardians must "fell—he took it "feel" was intended—his ground carefully. Now it did not accuse himself, as chairman, of thinking Mr. Meadowcroft an obstructionist, and he might take to himself that no one could accuse him of anything of the kind, and he was further satisfied that at that Board free expression of opinion was always granted. And there had been nothing before his presence, or that of his predecessor (Mr. Mclntyre) of an obstructive charac- ter. Rather Mr. Mclntyre had given them too much rope if anything. (Laughter.) He trusted that the writer of that paragraph would do them the favour and justice in saying that the Board did not regard any member as an obstructionist, and that they were quite satisfied that Mr. Meadowcroft was not an obstruction- ist in any way. (Hear, hear.) This concluded the business. QUEEN'S SUNDAY AT RUNCORN. Last Sunday, special sermons were preached at the Established Churches, and collections were made in aid of the Lord Mayor's Fund for the relief of soldiers, their wives, families, etc. USEFUL PRBSIINTS of every description, See Win- dow.—J. S. G.ARLAND Ironmonger, Bridge-st.—Advt. LAMPS, 011 Stoves, Coke Stoves, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—ddvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held at Dutton Workhouse, last Tuesday. There were present :—Meesrs J Knowles (chairman), Admiral Jones- Parry, J Mclntyre, T Darlington, J Stakes, W Bradford, George Piling, W Fletcher, T Lewis, T B.tater, J Mose- ley Williams, J B Chadwick, T Wright, S Horton, J W Baugley, J Spruce, P Leather, W Turner, E Carke, with George F Ashton (clerk). W E Hough (deputy clerk), James Farrington (inspector), W Diggle, and J Swinton (surveyors) and Dr J Adams (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>AN APOLOGY FROM LOWER WALTON.</head>
        <p>AN APOLOGY FROM LOWER WALTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. M-o-un-fi-eld-,-Ivy Lea, Lower Walton, Warring- ton, under data January 11th, wrote: "I find we have intdvertantly committed a breach of your bye-laws by allowing a house in Whitafield-road to be tenanted with- oat a certificate. This I regret, and hope you will over look it, as I assure you this is not a wilful disregard,but simply an error, as to the proper authority to whom this application should have been made. Your Inspec- tor Mr. Farrington kindly called my attention to the tact, for which I feel obliged. The explanation was considered satisfactory. WATBIZ SUPPLY OF GIiAPPEIiELiLL.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Deas, water and sanitary engineer, mun- cipal officers, Warrington, wrote stating that the cost of extending the present main to the point marked on en- closed tracing would bs £46 10i., it being a distance of 175 yards. The cost for water supplied to the cottages for domegtic purposes was 6 per cent on the assessment or is. 2 2-51 in the pound exactly the same terms and conditions as in town. Baths were charged extra to the above rate. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Tne consideration of this subject was deferred. SCARLET FEVER AT FRODSH&amp;M. The Clerk read the following letters : Hillsboro, Frodsham, _ January 13, 1900,</p>
        <p>To the Caairman of the Runcorn Rural Co-uncii.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l have received a letter from Dr. Adams relative to the scarlet fever case to which I drew the attention of the Council, and now enclose it with a copy of my reply. It seems to be a matter between the Coun- eil and the Medical officer. Referring to the report of the discussion at your last meeting, I may say that the people seem t 3 have done everything in their power to prevent the spread of infection, sending the other chil- dren away, and isolating the fever case; and in view of the long time between the first and second cases,it seems probable that had the house and bedding been disinfec- ted the fever would net have broken out again. Now that the whole family has had the fever, disinfection will of course ba a more difficult matter. Dr. Adams' letter does not put the matter in a satisfactory position. Yours truly,</p>
        <p>WILLIAM HUTCHINGS. [coYe.] To J. Adams Esq.—Dear Sir,—l be to thank you for your letter of January 8 about the scarlet fever case at Frodsham, but do not propose to reply to or comment on it, as it does not seem to me to be at all a question between you as medical officer and myself. I should like to say that in drawing attention to the matter, I have no personal motion or interest, beyond thinking it very hard on the f imily in question. I will, therefore, forward your letter with a copy of this to the District Geuneil.—Yours truly,—W. M. HUTCHINGS. Warrington, January 8, 1900. Mr. Hutchings, Frocistiam,—Tne District Council have laid before me your letter read at the last meeting, and lam directed to reply. The first case of scarlet fever was inspected by my partner, Dr Curtis ; the , Second case was not inspected, at a later date, it being ; seldom requisite, to make recurring visits; but when the the lass cases occurred, I went myself at as early a date as possible, and issued the usual preventive orders, in- cluding removal of clothing and bedding. This was I ' think before your letter was sent. The premise, are not favourable for the best results from the limit...l means of disinfection at our disposal. What is needed is an in- fectious diseases hospital, which is under the Council's eonsideration. I deeply regret that s,eie have suffered the inconvenience stated in your letter,and sincerely trust that no further inconvenience will be caused. A request,either from yeti, or the medical attendant for disinfection to be carried out would have met with immediate action, but, unfortunately, my colleague omitted to I;Ave the usual request for the medical attend eat to intiu.,...te when the premises would be ready. I may add that I was from home when the first case occurred.—l am, dear sir, with deep regret, faithfully yours,—Joseree ADAMS, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>The Council made no comment upon the letters, and he uext business was procedded with.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR INCREASE OF SALARY FROM THE SURVEYORS.</p>
        <p>The following applications ware read by the Clerk, and subsequently referred to the Highways Committee : —Mr. John Swinton, surveyor, wrote as follows :—Sirs, I beg to make application for an increase in my salary and hope you will kindly give the matter your favour- able consideration. I think many of you will understand that my present salary is insufficient to live on, after providing for horse, trap, and travelling expenses. I am now getting to an age when my future gives me some anxiety, as up to the present I have been unable to make any provision for old age. Mr. William Diggle, surveyor for Frodsham District, suiting under date January 12th 1900, begged most respectfully to ask if the Council would kindly eonsider his salary and the numerous duties and responsibilities devolving upon him as their surveyor with a view of granting hint an advance.. It would not be necessary for him to set out in detail the numerous additional duties which had been pla cod upon him since he was appointed, but he may be permitted to refer to the extra services rendered by him in connection with the various Paro- chial Committees of Fredsharn, Frodslaam Lordship, Heleby, and Halton, and the Joint Cammittees of Frod sham and Frodsharn Lordship. In addition he had, as engineer, prepared several plans, specifications, quan- tities and estimates for private streets works at Weston, and Frochdaant, and sewerage disposal works at Weston, Frodsham Lordship, Frodsharn and Halton. Having mud to the wishes of the Local Government Board that considerations of this character should be made by way of salary, he trusted the Council would be willing to favourably consider the application. He bad a large amount of clerical work to do in connection with his appointment which kept him employed late almost daily. The large area which he had to superintend rend-red it absolutely necessary that he should keep a horse and trap which of itself absorbed a vary large portion of his present salary. He bad always striven to do his duty to the Council and the various parochial committess in all matters and would continue to do so to the best of his ability. He trutted the Council would favourably consider his application. Admiral Jones-Parry thouglat the latter part of Mr. Diggle'e letter was all wrong, They had taken all the extra work from him and perhaps they might reduce his salary in consequence. (Laughter.) THE PROPOSED ELECTRIC RAILWAY FROM WARRINGTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TO NORTHWICII.</head>
        <p>TO NORTHWICII.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mclntyre, in submitting the reeort of the Par- liarnentary Committee, which had met that morning to interview Mr. Seotter, chief engineer of the proposed light railway from Warrington te Northwich, said they could gather from the interview that the sincere wish of the Company promoting the scheme was to please every one of the various interested parties on the route of the line. Before going further perhaps it would be best for the Clerk to read the points that had been formulated by the Committee) of the County Council, the Northwich U.D. Council, Northwieh R.D. Council, the Runcora Rural District Council and others, which had met in Chester a short time previously, and at which the Coun- cil was represented by their clerk and surveyor. The Clerk then read the following :—" The Commit- tee asked for and the report of the cemPanY Promised favourably considering the following points :—" Tna.t the company take power to acquire lands compulsorily. —That the roads over which the line is laid be not in any place less than 30 , feet for carriage way, and that they be widened at all curves. That the company complete the works within three years or such extended time as may be allowed by the Snarl of Trade. That the Councils through whose districts the line passes have a voice as to the construction of the cars to be used. That waiting room be constructed in each Parish Coun- cil District. That the various Councils be allo wed free use of the rails for their own trucks for the purpose of carrying of night soil, road material, etc., during the night time. It was also resolved that ip boa recommend. ation to the Council that subject to the company carrying out the recommendations of the committee no opposition be offered to the scheme." Mr. Mclntyre edded that was what the Parliamentary Committee passed that morning, that provided they favourably considered all those poines—which they had done—there would be no opposition from that Council. There was no question about it; it was a difficult matter to carry on large works of that kind. This person, and that person, had his particular hobby, and one wanted one thing, and one another thing. Wnich of course made things very difficult. He alluded to the fact that Stockton Heath was apparently very much opposed to the scheme, though he could not see the cause for it. un- less it was owing to the narrowness of the streets. He had put that to Mr. Scotter and he thought he would be able to meet all reasonable objection by making provi- sion for the widening of the streets. Some per- sons wanted them widened to 30 feet. but Mr. Scotter considered that most of them would be eetis- fiel with 27 feet. Still that was a subject he (Mr. .Intyre) tuoLght more for the County Council than for them. With regard to the completion of the line in 3 years, the engineer thought it would be complete in con- siderably less. Some people considered the scheme was a step in the right direction. They admitted that the district wanted opening up, and he thought it would be best to open it up with facilities for carrying goods and passengers, for wherever they were established business was increased, and indeed was bound to increase, And it would be a great benefit to the Union eventually, and might induce their friends from Warrington to go and build, where the air was good and the water was pure, a kind of health resort. (Laughter, and hear, hear). He,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Mr. Moseley-Williams, chairman of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, at the Tuesday's meeting of that Board took exception to two remarks made in my notes of January sth, relative to the attitude of the Board of Guardians towards Mr. Meadowcroft. After an attempt to connect the authorship of the notes with the local representative of the " Examiner" Mr. Moseley-Williams wound up an apparently impromptu defence of the Board by say- ing that body " was satisfied that Mr. Meadowcroft was not an obstructionist in any way whatever." I acknowledge it. What I said, was, the Board "evidently" regard Mr. Meadowcroft as an obstruc- tionist, and that I reiterate. But they treat him as such—though now they tell us they do not. " Actions," however, speak louder than words.</p>
        <p>In my opinion Mr. Meadowcroft is a legislator in spirit, and has endeavoured to be so in fact but the prejudices and the idea that what is must be of "us farmers" has caused him to be a leader with- out a party. On the assessment question with the full weight of the fifty-five Guardians of Prescot Union behind him he finds himself more alone than Robinson Crusoe on his Isle, for even he had his Man Friday. A word as to the general tenour of Mr. M. Williams' remarks. He sneaks of the "free- dom of speech " and the" length of rope " allowed to the members, and seemingly takes a pride in it. Instead of which, we know that it is policy to let some members have sufficient rope, if not an unlimited supply, though it leads to waste of precious time; for, perchance, those members would regulate their own rope in a very drastic fashion if there was any attempt to tether. As things exist at present for "plenty of rope " it would be truer to say thet the Standing Orders are, alas, too often, a dead letter.</p>
        <p>--- The annual report of the Runcorn Savings Bank shows increases of business in every department. and indicates clearly the return of prosperity to Runcorn. To qutlte Mr. W Handley (the actuary) " A satisfactory feature is the comparatively large number of Friendly and Charitable Societies having accounts with the Bank, numbering 104, and the amount invested by them is £10,962 183 llgshow- ing the provident character of the inhabitants of Runcorn, and its immediate district." As towns- people we should all be proud of these figures, and do our utmost to increase them and so increase our credit and stability. The state of the Bank is shown to be thoroughly sound, having a large sur- plus after deducting liabilities, and the auditors certificate is one of high compliment as to the accuracy of the bookkeeping. The Bank should command the confidence of all</p>
        <p>Supplementing my remarks concerning the I Transporter Bridge, the publication of the Bill dis- closes that, amongst other gentlemen, Sir John Brunner, Bart., occupies a prominent position as one of the chief promoters. I also understand that the fare has been fixed at id per foot-passenger and agreed to; and, also, that Sir John Brunner has been instrumental in getting a clause inserted , whereby provision is made for the four Councils— ' (County Councils of Cheshire and Lancashire, the Town Council of Widnes, and Runcorn Urban Dis- trict Council)—to subscribe towards its erection, should they think fit to do so. This clause should most certainly be made full use of, as the principal promoters appear to be gentlemen of such inde- pendent position, as to almost ensure the under- taking becoming a purely public trust. Let us hope each of these public bodies will be up and do- ing ; probe to the bottom the feasibility of the scheme; and if convinced of its workableness, let them support it generously and unstintedly.</p>
        <p>The scheme for a light electric railway from Warrington to Northwich gives promise of getting through all right. Locally it is well received, and the company and local governing bodies appear to be workiug very harmoniously ; the company on their part desire to meet" everyone's" wishes, and if the units that make up the " everyone ' only wish reasonably, the scheme will in all probability be carried out.</p>
        <p>Another scheme for the establishment of an elec- tric railway and tram service from Warrington to Weston does not seem to be progressing so favour- ably. Should it ever attain fruition it will un- doubtedly, prove a boon to the town and be very popular, The popularity of the scheme, however, would receive a considerable fillip at Runcorn if a branch line to Weston Point was included in the venture. The need of an accessible road to that growing centre cannot be too strongly dealt upon; and the inclusion of it in the proposals of the scheme would considerably enhance its value to Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Runcorn won, and Rochdale Hornets lost two points last Saturday, and there is no question as to which team was the better, for truly can it be said that the " Linnets " outshone the" needlemen " as greatly as the sun outshines the moon. Still the game was not anyway satisfactory.</p>
        <p>The secret of Lha dissatisfaction lay in the weak- ness of the referee. One may suppose that the Northern Union referees are pArfection,but one soon has the delusion dispelled when brought face to face with such adjudicators as the one who officiated at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday Runcorn visit Swinton, and will most likely experience a sad reverse. One would like them to win, but loeking at past records, Swinton has always proved an ill-fated ground for Runcorn. Still the" Linnets "are taking a good team and will try hard to maintain their lead over Oldham.</p>
        <p>- FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>PARISH CIIIIRCH.—On Sunday special sermons were to have been preached on behalf of the Curates' Aug- mentation Fund, but in the absence of the secretary through an attack of influenza. the Vicar, the Rev. H. B. Blogg preached. The offertories amounted to 27 17s 6d. FIVE CROSSES INFANTS' SCHOOL. —The children attend- ing the Five Cr)sees Infants' School, (Miss Dallirnore, headmistress), on Saturday were, through the kindness of Mies Ashley, treated to an excellent tea in the school- room, after which Miss Voilet Hayes distributed oranges to each. In addition to the teachers there were also present Mrs Blegg, Miss B Lewis, Miss N Wilkinson Miss Ashton, and Mr E G Steward. INFANTS' SCHOOL TEA PARTY.—The annual tea to the scholars attending the Church-street Infants' Schwl was held in the schoolroom on Friday afternoon. Ths ex- penses of the tea were defrayed from the proveds of the recent successful concert given. The room had been prettily decorated by Miss Harrison and her staff, Each child received a beautiful Christmas gift from Miss Vmlet Hayes, who ever takes a kindly interest in the welfare of the children. Subsequently they were also preshated with buns and'oranges.</p>
