        <p>INSPECTION INVITED</p>
        <p>NAVIGATION OF THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>OUR</p>
        <p>Damage for Sinking a Runcom Flat. At the Liverpool County Court, on Saturday, before his Masseur Judge Collier, assisted by nautical assessors Captains Quin() and Thompson, an Admiralty action was heard, in whieh Richard Abel, owner of the narrow boat Nellie, was plaintiff, and the owners of the steemship Duke of Leinster, which is a trader between Manchester and Dublin,were defendants. The claim was for damage sustained by the wash of the steamer when passing the Nellie on the Manchester Ship Canal at Rencern,causing her to sink. The amount of the damages to be assessed. Mr. A. D. Bateson (instructed by Messrs. Bateson, Warr and Wireshurst) was for the plaintiff, and Mr. A. G. Steel (instructed by Messrs. Simpson,North and Co.) represented the defendants. William Millington, master of the Nellie, said that on the 11th August, about 4 30 a.m.,len was moored against the quay at Wigg Bros.' works at Runcotn. The Albert (another narrow boat) was on the starboard side of him. His beat was ready to discharge. N heard the "swirl" of the Duke of Leinster coming along the wall. The steamer was coming at a tremendous pace, and witness hailed her, saying she would do some demage at that pace. Shamowever, kept coming on till she saw the boat filling with water, and then she stopped. The " flush ' from the Leinster filled the boat, and the other boat had to be quickly loosed, or she al-o woule have sunk. The speed would be about eight or nine miles. In a narrow way like this the water runs up the bank when a packet comes along. Mr. Bateson said he understood the defence was that the Nellie had no business to be in the canal, but he must say he could not appreciate that. Witness, continuing, said the boats have no protection to prevent the water coming over the side if there is any wash. All firms use side-elolas with the objeet of protecting the cargo from rain, but as he only carried sulphur stones he did not require to be kept dry. When fully loaded his freeboard would be about 10 inches. He was not then fully loaded, and his freeboard would be about 13 to 14 inehes, and not S inches only as counsel suggested. He was headed to Manchester. Even if he carried side-clothe they would have been off at the time because he was stripped to discharge. The Judge : Apparently the flush or wave was ahead of the steamer, and the assessors say that would be im- possible. According to the witness the Duke of Leins- ter stopped, and went astern sloe ly when he saw the boat filling. The witnese, recalled, said the Leinster shut steam off when 50 yards off, and she shot past witness; she gave a stroke or two astern, when she had just got past. She saw what she had done as she went past, and kept go- ing on, and did not stop altogether. There was no other steamer navigating the canal at the time, Albert Abel, son of the owner, said this was the only time they had had an accident of this kind on the canal. For the purposes of dues, he reternei the beat at about 30 tons, which was not her full carrying capacity. Nis boats had no sidecloths. The question of water coming from the canal is never considered. The cloths are to keep the rain out. For the defence. Joseph Swords' master of the paddle steamer Duke of Leinster, which is 289 tons register, and about 250 feet long, said he had had considerable experience of the Ship Canal. On 11th August be sailed from Dublin for Manchester, and entered the canal at daylight (4,22 a.na.), on the 12th in clear weather. He and his mate were on the bridge. He went slow, half speed, and stopped occasionally. After passing the swing bridge he put the telegraph at half speed. The narrow boats were a distance away, but a steamboat lying in the wharf prevented him seeing them. Ha opened out this two narrow boats after be got round the bend. The ship was going about five knots, and when he got abreast them he stormed on being hailed from the boats. Before the bailing he did not anticipate danger ter them. The wash undoubtedly did sink this lat. He had often seen these flats in the canal and it was usual to have is protection ; some have canvas and some heave boards. It he had seen the narrow boats in timer he would have gone dead slow, provided he was making too much wash. There is a corner at the lay-by which obscures the view. The canal here is about 200 feet wide. If the boats bad had protection there would have been no damage. Alexander Carmichael, mate of the Leinster, said the order was given to go half speed. George Neasley.marine superintendent of the Eastham sectiereof the Manchester Ship Canal, said the Nellie had no protection, but every other owner who sends narrow boats into the canal has so:r s protecti4n from the wash, whatever the cargo may be. Protection would not avail against improper speed. The speed islimited to six miles but with special permission eight miles. He did not consider the narrow boats, with the great traffic on the Ship Canal, are fit without protection. Ho had not known any other narrow boat sunk at Wiee's Works. Addressing the court. Mr. Steele admitted that the Leinster's wash sank the boat,and he put the point, was the Leinster right in taking the course she did of going half speed? Again, he submitted that although there had not been any accidents which have been directly at- tributable to the fact of these narrow boats being unpro- tected, on the evidence it was abundantly clear that in an unprotected state they are a danger to themselves, and in certain circumstances they undoubtedly become dangemus to others. Further, he submitted, that It seemed probable that if the ware was of such a volume as to go over the cabin it would have snok the Albert. The probability was that the wave after hitting the wall of the canal fell hack into the Nellie. Mr.Batsson submitted that it being admitted that the wash sank the flat, and that it was moored in a proper place in daylight, the onus was on the defendants to show that they were not at fault. No protection would have prevented this accident, because the water came over the stern. He put it as a general proposition that nobody was allowed to create suck a wash as would sink a properly equipped barge entitled to use the canal, and being there lawfully, having paid toll. Judge Collier put to the assessors the following ques- tions, to which the appended answers were returned :- 1. Was the sinking of the Nellie caused by her not having been properly protected by her being overloaded? —No. 2. Was the Nellie se loaded as rot to have sufficient freeboard for the navigation of the canal ?—No. 3. Was the sinking caused by the Duke of Leinster navigating.the canal at too great a speed, taking all the circumstances inte consideration ?—Yes. 4. Was the sinking caused by a bad lookout on the part of the Duke of Leinster P—Ne. 5* Did the Duke of Leinster take a proper course ? Yee. 6. Could those on board the Nellie have avoided the wash which came on board ?--No. Consequent on these answers and the aelv;ce he had received, his Honour held that the Duke of Leinster was alone to blame, and that there was DO negligence attri- butable to the Nellie. Judgment was given for the plaintiff with costs.</p>
