      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>NFLUENZ A. INCREASING.</head>
        <p>NFLUENZ A. INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The lefluenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly en the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circuntstancea which bring it abut is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he had only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Com- plaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of In- fluenza, whose after effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad:day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has :lever since failed to revive at its due time, and to excort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Headache, accompanied by difficulty of breath- ing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of GwilYne Evans' Quinine Bit- ters. By common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an at- tack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness and Lan- your, with greater success than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indiges- tion, Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Nervoueness, and Loss of Appetite. Gwilym Evans' Qiiinine Bitters stands un- equalled. It is sold in bottles 2s 9d and 46 fit, by all Chemists, and Patent Medicine Vendors, or direct car- riage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name " Gwilytn Eva* s " on Label Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>NEGLECT Or CHILDREN AT RUNCORN. William and Elizabeth Baxter, man and wifa, residing at 2, Ashwortla's-row, Windmill-street, Runcorn, and late of Warrington, were charged before Messrs. A. Thomas and F. Wiswall, at the Runcorn Police Court, with the wilful and cruel neglect of their four children, on the fitb of January and divers other dates, whereby unnecessary suffering and injury was caused to their health.—Mr. Knowles, Widneseprosecuted for the police. —Evidence was given by Pelice-Constables Carrington and Fry, and Inspector Hackett, of the N'S.P.C.C. (War rington) to the effect that the house of the defendants was visited by them. The children were found sitting on a bucket before the fire, which was very scanty, with their feet in the ash-hole. There was only one aid chair in the room. Upstairs there was an old rickety bedstead and a chaff bed which was so small as to be inadequate for the six perms who slept on it—eho defendants and the four children—who slept three at the top and three at the bottom. On the occasion of one visit Constable Carrington found one of the children with its head _dangling between the irons of the bed. It we 3 a most dangerous position. Inspector Hackett, who bad examined the children testified t) their being well nourished and fed, but they were filthy dirty—the colour of their bodies being " copper colour "—and very much vermin bitten. There was not sufficient clothing in the house and hardly any furniture whatever. A large iron vessel containing unwholesome m ttter was under the bed, and the corners of the room were evidently made receptacles for sweepings. The state of things was attributed to the mother, who it was said was ad- dicted to drink. The husband earned 24s or 25; a week and gave his wife a pound a week. He was characterised as a hardworking respectable fallow.—For the defence the male defendant stated that previous to his leaving Warrington he was out of work a month, and since he had been in Runcorn (a matter of six weeks) he had to spend the first week in looking for work. Under the circumstances be did not think he cnild do anything more.—The female stated that the reason the constables found the children in a "wet and dirty state" was because they had gone out and got wet through just after she had put them in clean things, and on coming home she had to put them back in the dirty, but dry clothes, whilst she 'encashed and dried the others.—Mary Gee, wife of David Ges, 21, Windmill-street. and Maria Ross, wife of James boss, 2.58, Winwick-street. Warrington, sister of the female defendant, gave evidence : the former as to mangling the clethin for the defendants, and the latter as to her sister being in the habit of taking beer, and having fallen into bad company. She characterised the husband, however. as "the best husband any woman could have." —The Chairman observed they were going to deal leniently with defendants on the understanding that they would do their best to reform. They would be bound over in P-51 each to come up for judgment when called upon within the nest six months. In the meantime the police would have their eye upon them and they trusted defendants would reform and get some proper furniture and bedding.—The female: I will watch it does not occur again.—The Chairman : I hope so ; for if you come again, and lam here, you will be very severely dealt with.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT TiVosfeN.—POLICE OPFICER AND A. BOY ASSAULTED. _ _</p>
        <p>At the Warrington rolice Court on Tuesday, befere Mr. Birchall and Councillor Sasethurst,lldary Fitzpatrick was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Sankey-street at 6-35 the previous night. She was further charged with assaulting Joseph Oakes, a boy, at the same time and place and also -grth asiaulting P.S. Cheeney at Bank Quay Station at 2-10 ha the aftnaoon. —Prior to the evidence being called, the Chief Constable</p>
        <p>- explained that the prisoner was the wife of a man who was dealt with at the Court the previous morning. lie was being escorted to gaol together with' some other prisoners in the afternoon when his wife most savaeely assaulted the officer who was in charge of him.—P.C. Whittaker said that at 8-35 p.m. en Moadcty he was on duty at Market Gab.e when he received information that a woman was creating a disturbance at the top of Cairo- street. On proceeding there he saw the prisoner, who was under the influence of drink aeting in a disorderly manner. She was making use of filthy language and struck a boy twice, nearly knocking him through a shop window. P.C. furner came on the scene and they took her into cugtody.—The boy Oakes was called and said that the prisoner struck him in the chest and ribs with her fist without any provocation. She was drunk.—P.S. Cheeney said that at 2.10 p.m. he was escorting five priseners te Mancueeter. lie was about to enter the room leading to the Booking Office when the prisoner stepped in front of him to stop him from going in. He got hold of her and put her out of the way. She was under the influence of drink. When he was going through the ba:rier someone shouted " Look out sergeant" and on turning round he saw the prisoner abeut to strike him. She kicked him twice on the legs, but he prevented her from doing anything further.— The Chief Cenittble said be had known the woman for great number of years. She wee an old criminal,hav- int served a term of penal servitude. She and her hus- band ware travelling criminals. One of the most earious assaults it was possible to commit was to assault an wort in charge of convicted prisoners because it might lead to the prisoners eecaping.—Prisoner was sent to .gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MINOR CASES,</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES,</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wright, Dr. Hornell. Councillor Smethnrst. and Mr. J. Hepherd. FAMILY BEGLECT.—MiehaeI Murphy, 3, Coekhedge-lane, was charged on a warrant with running away from the Borough on the let of June 1899, leaving his wife and five children chargeable to the Union.—Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Board of Guardians. He said that prisoner's wife and children bezame chargeable to the Union in June, prisoner having deserted them. They were not admitted to the Workhouse. but were provided with 43 9d worth of food each week for nine weeks. Afterwards so far as he (Mr. Sheerer) could make out he had sent home money for some Pine. He returned home at Christmas. He (Mr. Shearer) asked for an adjoirnment fot a month to g.:ve prisoner an Opportunity of paying the money.—The case was adjourned accordingly.</p>
      </div>
