        <p>ALL COUNTRY PEOPLE should real The Rural World d. weekly, which contains artLtles oc It.. ses, Cattle, thie Dairy, Poultry, Bees, I? tbbits, Pigs, Gardening, Ntturai History, Botany of the Farm and Garden, the Home. Foreign and Colonial Intelligence. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustraLed and is altogether, a most interest• in; and instructive farming ani country paper. A good medium for servants requiring tituations. The Manazer ffen to send a specimen tree. The address is 110 and 11 STRIND, London, W.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>MornaY.—Before Mr. F, Monks (in the chair), Alderman 1 Blackburst, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs. T. Birchal and S. Wright. THEFT or A BICYCLE.—Harry William Herbert, last from Manchester, was charged on remand that he, being the bailee of a bicycle, tiae property of the WarrinWon Syndisate Limited on September 30th, did co .vert the same to his ewn use and thereby steal it—He pleaded guilty—Mr. W. J. For- shaw prosecuted on behalf of the Syndicate. He said that the prisoner was employed by the Syndicate for a short time as a cycle maker at their works in Liverpool-road. On the 30th September he was told that his services woal I no louver be required as there was not sufficient work for him to do. He asked if be °laid hire a bicycle to go to Liverpool to look for work. tie was allowed to hire a second hand machine value 25 for a week. Nothing further was heard of hirn and the m usage- was sent to Liver- pool to find prisoner's whereabouts. As he could not find him the matter was put in the hands of the police and it was found that prisoner had sold the bicycle to a coachman at Chester for 50s. Superintendent Monaghan subsequently arrested him in Manchester and he admitted having sold the machine.—The Chief Constable said the police had not dis- covered anything as to the man's antecedents.—lt appeared that since leaving Warrington he had served a term of im- prisonment for stealing another bicycle.—Prisoner was com- mitted to gaol for two months with hard labour. Meowamarir ASSAULT IN MERSET•STRETIT.—ThOTDAS Fitz- patrick and John Murray were charged, the former with assaulting Charles Naylor in Mersey-street at four o'clock on Saturday afternoon, and the latter with having at the same time and place.—Charles Nayloe. a joiner, residing at 24. Eldon-street. said that at four o'clock en Saturday afternoon he was passing along Mersey-street near Hall-street when he saw the prisoners. Murray stopped him and asked him for a few coopers. He told him he had none to give him, and Fitzpatrick deliberately struck him in the eye, knocking him to the groni 1. As he was getting up he struok him in the mouth. A postman then came on the scene, and the prisoners seemed to take him for a policemen and "totted." Apoliceman was sent for and they were taken into custody. —Thomas Walsh, a postman residing at 8. Fothergill-street, gave evidence.—The Chief Constable &amp;aid people were often pestered by violent beggars after dark.—Prisoners denied that they were begging. —The magistrates deoided to send Fitzpatrick to goal for two months with bard labour, and Murray for a month with hard labour. ALLEGED THErr OF BOOTS. —Bridget White or Lailey was charged with being found with a pair of boots in her posses- sion. suppo,od to have been stolen on Saturday night.—The Chief Constable in asking for a three day's remand, in order that the palice might ascertain who the owner of the boots was, said that shortly after ten o'lloak on Saturday night, Sergeant Lewis saw the prisoner offering a pair of new boots in pledge. Knowing her ta be a notedous thief he questioned her and-she said they were not her boots. A woman had given them to her in Ship-yard, Prisoner was going under the name of Clare. As she was unable to give any satisfac- tory account of how she obtained the boots she was locked up. The boots bid never been worn, but were rubbed in the mud. —The remand was granted.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY—Before Mr. T. Birchen (in the chair), and Council ,r Smetlanrst.</p>
        <p>Ac ICM CREAM DEALER IN IR 11113 .—On the 1111218 Of Barnacle Monfredi, r-a ice cream dealer, being called, the Chief Constable said that the case was one of opening two re- freshment houses during prohibited hour. on Christmas Day. Mr. Browne was appearing for the defendant and agreed with him (Mr. Talbot) to take an adjournment for a fortnight In order that a somewhat vexed point of law might be reerred to "The justice of the petce." Mr. White, the ranistrates' clerk, advised the law in a certain way which was corroborated by the answer they had received from 'The justice of the peace." Mr. Manfredi was clearly in the wrong in opening his houses on that czeasion.-but he did it on the advice of Mr. Browne, who gave him the a lvice in perfect goad faith and not in open defiance ef the law. The point was a somewhat vexed one and somewhat obscure and one might be pardoned for havinl made some little error in its interpretation. Taking this into consideration ha (the Chief Constable) was willing to withdraw the case on defendant raving the costs and giving an undertaking that he would not again break the law now that the point bad been made clear —The case was accordingly withdrawn. The costs amounted to 7s.</p>
        <p>A NEGE.IGENT FIT-SER.—Arthur Longshaw was charged that he, being a person able to maintain his family, did neglect to do ea, whereby his three children became charge- able to the Union —Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Board o! Guardians. Ile said that the prisoner was last admitted to the Workhouse with his wife and children en the 4th of the present month. They had been lathe Workhonse the greater part of the past two years, and had cost the Union over £lOl. Prisoner was an able bodisi man, but he would not keep his family. Ile was formerly connected with the greeagrocery business, but lately had not done any• work. Situations had been found for him but he would not ta,,e them. Work was found for him last week,but instead of taking the situat'on ha went about the town drinking. His wife was just as bad if not worse. The family had been admitted nine times during the last two years.—The Chief Constable described the prisoner as a Inv, idle thief. He was almost too lazy to steal anything heavy. Ile had been convicted eight times for various offen3es. The last time waS in 1897 at Liverpool Sessions when the had three mo tbs imprisonment. He had had two months for neglect of family.—Prisoraer was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>_ DISGRA.CEFUT, Co:M/0r IN BUTTCRICA.RIC IT-B'n ETT -- George Williams or Waaver was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street at 4.20 the p-eyious afternoon.—The Chief Constable said that at the time in question he was in Batter -narket-screst wises he noticed the prisoner, wha was strongly under the influence o! drink pushing pt wle off the footpath. Ile (Mr. Talbot) was talk- ing to afr. Viney, the Postmaster, at the time. and the prisoner came across t'.e road and deliberately drove himself between them, m skiez, use of a foul expression. They took no notice of him, and he then went into several shops annoy- ing people. Seeing that be was determined to ge' into trouble he (the Chief Constable) called an officer and he was taken la the Pelee Station.—Prisoner was a stranger to the town, and strangers to the :own ~hought taiy could do just what they lik ?Ai. If Warrington accpired a reputation for severe dealing they wcrild p Thy, give it a wider berth. —A fine of 5s and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY—Before Messrs. Geddes (in the chair). and Birchall.</p>
        <p>SvEeTfl APPEARINCE. —Eliza Smith. 60 years of age, who made her 77th appearance was committed to gaol for a month for being drunk and disorderly In Guardian- street at 11-33 he previous morning. BEGGIVG. -Samuel Mitchell, was charged with begging at six o'clo3lc ths previous evening.--Ele pleaded guilty.—P.O. Turner said that at the time In crestion he and P.C. Clough were on duty in plain clothes, and saw the prisoner go into Mr. Honghton's pawnshop. Re asked for some coppers. and thee' took him into custody. —Prisoner was sent to gaol for 7 than hard labour. _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>. . THicrr OFAS IRON GATE.—John Edwards was charged with stealing from the premises at the rear of 57, Hors.- market-street at 7-30 ()I Tuesday sight. an iron gate, valued at Vs. the property of Jose.ih Banks.—lie pleaded guilty.— P.C. Turner said that at ths time named he was on duty in company with P.C. Clough in plain clothes In Golden Grove- street. Prisoner came :ep carrying the iron gate produced. They asked him where he got it from and he made use of filthy lanvage. lie ultimately said he was taking it to Ptiastotes, the lyokers They took him into cuttody and brought hint to the Police Fitat;on. It was ascertained that the gate betonged to Mr. Jo.;eoh Banks, hosier, S 7, Ho:se- market-street. The prisoner was drunk.—Prisoner : I was very drunk and didn't know what I was doing.—The Chief Constaqle said he oufett realty to have asked for a remand as it was found that prisoner had been dealing in lead which had been stolen. The qamatity: however was not large, but it was suFfic'lat to make it evident that the man was living by theft. Treat P 7,13 hr 31th Rope trance, and he had been before the Court many times for ther.t.—Prisoner was com- mitted to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—B store Mr. John Painlough Sin the &amp;Wry,</p>
        <p>Aldermen Sutton and Blackhurst and 311:.-J. Haphard.</p>
        <p>NoToItIoUS THIEF SMARMY PUNlsHED.—Bridget Whites or Lalley was charged on remand with stealing from the door- way of the chop No. 9 Ho-samarket-street.semetime hetween 12 o clock neon and 12 midnight on Saturday a pair of lady's b.oots valued at 4s lid the property cal Messrs Stead and Simpson, Lsmited.—ln answer ta the Clerk, prisoner at first 801 she preferred to be tried at the Lieerpeol Sessions, but subsequen,ly changed her mind aid elected to be dealt with sunimarily.—She pleaded not gullty.—Arthur Allen, an assistant in the employ of Si[e3srs Stead and Simpson said ,the boots woduced were the property of the firm. He saw them hanging at the shop door on a rod at noon on Saturday and missed them when ha finished work at 12 o'clock at nii,ht.—Josenh Haery ;long.)_ ton, pawnbeokee, of Mersey-street said that at 10-10 on Saterday night, prisoner came to his shop and offered the boots in pledge for 31 6d. Knowing her to te a persistent shop-lifter and a notorious thief he detained her and sent for the police. She said the boots were her own and gave the name of Bllz ibeth Clare.—P.S. Lewis said he received the prisoner into custody from the last witness and took her to the Police Station. Ha endeavoured to ascertain the owner of the boots, but it was not till Monday that it was found out who they belonged to. On the 16th inst he charged prisoner with the theft and she replied " didn's 'steal them."— Prisoner had nothing to say.—Superintendent Monaghan said that wee the women's 49th appearance. She had had three months twice in succession, the last time for stealing boots.—Prisone7 was committed to gaol for three months with hard labour. ANITHER Titerr ov BoOT3.—Sarah Maloney was charged with stea:ing from the doorway of the shoo No. 4, Horse- market-street, betweee 11.0 and 6.30 the previous evening,a pair of hoots value 6s 6d, the property of Messrs. James Baker and Sons.—Superintendent Monaghan said that at 6.30 Dectlve Foster met the prisoner at Bridge Foot with the boots in her posession. lie broagh: her to the Police Station and made inquiries and it was found that the boots were stolen from the doorway of Messrs. Baker and Sons' shop.—Prisoner, who made her 11th appearance, was com- mitted to gaol for a month with hard labc Ir. DRUYKENNE33.—Bdith Connolly or Jones was committed to goal for a month with hard labour for being drunk and disorderly in Marsh House-lane at 10.55 the previous night ; Thomas Roscow and John Penn, were fined 5s for being ci-unk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street at 1.30 p.m, the previous day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>County Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDxranir.—Before Views, J. White (in the chair, and</p>
        <p>3. Ball.</p>
        <p>Highatn. Woolston, was fine"' 2s 6:1 fcr! having no light attached to his bicycle on the night of the 11th inst Henry Bennett, Sankey, was fined bs for a sintilar off,moce ; Henry }Olson was fined 5s for not hiving his hot .to under proper control at Penketh, on the 6th inst.</p>
