      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EXAMINER, FREDAY JANUARY 19, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FREDAY JANUARY 19, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>I N view of the approaching Fo Abell Beason, it may be in teresting to state that tha world-famed manuf icturers Ggo. G. BUSSEr 8; Ca., of London. are prepa,ing a stook o Balls, which for quality. uniformity. ant durability, will b unique. These balls will be distinguished by the appAaranc thereon of their well known trade mark 4-Gese. Ask the local Sports Dealer for their Catalogue. If not procurable. apply to 36 &amp; 33, Queen Victorla-atreet London, Factories —Peckham, London; and Blnis well, Suffolk.—Adot.</p>
        <p>* * Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of mttches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office, 13.twsey-street, Warrington, n3t later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LAN-C-AS-H-IRE. SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LAN-C-AS-H-IRE. SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Swinton's First Win at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Swinton accomplished a performance they have never done before, by defeating Warrington at Wilderspool last Saturday. For years they have had to succumb on the Warrington ground, no matter how strong a team they brought.but on Saturday they succeeded in getting the better of the encounter by a single peint. Both clubs were strongly represented, and the weather' beirig fine a crowd of about 5.003 people witnessed the match, which was splendidly contested notwithstanding a cross- wind which hampered both sides. Mr.S.Williams(Salford) was the referee, and the teams lined out as follows : Swieton . Goosiman,full back ; Lewis,Messer.faylor and Hampson,threequerters ; "Dai" Davies and Morgan half-backs: Evans, Vigore, Elolmes, Hsrris,Tickle, Pres- ton. Jones, and Pollitt, forwards. Warrington: Boscow, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Smith, and Jones, three-quarters; Duckworth and Allen half backs ; Taylor, Boardman, Morrison, Fairhurst, Eden, Wray/gather, Hatton and Scholtzs, forwards. Jones started the game for Warrington with a Sue kick into the Swinton quarter. Here a penalty was given against the visitors, and Warrington pressed. Allen punted strongly from touch, and Tigors mulling the catch a try was nearly registered. Allen got pos- session from the pack and threw to Smith, who failed to get clear and feed Jones. Again the ball Came out on the home side and Smith took the pass, but ran foolishly into the thick of his opponents instead of sending to Isherwood. Several passing movements by Warrington same to nothine, Isherwood on one occasion throwing wildly over Fish's head. Morrison got posseseion after speedy folio wing-up,hut knocked on near the line. where some tight scrimmages were formed. Lewis broke away only to be bundled in touch by Allen and Isherwood. SwintSh continued to act on the defensive, and had several near escapes. At last Boscow fumbled in returning a kick and the speedy Lewis was upon him in a second. Play now proceeded in the home half, Hampsoa making a smart dash for the line, but Boscow tackled grandly. Warrington raced back again, Jones trying hard to get in at the corner. Then Morgan dodged into the open and punted across to Lewis, who looked all over a scorer when Fish dashed up and saved amid cheers. Isherwood took the ball from Fish and banged it down the field again, Jonas catching at top speed and dashing to the corner flag where he was again pulled down. The effort was a brilliant one and was deservedly cheered. Boscow was tackled again and Swinton had a turn on the attack, Lewis being robbed on the line. A determined siege of the horns line followed. Lewis fleshly sending in toneh. to Warrington's relief. The game fluetuated.and Warrington attacked, Fish securing and passing Good- man in clever style only to be grassed from behind by Lewis. Swinton got back to the other end by com- bined play, and from a scrimmage near the lino.Motgan outwitted the home becks and scored. Goodman failed at goal. The reverse roused Warrington and they made a furious onslaught on the Swinten line. Goodman mulled badly, and Eden raced over near the posts, frantic cheers greeting the try. Groans followed when the referee disallowed the point for some 1v0r,,-.71 reason. Warrington continued to attiek, nee tt.,.; the hardest of luck behind the posts where the bail was knocked from under him. Half time, Swinton one try. Warriegten nil. Evans restarted and Swinton were at once aggreaeiva. Morgan dodged towards the line and then kicked over, Fish's speed etcabling him to save at a critical moment. Morgan was to the fore a moment later, with a deo.) at glee, the halt hitting the pas; and falling ineo play. Davies tied to cross but was held, end some evoking times followed until a free kick for Wsreington redeeed the pressure, temporarily. Smart iv.s dog emeriti the Swinton backs gave Lewis a chance, aed the flier was all but in at the corner when Fish upset him. At last Warrington got away, and Swinton had to defend. Toe home team played despsretely in answer to the shouts of their supporters, Mere weather being collared a yard from toe line. Off- side pity by Swietoa gat them inte trouble and Jones reduced the lead with a fine penalty goal. Warr:niton kept hotly at it, but the defence was very strong J 113 tried at goal again from near centre, the ball going true for the mark but falling a foot or two sbort.Th aFs was cheered for a wonderful piece of dodging cbcra tnn-h, and it looked as if Warriegton must aeon. Davies and !Jameson changed the venue by a clewer piece of piec- ing, but liVarrington attack,el again before the close. Isherwool tried to win the match with a drop at goal, but luck was against him as the ball just passed entiide the posts. , The final soon came, Swinton winning a hard game by a try (3 points) to a goal (2 points). Runcorn Secure a Further Two Points.</p>
        <p>Before a crowd of considerably less than 2,003 Roch- dale Hornets faced Runcorn on the Irwell-lane enclosure, in a damp cold bleak atmosphere, and on a heavy greasy ground. Ths previous encounter at Rochdale, was a victory for the " linnets " by eight points to nil ; but anything more than a bare victory was net anticipated when about 23 minutes after time Taylor started for the homesters. The teams were as fellows :</p>
        <p>Runcorn : Houghton. back ; C Butterworth, J Butter- worth, Wilkinson and Jones, (three-quarters) ; Faulkner and Jolley (halves) ; Reece. Walker, Lightfoot, Taylor, Gavter, Darlingteu, Tomling" Langley (forwards). R ichdale : Rig, (back) ; Wightman. Davies, Platt, and Vaughan (three-quarcers) ; Kruger and Allen, (halves) ; Gillislarith, Chadwick, Ashworth. Trembath, Langhorae, Wilson, Wood, and Howarth (forwards). Early on, Runcorn showed a superiority over Roch- dale, and some quick passing initiated by Faulkner took the ball dangerously near to Rige's charge. C. Roam.- worth could net, however, be blamed for not taking an impossible pass. The first real advantage was gained by Wilkinson, who marked near the visitors' poste. The cross wind just toot J. Butterworth's effort at conver- sion outside. Shortly after Rochdale rushed away and Houghton was tackled before ke could clear. Kruger was useful several times, and extricated his team from many difficulties. Runcorn were called upon to defend once ar twice at this stage, but Rochdale had the worst e.r. luck. Securine a pars,cons:quent upon a mistake on Rachdale's part, Taylor got an opening which resulted in a five yards serum. Receiving the heal ant, Jolley popped over as fins a drop as could be desired. Roch- dale backs then got away with zeal, and good worn was accomelished by their forwards wheeling the scrum- m.ges, but perhaps it was to Kruger alone that several of their headway naavement3 ware due. Before half time Jones got an opening, hut failed to use it. At the inter- val Runcorn led by two points to nil. The second half opened more favourably to Runcorn, and the forwards showed up keener than ever. The game deve'oped rouel-, and even " free-fighting " was indulged in ; but luckily, as it transpired, the referee did not turn anyJne off, though he would have been better thought of had he done so. Rochdale succeeded in keeping Runcorn at bay for some titn., but Faulkner making considerable way, gave to 0. Butterworla most opportunely, and he scored at the corner. Houghton failed to increase the advantage. Indiffirent play fol- lowed, Runcorn having considerably the best of a poor game. J. Butterworth scored from a pass by Faulkner, hut again the attAmpt at goal-kicking was abortive. Walker, Jones, Butterworth and Ligatfoot were several times dangerous, hut could not secure any material ad- vantage. One run by Batterworth was especially ex- citing but Rigg was too good a back to be caught nap- ping. Final result : Runcorn one goal two tries (eight points), to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Swinton v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>This match was ph7ol at wilt•on on Saturday in damp and cold weather. Warrington were minus Hallam and played oily 14 men- It will los remembered that Cats match at Warrington was notable because Swinton, although behind at he interval by 8 points to 3 gained a splendid victory by 16 points to 11. Th:t opening, stages of the game under notice com- manned will Swintm playing up the slope. Play was even until the hnnas full bactc almost let the visitors in. After twenty minutes' play Wightman with a timely pass from Pearson scored at the corner for Swinton, the kick at goal failing. Following this the visitors pressed nutil Swinton kicked dead. Warrinton now _l's turn</p>
        <p>attacked, wh..m a kick by Pearson relieved. Half-time: Swinton, 1 try, (3 rmints); Wa7rington 0. Final, Swin- ton 11 points, Warrington nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Newton-le-Willows v. Wirral Railways.</p>
        <p>Railw..ys were the visitors to Nursery Park on St,a...Aay in the Liverpool and District Counbina tion. Newton were strongly represented and the visitors were also at their full strength. Owing to the recent heavy rains the ground was very heavy and this accounted for the poor exhibition of football given by both sides. The houtesters had the bet of the play at the outset, but they failed to make any impreasion ea the Wirral de- fence. The °lay all through was of a very scrambling nature the Z. Jwtoners easily holding the yisitora well in hand, ;Jut, up t tc interval' no sore had been</p>
        <p>THB NEW FRFNCH REMEDY. THERAIPION This successful and highly, . popular remedy, as em- ployed in the Continental Hospitals by Rieord RostanJobet, Velpeau, and others, surpasses everything hitherto employed for impurity of the blood, spots. t lotchos, pains and swell- ings of the joints, kidney, leladder, and urinary dlseaces, 4: stricture, discharges, plies, avel, pains in the back, rheu- matism, gout, exhaustion,eleeplessness,narvom and ph vsical debility, etc. Therapion is prepared in three nifferent forms Nos. 1,2, and 3, according to diseasas for which intended. Full p qticulars send stamped adiressed envelope for phamphlet to</p>
        <p>MR. R. JOHNSON, 43, HOLFORL-SQUARE, LONDON, W.C.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By "Oval ist.] "Alone we did it," might be Switatcpf.; cry after Saturday's match, for they Ercovd their very first win on Warrington territory. But the qul-,ation would not be the truth, the whole truth, ani noWng but the truth, for that important individual, the ieforee, assisted very largely in Warrington's defeat. Next to winning the championship, the one thing needful to the Swintonian footballer's happiness has been for years a victory at Warrington. The " Lions " have played for it, prayed for it, even cursed for it, but no ! it seemed that success was never to be theirs at Warrington. Until last Satur - day! Than after only defeating Warrington by a narrow margin at Swinton and showing only moderate form the "Lions "came, saw, and conquered at Wilder- spool, where angels wept. strong men groaned, and ordinary mortals said a lot of things they should not have said el near Sunday.</p>
        <p>lam sorry "Jim Val" was not playing, as he had not missed the annual funeral at Warrington for a long tune until the very day on which it was postponed. That they managed it without him is all the more to Swinton's credit—and I do give them credit for the win, though they were distinctly lucky. It was a bitter disappoint- ment to the Warrington men, for they had msntally determined that Swiaton should never break the record, and but for one or two unfortunate circumLtances their spirits would now be in the ascendant instead of down, vary low down.</p>
        <p>Both sides played their picked teams, so that there was no advantage accruing to either side from the weak- ness of the opponent, and that a Titanic struggle should follow was only natural. Warrington started w. 41, but , they did not show that superiority forward which was so evident at Swinton. Consequeatly the task of scoring was no easy one. A prolonged attack was kept up on the Swinton line, several attempts at scoring failing at the critical moment. Then Boecow was caught napping, Swinton raced to the other end, and very soon after Morgan slipped over in a quiet, unassuming manner whieh left nothing to be desired, except the goal. Warrington executed a frontal attack in turn and Joubert—l mean Goodman—shelling—no mall- in! Eden captured the trenches gallantly and well. Mr. Williams blew for a halt to ascertain the damage and finding there was none ordered Eden to come out of the trench, and it dawned on the attacking side that the victory was not won. Nine referees out of ten would have given a try, but whether Mr. Williams thought something shady had been done under a flag of truce, or that the scorer had knocked on, is merely sur- mise. At any rate it was hard lines for Warrington, who prepared a new plan of campaign during the in- terval which was to —(here the blue pencil of the censor deletes matter which he thinks should not be divulged.)</p>
        <p>Warrington showed up to little advantage in the second half, and for some time had, to de fend desper- ately or the citadel must have fallen. They made one desperate, frantic endeavour and were rewarded by two points, for Jones shot a goal. If he'd shot a " lion " some people would n 4 have openly shown their grief. There was no more shooting or storming of the trenches after this, and Swinton finally carried off the honours by a single point's margin.</p>
        <p>The best team won, without doubt. Warrington did not play in their best form,and were repeatedly let down by the slownes3 of the halves. Duckworth and Allen were no match for the two wily Welshmen opposed to them, and their chances were worsened by the home forwards having slightly the worst of the argument. Davies and Morgan played finely, and it was only the grand tack. Hog of the home lot which prevented them augmenting the scere. The best men of the field were the four wings, Fish and Jones on the borne side, and Lewis and Hampson on the other side. Centre play seemed at a discount, neither Smith ner Isherwool playing up to form at all. while Messer and Taylor were littlb better. Both full backs were shaky, Bosco w once or twice show- ing an unusual lack of nerve and confidence.</p>
        <p>It was hoped that Warrington would win all the three successive home matches, but they have made a bad start. Better play and more luck will be wanted to surmount the remaining hurdles.</p>
        <p>The League table.up-to-data is as follows For. Against. P. W. L: D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Runcorn 17..15 1.. 1...24 34-150.., 5 4... 22...31 St. Helens 20..14.. 4. 2..24 48-192... 7 17... 65...30 Oldham 17...14... 2... 1...22 62-230... 7 13... 53...29 Swinton 17...13... 3... 1...12 33-123...16 10... 62...27 Salford 19...10... 9... 0...22 42-170...23 26...124...20 Warrington 18.. 8. 9. 1...12 30-114.. 9 21... 81...17 Widnes 18. 7. 8. 3...12 30-108...13 29...113...17 Steepen lB.. 8. 9. 1...16 21— 93...13 27...107...17 Leigh lB.. 7. 3. 3...17 24-103...17 37...145...17 *Brought'n Rau'r'sl7... 7.. 9... 1...11 19— 79...13 18... 80-.13 .Rochdale li'netslB... 1...15 14— 72...12 34...126...13 Wigan 18. 5...13... 0... 6 18— 68-18 42...162...10 Wilma 19. 2..15.. 1...12 15— 69...21 53...201... 5 Tyldesley 20.. 2...18... 0... 7 15— 59 ..37 72...294... 4 *Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornets have each had two points deducted for breach of rules.</p>
        <p>To-day will sea another bittle royal,for the repro manta- ties of the Royal Borough of Salford visit Warrington. Like Swinton. the ReJs geJerally get their oi:stirs at Wilderspool, but the issue this par is donisti-ul. War- rington ought to win, but both clubs are playing in such in and out form that there is no relying on them at all. Still, if I wanted a new hat. I should back Warrington. Salford are yet a few points ahead in the League table, and this is the time for pulling them down.</p>
        <p>The "A "team seems to have fallen on evil days all at once. Successive defeats by Swinten have quite knocked them out of the running for the medals, which are almost certain to go Salford way. The latter club have only been beaten once, and that at Warrington, so it may be left to the imagination to conjure up the sort of battle at New Barns to-day. The Warrington lads are not frightened and mean to strain every nerve to once more prove their mettle. By the way last Saturday's defeat was due to Hallam failing to turn upsand the dis- ablement of Townend through injury. Burgess, the Flixton man, played exceedingly well, showing both speed and cleverness in 'getting away from tbe pack.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>HARRIHRS.</head>
        <p>HARRIHRS.</p>
        <p>Manchester Harriers had a pleasant outing last Satur- day, when they accepted the invitation of the Sankey Harriers to visit them and indulge in an inter- club race. Thirty members jonlaeyed to Warrington, where a four-in-hand was in Incliness to convey them to the Sankey headquarters, the Sloop Hotel, which is about two miles outside the town. Trail was laid by H briainley and J Watsoz, two members of the home club, and it covered about eight miles of exceedingly heavy country, there being a preponderance of ploughed land. The pack numbeeed 47, compoeed of 24 of Manchester, 16 of Sankey, and seven of Runcorn,and it was agreed to count the first six of each club. It was a race right through, and the first to reach home was B Bennison, of Manchester, his clubmate, Ted Barlow, cantering in behind him, well in front of T. Devlin and John Riley, of Sankey. Then came C. N. Snowden and H. Markland, of Manchester, fifth and sixth respectively, who were followed by L. Green and Joe Riley (Sankey), J. Cooper (Manchester), R Tlarostle and J. Wilding (Sankey), and G. Wood and C. Saynor (Manchester), the points being :—Manchester 35 ; Sankey 43. A. 0. Smith wont ie gold-centre medal given for the first novice home of Manchester. The members were afterwards entertained to tea, and a jolly , evening was spent together. This inter-club race is the I first in the history of the Manchester Harriers, the com- mittee hitherto not being very partial to competitive racing outside the championships. But it doubtless will net be the last, for all thoroughly enjoyed themselves, and it will be a pleasure to the Marchester Harriers to be able to show their appreciation of the generous hospitality of the Sankeyites by extending to them a re- turn invitation at the first opportunity.</p>
        <p>Sefton Hat liars were visited on Saturday by Warring- ton, when fifteen of the home team and ten Warrington H. turned out for a nine-miles race through Broad Green and Childwall. The SelZonians managed to retain their unbeaten record this season by winning by five points. Score g :—James Roberta, 1 ; G Barris, 4 ; W Longmors, 6; J Griffiths, 7; T J Roberts, 8 ; A X Holmes, 11-37. Warrington: S Parsonage, 2; W Holt, 3 ; W Mather, 5; W Price, 9; R Price, le ; W B Smiih, 13-42.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>HOCKEY. –</head>
        <p>HOCKEY. -</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Stockton Heath 2nd.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Latchflrd on Saturday to play the return match of the season. Latchford had the better of a so,ne what sorambling gam,, and cams oat victo re by three goals to two. Fr the visitors Newcombe Played a good gams in goal, whilst in the forwards Wil- kinson and Masan notched the two goals. For the bens e team Carter slored all three. Latnnford did not play up to their usual form, bat probably this may be iaccountsd hr by them having had only one match dur- ng the lac; five weeks.</p>
        <p>"SOME ENGLISH CATHEDRALS."</p>
        <p>Lecture at the Co-operative Hall.</p>
        <p>The above was the subject of a leeture given in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Tueeday evening under the aunpices of the Warrineten Literary and Philesephicil Society by Mr. Alfred Derbyshire, F.S.A., F.R.1.8.A. It was freely illustrated by lantern slides specially prepared :by Mr. Mentegu B. Copland. and ehown on the screen by Mr. H. N. Houghton. Mr. J. Rymer Young presided in the ableace of the President (the Rev Morley Stevenson). who he explained was indisposed. He expressed his regret that the lecture cleshed with the Musical SOCitttVg rehearsal, but that could not be avoided. Wttenever the society had anything particularly good to offer they had generally invited the outside public to share the plums. (Hear, hear.) TII3 lecturer then proceeded with his lecture, and at the outset offered a few observations on the arehitectnre of the world anterior to that phave which we called Gothic eccloliastic al architecture, or the architecture of the church. Paeeine over the art of the arohaic ages and commencing with that of Egypt, he said the country of the Nile was the cradle of the art of the Western world. The healers of Ezypt recognised the fact that to construct a temple it was necessary that a number of uprights placed in rows shculd e set up, and that these should be connected at the top ny horizontal stoeee or lintels. This combination eonsti- toted the initial stage in architectural art. Several ex- amples wore shown. Durhen Cathedral was afterwards given as an illustration of the Norman work which con- stituted the foundation of our English Gothic archi- tecture.' Pictures were also shown of Henry the Seventh's chapel at Westminster Abbey, Lincoln, Salis- bury, Gloucester, Canterbury, Exeter, Wells and Ely Cathedrals. Th see were fully explained by the lecturer. In conclusion, he said he thought they would all agree with him that Gothic architecture was a thing of which Englishmen might feet proud. It was a certain fact that for the true history of art, they must look to the rslie.eu.s of the world. From the earliest ages, and amongst ad nations whether Pagan or Christiae, a:thiceeture was the medium of the highest and noblest external expression of religion. Man had done inazh that was noble and beautiful in the erection, deeoration, and eleberatien of his dwelling, his places of amusement and in his civic edifices; but it was upon the temple "he God tint he had exercised his greatest ensineeriug thd constructive skid and upon which he had lavished hi; best and grandest art. There was nothing in 'he history of art Co beautiful,so truthful and so majestic, as a Gothic cathedral. Even if they did not uniercael the principles upon which a cathedral was realised, they felt a sense of awe and veneration as they passed it; sacred aisles. It was full of a mysterious atmosphere through which the slender shaft,g penetrated till- they ...ere almoet lost in the net work of the vaulted roof, and their theuehte were carried away into centemplation of the Inflate. The carved angels looked d - and they pictured the heavenly host ; the fe'ree over' eel eceee peiating, heavenward and one left the sacred peeeie,..ts yr,th a Icoo gledee that the place was holy. Let it always he borne in mind that the Gott.'re are,:itecture, of our Cathedrals had an individua- lity distinct from the Gothic of other countries. It had maintairaod this individuality from the moment it took root in the soil. It was pure in thought and refined in coaceptiee, it was truthful, and beautiful exceedingly. Truly the old maditeval builders had left a legacy rich in honesty, truth and beauty, for, " In the elder days of Art. Builders wrought with greatest care, Every minute and unseen part, For the Gods see everywhere,"</p>
        <p>The lecturer was cordially th tok.d tor his services on the prop,sition of Dr. White, roc )nded by Mr. May, and the moce3dings terminate..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Annual United Communion Service.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Annual United Communion Service.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the annual united Communion asevice in connection with the Evangelical Free Churches of Warrington was hold in the Bold-street W esleyan Chapel. There was a fair attendance of the members of the various Evangelical Churches, including the Revs. c. Fiech, J. F. Bdatthews, E. T. Reed, J. T. Schofield, and J. Warnock. The Rev. Theodore Bishop presided, and a short address was delivered by the Rev. John Yonge, who took for his text the words, "And they sung a new song, saying : Thou art worthy to take the book and to open the seals thereof ; for Thou wast slain, and least redeemed us to G)1 by Thy blood out of every kindred, and tongue and people, and flatlets • and haat made us unto our God kiugs and priests, and we shall reign on the earth." (Revelation;v. 9-10.) At the con- clusion of his remarks he said that two years ago when ke addressed them he appealed to ;hem—to the brethren around the Holy Communion table—for a common crusade in which all the Churches should unite—a common crusade against the ungodliness, the neglect of the Sabbath, and the horrible intemperance of Warring- ten. He was lenginfe for the tirne when all the ministers weld be more closy.united in order that they might rescue all those is sin in Warrington, and lead them into the love and peace of their heavenly Father.</p>
