      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>icHtiE.L CONIQPIT.</head>
        <p>icHtiE.L CONIQPIT.</p>
        <p>(From a photograph.)</p>
        <p>The endorsements of Scott's Emulsion come from persons of all walks of life. The medical profE-sion praises it highly and uses it moot extensively, and persons from all the rounds of the socisl ladder unite in testifying to the splendid properties of this v.lrt- able remedial agent. You cannot doubt the wide range of usefulness of cod-liver oil, and, whereas for about a century this oil was prescribed in its raw state, in spite of its taste and the difficulty of digesting it, 6 leaco has now found the way to get the ;most good cut of this oil without having to meet the ohatlebs of taste and tax upon digestion. Scott's Emulsion comprises not only cod-liver oil, but also hypophosphites of lime and soda, and glycerine. The byr.opheephitl aid digestion, feed the nerves, sustain the brain, and assist in the healthy formation of bones of children, while the glycerine prevents fermentation and is of important use in facilitating the ab;orption of the oil. The combination, therefore, of these ctandard remedial agents which make up Scott's Emuls'on, is an exceptionally valuable one. _ _</p>
        <p>For throat and lung afivetions Scott's Emulsion is not only a local specific. but also of grt,lt use in building up the system) so that it can resist further attacks. Here is proof of this fact :- " 17, Back St. Helena-road, Bolton, Lancs.. "April 181 a, 1109.</p>
