      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Handkerchiefs and Periodicals Wanted.</head>
        <p>Handkerchiefs and Periodicals Wanted.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me through the medium of your paper to appeal to the inhabitants of Warrington and the district tor further help for our soldiers Handkerchiefs are much needed (old ones for tearing up and others for general use). If the inhabitants of this district would each contribute one of each kind we should have a goodly number to seed away and I should be very glad to collect them Our vicar, Rev. E. R. Tarbuck and Mr. Parker have kindly permitted me to use th's school as a collecting place where I should be pleased to receive parcels or, en the receipt of a pot t card to call for any. An officer, in acknowledging the receipt of hooks and periodicals, said we could not realise the boon such were to our men on the transports. As lam collecting such from my friends I should be grateful for any from the public,. Yours truly,</p>
        <p>Heathside Boys' School, January 12ch, 1900.</p>
        <p>(MRS.) I. GRACEY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>Successful Year.—An Increase of Business All Round.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the trustees and managers of Runcorn Savings Bank was held on Wednesday at the bank prem'ses in High-street. Mr E liaison, C.C., pre- sided, and there were present Messrs. James Handley, J.P., J M Frith, D H Jones, W Hankinson, J Lea, and W Handley (actuary.) The Actuary read the annual report as under:— The Bank year which ended on the 20:h of November last added ancther one to its rucceasful and continued pregress. The number of open a:ceunts, the number of trassactions; and the total volume of its business, exhibit an advanee over any previous year, and as a consequence the benefit to depositors in the amount of interest and dividends paid and credited to them during the year has, been the largest in the history of the Bank, amsuntirg to the satisfactory sum of £1,049 17s. The auditod abstract statement of the accounts of the Bank eet out these facts in con- eiderable detail, cepies of which may be had by depos-- tore on application, but a few of the items therein con- tained may be appropriatly referred Is. On November 20th, 1898, the total amount of deposit was £41,264 is 6d ;on November 2Gbn, 1399, the tote' amount of deposits was £43,084 Os 8,1; an inc,e.ase of £1,819 19s 2d. On Nevember 20th, 1898, the nurnher of open aeoounts was 1,195 ; on November 20th, 1899, the number of open accounts was 1,213 an increase of 18. In the previous year the total interest and div•dends paid and credited to depositors amounted to 4996 Os 2d ; in the year just closed to £1,049 17s; an increase of £53 161 103. In the year ending November 20th, 1898, the number of transac ions was 3,752 iii the year ending November 20th, 1899, the number of treheactions 3,950 ;an increase ot 200. These embraced 3 total turn- over during the year of £25,689 10s id ; as agaiest £24,937 is lid in the previous ; an increase of £762 Be 2d. A satisfactory feat ore i 4 the comparatively large number of Friendly and Charitable Societies hav- ing accounts with the Bank numbering 104, and the I amount invested by them is £10,962 18s 11d, showing the provident character of the inhabitants ot Runcorn and its immediate district. Against the £43,084 Os 8d owing to depositors, there was invested with the National Debt Commissioners on 20th November last, as shown by the certificate £42,694 03 id ; and the treasurer held for curie tit requirements, as certified by him, £5lO is Bd, leavieg a clear surplus over all liabilities of £l2O ls id. The auditors have attended silliest continuously during the year and by their report certify to the correctness of the accounts+. The inspector appointed under the Saving Bank Acts also paid his usual visit which extended over two days, one of which included the hours during whieh business was conducted, and subjected the books and system of busi- ness to a thorough examination. Daring the year the Bank has lost by death, two of its trustees I and one of its Managers : Mr W.llia.m Whittaker, whose active coonection with the Bank dated frsra its early years ; Mr Philip Speakman whore unexe.cted death re- nneved a personality actively identified with so many forms of public usefulnese,and Mr Joseph Pritchard who up to a few days before his decease took an active and onerous share as one of the Bank Managers. The re- moval to larger and more convenient premises has fully justified itself,and has no doubt, ecceuraged an increase of business, and it is to be hoped that with the return of a measure of prssperity to the district, the rate of progress will be maintained, and the Batik long continue its useful work as one of che permaneat institutions of Runcorn. (Hear, hoar and applause.) file Chairman moved the adoption of the report and commented upon its admireb'e and camp!, to character. The growth in the bank's businsts was highly satisfac- tory to all concerned. A feature cf the bank calculated to give c )nfidence to the public was that beyond the local audit :here was also au annual inspection by a. Government official, whose visit extended over two days, one of which days was spent in the bank during the time the customers were coming in and out. The appeared to him (the chairman) to be a very great advantage as it was perfectly evident that the inspector had the opportunity of comparing any customer's book with the books of the bank and of ftivi- lug out any deficiency if a defie:ency existed. The Chairman endorsed the observations of the actuary in respect to the loss sustained by the bank through the deaths of !Abe three gentlemen named. He moved the adoption of the report, and the re-appointment of the auditors at the same remuneration as previceely. Mr. J. Handley seconded the motion, and commented upon the usefulness of the institution. The increase in the amount due to depositors showed the thrifty nature of the people and that the people appreciated the facilities afforded by the bank for investing and with- drawing money. Some years age £30,000 was the goal at weicte they aimed, now that ambunt had been ex- ceeded. They all hoped the bank would still further prosper and progress. The report was adopted. The Chairman then moved the appoint.nent of the following additional trustees and managers :—Trustees : Messrs Walter Timmins, J.P., Francis Wiswall, J.P., A R Norman, J.P., A Salted, Thomas Wright and J Burton. Managers : Tnomas Jones, Leinster Gardens; John Pritchard, Higher Runcorn; J H Weston, Victoria- road ; Edward Gregory, Bridge-street; Francis Beck, Greenway-road, and Horace W Handley, Greenway- road.</p>
        <p>A ••• . ••••• Mr. W. Hankinson seconded the motion which was carried. . .</p>
        <p>Tns Chairman remarked that Mr. Handley' s pre- dec.essor as actuary, Mr. W. F. Salkeld had a salary of £7O per annum, and Mr. Handley took the appointment at a reduced rate in accordance with a good and safe rule. {Since Mr. Handley'qappointment the amount due te depositor' had increased from £23,000 to £43,000, which was the high water mark of the Bank's capital for investment. There wao £3O a year more available for expensei than when Mr. Handley was appointed and he (the Chairman) was rerfictly satisfied that the Bank could safely increase the actuary's salary by £lO and bring it up to the £7O, which was paid to li;s predecessor. There wise no advantage in the Bank hoarding up a balance. Each depositor received the lull amount to which he WAS entitled, and ample eta3rity as afforded by the manner in which the money was invested. He moved that the actuary's salary be increased from £6O to £7O. Mr. J. M. Frith seconded the moticn, and remarked that the amount of work entailed by carrying on the bank was worthy of more remuneration, and the audi- tor's report showed that the work was thoroughly and efficiently carried out. If all auditors were bound to present such a report there would be no Millwall Dock scandals. (Ileir, hear). Mr. Hankinson supported the motien. The Chairman said not Ling could be snore perfect than the system of the beak, and na busisess could be done in a more ~..,ffijent manner. The motion was carricd unanimously. Mr. Handley in reply said he was very pleased, not because of the advence but teceuse of the expressions that had been used. The work was a delight to him. He had never viewed:the work from a monetary stand- point fer the werk was more than could be compensated for by any payment which the funds of the back could afford ; and he was dergteed to bear the expressions of c-lifidence from the trustee and managers. A vote of thanks to the Chairman on the prop-sitien of Mr. T. Hanal.eY, seconded by Mr. D. H. Jones, con- cluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>The Theft of a_Watch at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At-th-e- Liverpool County Quarter Se-azdons on Tuezday, James Towey, 34, labourer, was charged with having stolen a watch, chain, and medal valued at 255, belonging to Joseph Charles "Eden, bedstead manufacturer,6,Church-street,Warrington. The articles were stolen frnm Mr. iliden's house during the temporary absence of its occupants on the afternoon of the 3rd inst, and later on the same day the prisoner gave the watch and chain to a woman named Maguire and induced her to pawn them. The prisoner, whose previous conviction dated back to 1883, was sentenced to 12 months' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL PROFESSION are extensively recom- mending the moderate use of Port Wine in all cases of debility. especially after Influenza. INVALID PORT of exceptional quality, thoroughly matured and guaranteed by W. &amp; A. GILBEY, can be obtained of their 3,000 Agents at Zs. ed. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>vsL2"--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The wPplrly meeting of the Earlestown and Dis- trict P.S.A. was held at the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown on Sunday, Mr. Brown presiding. The prinlipal speaker was Mr. Potter, and recitations were given by Mr. W Morris. Vocal items were submitted by Mr J Webster and Mr. S Dring, ac- companied. POLICE CASES. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning, before Mr. J. W. Emmett, W. Broxon, a collier, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night, and was fined ss. and costs.—At the Town Hall, Newton, on Tuesday morning, before Mr. T. J. Gills Tie, Mary Riley was charged with being drunk, and in default of paying a fine of lOs and costs was sent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>VOLUNTEER BALL AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER BALL AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The 31.4 t -annual ball in connection with the New- ton detachment of the 6th V.U.K.L.R., was held in the Town Hall, Newton, on Friday evening. The hall was very prettily decorated by Mr. 61. T.Evans, and about 80 persons were present. The music was supplied by Mt. W. Pearson' band of Wigan, and dancing which commenced at 9 o'clock was kept up with spirit until four o'clock next morning The M.C.'s were Corporal Hill mad Bugler O'Neill. The proceeds were in aid of the local war fund. The catering was efficiently performed by Mr.Hib- bert and Miss Baker.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. A m-eting of the above association was held on Monday in the Co-operative Hall, when there was a large attendance of members. Mrs Rawlins pre- sided and after a few opening remarks,Miss Greenell read a paper on " The sweating system," in which she touched upon the various trades and the poor wages earned by the workpeople in those trades under the sweating system. At the close of the paper, Mrs Rawlins briefly referred to the war, but said that was not a time for criticism At the close of the meeting a vote of thanks was passed to Mies Greenall on the motion of Miss Watson seconded by Mrs Presco?. Ts Vir4S afterwards served, and a very pleasant and profitable gathering was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GOD TEMPLART</head>
        <p>GOD TEMPLART</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown lodge held their meeting in the Co--,perative Hall,Wargrave-road on Nionday evening. Bro Cutler C T presiding. After the open- ing servicoi four friends were proposed for initia- tion, one was received on clearance card and one was restorPfl to membership The report of the New Tears' sonial qivAn by Bro Cutler showed a sub- stantial bslanc.4 in hand and the report was adopted with thanks to tl-e committee. The C T wished two members. -vho had volunteered, and been chosen for active serrice in South Africa, a pleasant voyage and a saf-, re.orn. Selections were given on tile mouth org- n by Brothers Nunnerlev,Ashcroft,Ellis. and P Onvie?. ( f-f,ivilock): rending "Only a Bro Ellis; sag.' Queen of the earth." Br 4 T Macklin; selection on the bones by Bro Phillips accompanied by Bro Wilson on the melodeon "Napoleon's Grand March," which was encored ; melodeon solo, " Blue bells of Scotland," Bro Wilson. Selections by the band were encored, thanks being accorded to them. Lodge closed at 9-30.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF AN EARLESTOWN RESERVIST. The sad intelligence reached Earlestown on Mon- day of the death in Gosport Hospitel of Private W. L. Lawrence, Royal West Surrey Regiment. De ceased was in apparent good health up to Fri4ay last, but on that day bis relatives in Earlestown re- ceived a telegram informing them that he had been uddenly taken setiouslv ill. The next telegram conveyed the sad tv)ws of his death. At present the relatives are at a loss to account for the real cause as also were themedical attendants who inquired of the family if they w )uld desire a post-mortem ex- amination of the body, to which they negatively replied. Lawrence-was thirt,7-sevea years of age, upon the 2nd Class Reserve list, and wonld have completed his service as a Reservist in March next. He was employed as a postman in the town and was much respected by his co•w3rkers, who, OR his leaving for the regimental depot on December 23rd ma4e him a suitable present. The mother of Law- rence had a strange foreboding that he would never live to reach the front. FALL FROM A TRAIN NEAR NEWTON.—MIRA.CITLOUS ESCAPE OF A CHILD.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning shortly before 10 o'clock, as the 6.30 train from Wigan to Warrington was travelling between Golborne and Lowton, a child named George Warden. 4 years of age, fell from one of the carriages on to the six foot, and had a most marvellous escape. The child was travelling with its parents, who live at 22, Sharp-street, Wigan, to Earlestown on a visit to some friends, and after leaving Golborne was standing against the door of the compartment, and when rounding the Lowton curve, the door suddenly flew open and the young- ster fell out on to the line. The passengers passed the alarm along the train until the driver was in- formed and he immediately stopped the train. The child's father got out, and going back found the lad lying on the ballast. He picked him up and ran with him to the train and went on with him to Newton where Dr. Watkins was seen, and he on ex- amining the child found it was cut about the face and head, one arm and both knees being badly scarred and bruised. Dr. Watkins dressed the wounds and the parents proceeded in a cab to Earlestown. Dr. Watkins considered the child had a miraculous escape.</p>
        <p>PATRIOTIC TEA AND CONCERT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>_ On Saturday a tea party promoted by Mr. T. Melyneux took place in the Viaduct Institute, Barlestown, the proceeds of which were devoted to the local war fund. There were two sittings, and something like 750 people partook of tea. An ad- journment was afterwards made to the Town Hall where a concert was given. The hall was packed to its utmost capacity and many who were present at tea were unable to gain admittance. The programme which was wholly sustained by local artists, was pleasantly varied, and was excellently rendered b3t Messrs 61 Kidd, W Lintond and Robinson, Misses Molyneux and Sirett, whilst Mr J Isherwood gave selections on the fairy bells and musical glasses. One or two pieces were well played by the orches- tral bawl, and at the recital of "The Absent- minded Beggar" by Mr. Robinson, a collection was taken up on behalf of the war fund. The concert throughout was thoroughly patriotic, the audience joining enthusiastically in the National Anthem, and "Soldiers of the Queen," As a result of the affair, Air, Molyneux has been able to hand over about £2O to the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>NEWTON COUNCIL'S APPRAL DISMISSED.</head>
        <p>NEWTON COUNCIL'S APPRAL DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>Before Justices Bigham and Phillimore, sitting as a Divisional Court in the Queen's Bench Division, on Monday, the appeal case of the Newton-in- Makerfield District Council v. Lyon was heard. Mr. Horridge, who appeared for the appellants, said that the respondent, Mr.lLyen, was a mineral water manufacturer, and that an action was brought against him by the District Council for exposing goods for sale in a cart within the town contrary to the local Act of Parliament, and no conviction was made. The fact was that his son, who was in charge of the cart, called upon a customer, a Mrs. Kirkman, without having received a previous order from her, and she took a dozen and a half bottles. This the local authority contended was an exposure of goods for sale within the meaning of the local Act, and the County Court judge decided against the view; hence the preen 1 appeal.—Mr. Day, for the respon- dent, submitted that the County Court Judge had taken the proper view, and that there was no expesure for sale.—Their Lordships held that the circumstances of the case did not bring it within the meaning of the local :Act, and dismissed the appeal with costs.</p>
        <p>Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Company.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the shareholders of the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Company, Limited, was held on Wednesday at the head office of the bank in spring Gardens, Mr. Abel Buckley, chair- man of the bank, presiding. The report of the directors, recommending a dividend for the half year of 15; a share and a bonus of 5; a share, both free from income-tax was adopted. An extraordin- ary meeting was afterwards held, when the pro- posal of the directors to issue 25,0C3 new shares.and thus increase the subscribed capital to £7,500,000, the paid-up capital to £1,250,000, and the reserve fund to £1,900,000, was unanimously adopted. A copy of the balance sheet, which, as will be seen is a most satisfactory one,will be found on our front page.</p>
        <p>1 To MOTELERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their Jhildren while teething, with perfect suceess. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes " as bright as a button."Of all Chemists is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900 THE WAR.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 19, 1900 THE WAR.</p>
        <p>strict and the change a very great one,but he knew they would prove themselves an honour to the Battalion. They would be able to show what the Warrington 1 Volunteers were made of. (Applause.) Before they left the Barracks he hoped to see them very often and per- haps give them one or two drills. In handing over the command to Captain Appletor he was sure he had done the right thing. (Applause.) He had done a great deal for the Battalion, and he felt sure they would treat him with the proper respect due to an officer. He would be ' always ready to help them in every possible way, and they need have no fear of appealing to him. He hoped the other officers would be as good fellows as he was. (Applause.) the MAYOR said the object of their meeting that day i had caused him a good deal of anxious thought and he eonf E sged it was quite impossible to give that adequate expression to his teelings that he should like to do, but he felt bound to say that they had in volunteering for active service net only reflected great honour and credit upon themselves, but they had conferred additional honour upon the town to which they belonged, and he felt he was only expressing, with sincerity, the senti- ment of the whole of the people of the town when he said ' that they felt proud of this act of theirs, and grateful to them for the part they were taking in the present coma- Con of the country's affairs. He was not going to attlmpt to enlarge upon the difficulties in South Africa. They were great enough, but he had sufficient faith in the men at the head of affairs there, and the men whom they were leading, to know that no matter how great the diffi- culties, they were net too great to be overcome. (Applause,) He was proud to think that the man who had recently gone out, supported as he was by perhaps one of the ablest administrators in the British army—Lord Roberts, who was the hero of a hun- dred fights and more, notwithstanding the gigantic nature of the task, had sttch experience and so brilliant a history that they all had abeohate confidence in him and the. men serving under him. (Applause.) He thought while they were applauding, and very properly applaud lug the work done by the members of the regular army, when their civilian volunteers offered themselves for active service it would be difficult to say how heartily and how thoroughly they admired their conduct. and how greatly they appreciated it. (Applause.) While they had been taking a keen interest in affairs in South Africa before, their interest now that their own towne- men were going out to the front would be keener than ever. They would watch with very anxious interest all the news which came from the front, knowing that their own men would do their duty, and trusting that they would comts back safely, when the war was over. (Applause.) For his own part he did not think that the war would now last very long,and it might happen that they might be disappointed when they got there, as the possibilities of active service might be denied to them by the results obtained before they arrive there, but be that- as it might, he was sure that for their Queen and country and their old town, they would do everything that was required of them,and come back, he hoped, alive and well, having done that which every Englishman ought to do in the hour of trial—his duty. (Loud applause). The men, having each in turn signed the attestation paper and received the Queen's shilling, had the oath administered to them by the Mayor in the following terms :—" 1,--, do make oath that I will be faithful and bear true allegiance to her Majesty, her heirs and successors, arid that I will,' as in duty bound, honestly and faithfully defend her Majesty, her heirs, and suc- cessors in person, crown and dignity,against all enemies and will observe and obey all orders of her Majesty, her heirs and successors, and of the genera!s and officers set over me—so help me God." The ceremony over, the band played the National Anthem, and hearty cheers were raised for the Mayor, Colonel Ridgway, Major Roome and Captain Appleton. The men, preceded by the Volunteer and Bugle Band, the former playing a lively air, mare:led out of the Drill Hall. A halt was made in front of the Town Hall, where a photograph was taken by Mr. Birtles. From there the men. marched by wa7 of Sankey-street, Horsemarket-street, and Orford-lane, to the Barracks. Large numbers of spec- tators congregated at different points of the route and cheered the men vigorously. At the Barracks Colonel Spratt inspected them and selected 50 men from the ranks for active service. In addition to those, there is the captain, sergeant-instruc- tor, two sergeants, two corporals, a bugler, and a stretcher-bearer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</head>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>BRITISH FORCES ACROSS THE TUGELA.</p>
        <p>Attack by Sir O. Warren on the Enemy's Left' Flank.</p>
        <p>SPEARMAN'S FARM, WEDNESDAY, 9.10 P.M. On Thursday last Lord Dundooald, with the mounted brigade, pushed for ward, and by a sudden movemen seized Springfield bridge. Hurrying on from there he took up a very strong position on Zwart's Kop, a hill commanding Potgieter's Drift. He found the Boers totally unprepared for a British advance. Some of them were bathing in the river. General Lyttleton's Brigade was sent to hold the position on Z wart's Kop, leaving a strong body to hold Colento, and General Hit. yard's Brigade at Springfield. Our whole forco advanced without delay. The ferry pont at Potgieter's was on the further bank of the Tugela, and in order to bring it to our side Lieutenant Carlyle and five men of the South African Light Horse swain across the river and brought it back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Four Days' Halt.</head>
        <p>A Four Days' Halt.</p>
        <p>After four days halt' on the south side of the Tugela our advance northward began on the 15th. Lvttelten's Brigado crossed the drift in the evening, and held the kepjes On our right. Sir Charles Warren's division has ma de an attack upon the enemy's left flank. Tl' e remainder of the column is now crossing the river. The news has been officially confirmed by General Buller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WITH FRENCH'S COLUMN, Australian Patrol Surprised. Two Killed and others taken Prisoners.</head>
        <p>WITH FRENCH'S COLUMN, Australian Patrol Surprised. Two Killed and others taken Prisoners.</p>
        <p>RENSBERG, WEDNESDAY. 4.30 pm.</p>
        <p>A. patrol of the New Beath Wales Lancers, consisting of ninetcen men, had an unpleasant experience yester- day. They had been scouting in the neighbourhood of Norval's Farm, and were returning to camp when a party of sixty 80-rs sought to cut them off. The Lancers immediately made for an adjacent kopje, intending to hold it against the enemy until they were relieved. It WI3 a hard race between thena and the mounted Boers. When they reached the kopje how- ever, they found that another party of Boers were in posseesion of it, and the unforturi ate Australians were thus between two fires. The enemy proceeded to shoot the Lancers' horses,though thrc 3 men managed to break through, and having good mounts reached camp safely. Of the remaining en, two were killed, and fourteen taken prieoners. LAIRR.</p>
        <p>Six of the Australians who were supposed to have be,m taken prisoners near Rensberg on Tuesday have returned to camp.</p>
        <p>General Hector Macdonald.</p>
        <p>General Hector Macdonald has arrived at the Cape from India.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Volunteers for the Front</p>
        <p>The final selection of members of the Runcorn Detachment of the 2nd Earl Cheshire volunteer Battalion has resulted as under :—Corporal F J Jeacock, (to rank as sergeant), Private A C Timmins, Private L Mack, Lance-Corporal E J Speakman, Private F Platt anti Private D Chadwick. The Frodsham companies of the same regiment also contribute the following:—Private F Evans, Private T Carter, and Private E Darlington. All th,. men are first class shots, and in the pink of condition. They will most likely leave for headquarters (Chester), on Saturday, but the date had' not yet been fixed. The battalion of the line reinforcements of which the volunteers will form part are, however, to sail for the Cape in thre e weeks.</p>
        <p>Peace and War.—PresentatiOn to a Volunteer. On Sunday the members of the St. Paul's Bible Clasg, Widnes, presented Mr. J. Yates, one of the members, who is about to leave for the front, with a pocket Bible hymn-book and prayer-book, as a mark of their esteem and good wishes for a safe sojourn in South Africa and a return to this country. The Rev. J. Jones, M.A., made the presentation, and said that through him the class wished Mr. Yates God-speed. While he was out in South Africa he might remember that the last lesson in this country had been given him from that Bible. They all beperl and trusted that he would have a safe return. The following inscription was put on the fly-leaf..— " Presented to Mr. John Yates, by the members of the St. Paul's Class, on the occasion of his leaving for South Africa to fieht for his Queen and country. Quit ye like men," Fight the good fight."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Warrington Volunteers Enter the Barracks.</head>
        <p>Warrington Volunteers Enter the Barracks.</p>
        <p>The Warrington volunteers who are to form half a Company for service in South Africa bade adieu to civilian life for at least some months when on Saturday they took the oath of allegiance and became "Soldiers of the Queen." The men paraded at the Drill Hall about isalf-past nine, and an interested number of spectators quickly assembled. The ceremony of swaaring in was performed under the presidency of the Mayor, who wore his robes and chain of office, and added dignity to a solemn and impressive ceremony. Never before in the history of the town has such an unique event taken place. It was a sight well calculated to make tine onlooker proud of the patriotism which had prompted it and to arouse enthusiasm to see the "thin red line" of local men responding to the call of duty and offering themselves for service in their country's cause. Most of thnn, it not all, were leaving comfortable homes, and with little prospect of any substantial reward, were offering themselves for a hazardous life on the South African veldt.</p>
        <p>Amongst tnose on parade were :—Lieut,nant Colonel Ridgway, Major Reome (Adjutant), Major Buckton, Captain Appleton (who goes out with the men), Captain Ede!sten, Captain J D Fair clough and Lieutenant Smith, Crosfiold, Medley and Willocks ; Sergeant Major Raw- linson and the foslowing volunteers for service :—Ser- geant Instructor Killen (pay sergeant), Colour-Sergeant Wilcock,Svrgeant Rooke (reaerve),two corporals,a bugler and a stretcher heirer,and 60 privates. Having answer-, the roll call,the men were put through a few movements ley the Adjutant,and were carefully inspected by Colonel Ridgway, No possible fault could be found with the 'r appearance, and it was pretty generally conceded that they presented a.very smart and soldierly appearance. By the time the inspection was completed a large crowd had assemble and watched the proceedings with eensiderable interest and not a litle display of enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>After the men had been inspected,the Mayor agcended the platform to perform the ceremony of swearin7-in. He was accompanied by Coloßel and Mrs Ridgway, Mrs Buckton, and Mrs E ielsten. Among others present were Captain Ridgway (transferred to South Staffordshire), Captain Chorley (retired), Councillors Smethurst and 136unett, Mr J Lyon Wnittle (Town clerk), Dr Gornall. Mr 8 Wiis 4n, and Mr Luke Talbot (chief constable).</p>
        <p>CAOGQI RIDGWAY said he was pleased to see th's) men there arid to compliment them upcn their smart appear- ance. us must say that they did very great credit to Warrington and to the!Battalion,and he was sure that if the men who were to join them in the following week ware as smaet a body as they were the Volunteer authorities of the country would have no reason to be ashamed. (Heart hear.) They would, he felt certain,be able to do good service for their country. He had asked the Mayor, as chief magistrate of the town, to attest them, and he thought it was a very right thing to do. Ho took a very great interest in the Battalion,and he wee very proud that his townsmen had at some sacrifice so willotgly given their services. (Appleuse.) There had been seme talk in the tJwn of the tremendous risks they would run, and it would be well to remind them the they were only 100 men out of 120,000 and it would moreover be some time before they got to the fretit. They would have noticed in the papers that the Line Battali .tn had only got as far as E4tcourt. Though he knew they were only too willing and anxious to get to the front, they would not arrive there before some con- siderable time had elapsed and in the mean- time would have to show themselves efficient in every respect. As regarded the climate in South Africa, he could tell them that it was the finest in ,he world. The Volunteers would be well looked after, and when the war was over there would be an abund- ance of good openings for men who wished to remain there. They would join for a year, and if they wished to remain in South Africa they could obtain their dis- missal there. He did not give them this information before they offered themselves for active service because he wished them to give their services spontaneously for their country. He, however, thought it opportune now to let them know of the advantages which might eccrue from their action. They were to be attested as Volun- teers to the Line Battalion. The discipline would be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FINAL SELECTION OF MEN</head>
        <p>FINAL SELECTION OF MEN</p>
        <p>The final list of volunteers selected for active service is as follows :</p>
        <p>Captain F M Appleton 4465 Pte W H Wadsworth Sergt.-Ins. KJLen 4337 Pte T Abbott 4647 Pte W Blackwood 4116 Pte T Massey 4613 Pte IL Heaton 4542 Pte R Barker 4572 Pte F E Jones 4544 Pte W Heesom 4329 Pte J Loundes 4768 Pte W J Cole 4626 Pte G Heath 4855 Pte C Hassan</p>
        <p>3533 Pte 1 Fearnley 4493 Corporal J Hunt 4651 Pte J Atkinson 4750 Lanc-Corpl. J Robinßon 4E38 Pte R Armstrong 4' ( 3 Pte W Ashton</p>
        <p>4703 Pte T Broeklehiirst 4701 Pe T S Ds.vies 4452 Pte T G Sudlow 4737 Pte J Parker 4194 Pte M Nixdorf 4671 Pte A Hori an 4861 Pte F Hughes 4409 Pte A Dean 4761 Pte T Corless 4479 Pte 3 Sideock</p>
        <p>3209 Col.-Ser, T W Moore 4569 Pte T Shalleress 4347 L-Corp P Rogerson 4512 Pte T Moore 4070 L-Corp 3 Allen 4365 Pte A Houghton</p>
        <p>3693 Pte F-W T,ylor</p>
        <p>4493 Pte B Bevan</p>
        <p>4584 iii -T 4782 Pte C Booth 4680 Pte It Irons 4629 Pte W Spencer 4742 Pt s W Taylor 4335 Pte H Warburton 4692 Pte H Dale 3876 Pte W Warburton</p>
        <p>3833 PI e T Massey 4712 Pte W Griffiths 4641 Pte CTairbrother 4667 Pe W Lea</p>
        <p>4316 Pte T A Inca 4474 Lanc-Corpl. T Hodges 3030 Cr Sgt J Willcocks 4739 Pie B Salt 3398 Corpl 3 Howard 4640 Pta T Bibby</p>
        <p>3494 Corp-1 3 J Eiouglaton 396 Pte J H Woodward 4612 Pte G Singleton</p>
        <p>MORE MILITIA TO !BE EMBODIED AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Notices have been posted on the church doors to the effect that five hundred non-cemmissioned officers and -nen of the 3rd Battalion let Kings (Liverpool) Regiment (late 2nd Royal Lancashire Militia) will be embodied at Orford Barrack, Warrington, at 9.30 on Tuesday next, and pre,c-,ed the same afternoon by special train to Preston. Only non-eommisa;oned officers and men who receive notice papers will attend, the whole of the Bat- talion not being required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>What the Yeomanry are Going Through.</head>
        <p>What the Yeomanry are Going Through.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. 0. Stubbs, late manager of th 2 English timber mill at Messrs R. A. Naylor and Company's and a well- known member of the Liberal Club, writes to a friend from Lichfield, where the Staffordshire Yeomanry, of which he is a member, are quartered. He says" They are giving it us pretty stiff now. We have to get up early and do our own horses and tackle, and then parade mounted by 9 a.m. Then we have to ao up to a very rough heath for drill, practise advancing and finding our own cover both mounted and dismounted. They have sent 13 home for bad riding. Some of the horses we have got here are brutes. Mine is only fair. F. kicks like h—hut I am master of it now. We have had no shooting yet as the range is under water.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Corporal at Ladysmith.</p>
        <p>We have been permitted to pub'ish the following ex- tract from a letter written by Corporal W. Heath, Ist Kings Liverpool Regiment. Corporal Heath resided at 18, Watkin-street, Warrington,and he write i on °etcher 28th, stating that he is in good health. He adds, ''we have had three battles alrea iy and are on the trot night and day. In the first engam,ement General Penn Symons, 15 officers and about 150 men rot killed. After the battle we went out to bury the dead and they (the Boers) shelled us agAin which is a thing not known yet with civilization —and the Boers are supposed to be civilised They are worse than a lot of savages for they even fired on a Red Cross and the flag of truce." After alai-ling to th second battle in which about 15 officers and men were killed, Corporal Heath says" We have captured about 500 of the Boors aad about 6 big guns. They are the biggest cowards I ever saw in my hfe, and as soon as they see a bayonet they will go on tEeir kneei. They can stand bullets for a while but when it comes to a bayonet its a washout with them."</p>
        <p>" Pay, pay, pay," and said he was sure they would all join Wail him in wie,hing od-speed to every soldier of the Queen. Mr. Seddon, the hon. secretary of the clot), with whom the idoa of the c mcert ori,.;iaeted, is to be ceingrltalated on having provided a ,most euccesaful evening's entertainment. An entertainment in connection with the Buckley- street Band of Hope was given in the schoolrootn on Monday evening when a large number of members and friends were present. Mr C Nurse occupied the chair, and gave a short address. Recitations were given by Miss L Fairhurst, Miss McMutrie, and Master W Cress. Mise EScott also recited a patriotic recitation, entitled "Fighting in the Line," composed by a Warrington gentleman. A violin solo by Mr Butcher was well IT- ceived, as were also solos by Mr W Richardson and Mr J H Cooke. The moat important item to the children was a singing competition, in which there ware nine entries. Prizes were offered by Mr J Palmer and Mr C Nurse, and were secured by the following : —l, Master Adams ;2, Miss L Fairhurst ; 3, Miss Fogg ; and 4, Miss Cross. A pianoforte solo was also given by Mr A Tr3w, who also accompanied. A collection was after- wards made in aid of the Mayor's Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>HELP FOR THOSE LEFT BEHIND.</head>
        <p>HELP FOR THOSE LEFT BEHIND.</p>
        <p>Entertainments at Warrington.</p>
        <p>For t''.e benefit of the Mayor's Transvaal Campaign Relief Fund, a concert was held at the Cheshire Cheese Inn, Latchford, on Friday evening under the auspices of the Latchford C7ciing Club. There was 9. good at- tendance, Mr. J. W. Mills presiding in the absence of the Presthnt, Dr. Leadbetter. The concert commenced with a pianoforte solo by, Mr A Hale "Battle March of Delhi." He subsequently sane" The Soldiers of the Qaeen " and "The British Lion," which were much appreciated. Mr R G-ounds sang " Let M 9 lik. a soldier tall" and " The Kerry Dance." Miss Mills (mandoline) made her (tibia in.a trio, " The Death of Nelson," the other performers being Mr R Grounds (cornet) and Mr Hale (piano). They were encored,and gave "Killarney." Mr Alfred Grounds, the treasurer of the club, who naturally met with a most enthusiastic reception, contributed "Soldiers in the Park," from "The Runaway Girl," with cornet obligate, a series of gramaphone selection., and the latest patriotic song, "Take thic muzzle off the lion." Mr Fred Scattergood sang "The Blind Bey," and for an encore " Hearts and Homes." In the second part he contributed " Robin Adair." Mr G H Smith recited " The Absent-minded Beggar" in his usual spirited manner, and the tambourine was taken round by Mire Mills, with the result that £4 2s 31 was tdded to the Mayor's Fund. Miss Mills contribution to the second nart of the entertainment was a mandoline solo, "The Rialto," and for an encore she gave" Alice where art thou ?" Mr. R Grounds contributing the vocal part. Mr. Beale gave his imitation of " The Charge of the Light Brigade," and the concert was concluded with " Rule Britannia " and the National Anthem. In reply- ing to the toast of his health, Mr. Mills thanked the cyclists for their liberal respouse to the invitation to</p>
        <p>asq;-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>VIADUCT WORKS OFFICE STAFF</head>
        <p>VIADUCT WORKS OFFICE STAFF</p>
        <p>DINNER.</p>
        <p>The tenth annual dinner in connection with the office staff of the Viaduct Works. Earlestown, took place on Saturday at the Bee Hotel, Liverpool. The party which included several of the foremen left in a saloon by the 1.10 noon train and employed themselves in various ways during the afternoon until 6 o'clock at which time dinner was served. A capital menu was disposed of, and the after proceeding' were presided over by Mr. C. W. Emmett. in the absence, through indisposition of Mr. T. Orraand.l:Sergeant Swann'whe should have been present at the dinner was unavoidably delayed and did not put in an appearance until afterwards. In the course of the evening occasion was taken to present Sergeant Swann, who has volunteered as a trooper in the Imperial Yeomanry, with a pair of valu- able field glasses, on behalf of the foremen and officials of the Viaduct Works as a token of their esteem and pod wishes. Mr. C. W. Emmett, who made the presentation, said they had amongst them two gentlemen who were about to proceed to the front, one had been with them in their annual gatherings for many years past, and it was thought. when it became known that he had volunteered to become a member of Her Majesty's forces in S-ea,h Africa, that they could not possibly let him depart with- , out some little memento of their kindly feelings towards him. (Hear, hear.) It had always been said, and he believed, well said, that one volu ateer was worth 10 pressed men. (Hear, hear.) The present sitaa- tien of affairs, he thought had proved most conclusively that our Volunteer Army and our Yeomanry were not, as had been described very wrongly ":Saturday afternoen soldiers," and so far as the Yeo- manry were concerned "Cabbage cutters." (Lau,hter.) They had all a deep interest in thosewho were going for- , ward:to E Ty,ht the battles of our Queen and country,and althoueh they only expected to see one of their Ivolunteers present, yet he felt quite sure they would extend a most csrdial welcome to Sergeant Owbridge. (Hear, hear.) Who tk 9 originator of the happy thought was, he could not say, but when the idea was mooted of presenting some slight memento to their friend, Sergeant Swann, it required no pressing. It had been decided by those who had subscribed that the most suitable thing to give him was a pair of field glasses, and although they might be saying good-bye to him that night, they hoped in the near future to see him again in old England. He then formally presented him with the gllsses, and wished him a safe journey to South Africa, and hoped that he and Sergeant Owbridge might de their duty whilst there, and that within the next four months they would return with the epei's of victory. (Applause.) Sergeant Swann suitably thanked the subscribers for the handsome present, which, he bad no doubt would be very useful to him. (Hear, hear.) All present then joined in singing" Auld Lang Syne," and after much hand shaking the troopers departed. An excellent programme of songs was gone through, Messrs. Lea, S. Swann. J. Owen, Smalley and Sergeant Swann contributing. Mr. T. T. Roberts efficiently pre- sided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[l3l' A CORRESPONDENT.] Still the war fever continues to spread in Earles- town, and on Saturday night it apparently reached a culminating point, when, nearly SO 3 people as- sembled in Viaduct Institute and the Town Hall in response to the appeals of 'Air. Molyneux, who as a nrincipal attraction got up a tc-a and concert. The idea was a happ-y. one, and whilst enjoying thecr- selves the people were assisting in a practical man- ner, the wives and families of soldiers fighting for their country.</p>
        <p>Enthusiasm was also directed in another way after the concert. Nine or ten young fellows who are members of the Newton detachment of the Ist South Lancashire Volunteers have ered their ser- vices at the front and have been accepted. They hal to leave Earlestown on Saturday night for War- rington, and they had a send off which they will no doubt remember for a very long time. Humdreds of people collected and aclompanied the embryo soldiers to the station, which they packed, with such a crowd that the officials were unable to deal with and it was fortunate indeed that the platforms were ultimately cleared without accident.</p>
        <p>I wmoder what the position of the District Council will be in regard to the collection of tolls in the future. It appears that the appeal case against certain mineral water manufactwers from outside the district, who had refused to pay the usual toll, has gone against the Council,and the mineral water people's objection has thus been sustained. I fancy the Council, in view of this decision, will find it a difficult matter to enforce the payment of tolls under such circumstances in the future. They ap- peared to be stretlhing the law in the case under notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FOOTEA.LL,</head>
        <p>FOOTEA.LL,</p>
        <p>It is hard ieeed, t-o-u-n—d-er-s-tand the vagaries of the Earlestown team. For some weeks past they have played a most consistent game, and have showed themoelves a much improved team, but. on Saturday the hopes and aspirations of their supplr- ters were ruthlessly dashed. Horwich was cola- sidered fair game, and it was confidently expected they would succumb to Earlestown.</p>
        <p>Tha usual team was selected, but from one cause and another, only nine man put in an appearance at Harwich. Consequently, Flannigan had to play full back in place of Cottam, and a supporter and enthusiastic follower of the team was persuaded to play centre forward vice Linton. With such a scratch eleven it was not to be wondered that Hr- with asserted their superiority to the extent of two goals to nil. Chew the goal keeper, it must be said played a sterling game and it was due to his excel lent defence that the score was not heavier, and this in spite of the fact that he had entered the bonds of wedlock the same morning. Everybody i I am sure wishes him good luck n his new " sphere."</p>
        <p>Parr, who .was injured a few weeks ago in the match with Adlington, has now fully recovered, and he is most anxious to resume his usual position at centre half back. I think, however, the committee will be well advised if they adhere to the half back division which has done so well lately. If they lack weight, the deficiency is fully made up in youth and cleverness, and then there is always the fear that Pares leg may fail him again. To-morrow Southport Central will visit the Earlestown ground and in view of the fact that Southport defeated, Earlestown early on in the season by 5 goals to ill it is quite necessary that the Earlestown team should be as strong as possible.</p>
        <p>Newton had a difficult task on Saturday to dig- pose of Wirral railways, but they managed to secure victory by a solitary goal. Kenny, late of Sham- rocks, was tried for the first time. and be scored the only goal of the match, and it is quite likely his position in the team will be secured.</p>
        <p>The Rovers continue on their unbeaten path, and on Saturday they defeated Warrington St. Mary's by 4 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>The Albions had a retura visit from their name- sakes of fluyton, whom they disposed of by 3 goals to nil.</p>
        <p>RECORDS OF THE CLUBS.</p>
        <p>Gm%ls</p>
        <p>Pld. Won Lost Dm. For Agst. Earlestown New.,on-le-Willews lB ... 8 ... 7 ... 3 ... 45 ... 31 Earlestown Rove s l4 ... 11 ... 0 ... 3 ... 45 ... 8 Earlestown Albions</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AD STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE —The fourth public meeting of the session was held in Hill Cliffe Schoolroom on Monday evening. There was a fair attendance. The chair was occupied by the President, the Rev J S Hughes. A capital programme was creditably given. Miss Ada and Masters Percy and John Grounds sang very effectively as a trio " Ready," which was very much enjoyed. Later in the evening Miss A:4 and Master John Grounds sang with equal suceess the duet "Where are you going my pretty maid" Master Leonard Sutton's rich voice was heard to advantage in 'The Childret.'s Home" (with organ and violin aceompani, mlnt) and in response to an encore he sang with equal effect " The Flight of Ages." Mr George Whitfield gave in his usual effective style the humorous sketch " To- day or To-morrow " which crested roars of laughter. Recitations were exceedingly well given by the follow- ing members ti C Whitfield, Annie Dean, Bettie Isher- wood, J Whitfield, May Isherwood, Edith Cole, J Atkin- son, G Arthur Whitlield,J T Barton, Ada Brereton,Jessie Ellison, Thomas Brereton, L Isherwood, and Harold Moores. During the evening Mr. John Hallows gave a short and instructive address which was listened to very attentively. The dialogue " Sam Daggert's Wife "was admirably given by the following members Misses S J Worrell, Clara Kenworthy, and Messrs George Moss, B Kenworthy, W Wild, J Worrell, S Moss W Hallam, E Wild and B MOON. Miss Ada Grounds and Mr A B Ken- worthy ably acted as accompanists. Several pledges were taken and the singing of the doxology brought a very pleasant evening to a (liege.</p>
        <p>- ASK or CUNNINGHAM' HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt</p>
        <p>. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WARRINGTON SCHOOL GIRLS' BAZAAR. A Satisfactory Result.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SCHOOL GIRLS' BAZAAR. A Satisfactory Result.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Hall in the course of its history has been used for many and various functions, but never, perhaps, has it been the scene of such a crowded and busy throng as that which attended the School Okla' Bazaar held within its walls on Tuesday and Wednesday. All the avail- able apace was utilised, bat still there was scarcely enough to meet the exigencies of the occasion, and had it been anticipated that the bazaar would have been such a success, a larger room would have been engaged. The bazaar was decided upon at a meet- ing of school girls held two months ago, and the Misses Ethel U. Roberts and Ethel Lyon Whittle were appointed the hon. secretaries. Since that time the arrangements have progressed satisfac- torily and the school girls have been generously supported by the mistresses and teachers at the various schools. The result of their labours was seen in the choice array of articles which were offered for sale on Tuesday. It was apparent that it would be impossible to dispose of the articles at one day's sale as was originally intended, and it was at once decided to extend the bazaar over a second day. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The stalls had be3n tastefully arranged in the Council Chamber, the entrance hall and one of the committee rooms. Small Union Jacks wore displayed in profusion and the manner in which the stalls had been draped with decorative material gave them an effective appear- aace. School girls were present in great numbers and with bright faces and beaming smiles eadeavoured to dispose of their wares. The following schools were re- presented : Wycliffe, Wash-lane (Latchford), St. Thomas's (Stockton Heath), St. James (Latchford), Mrs Reynolds, the Misses Williams' (Bold-street), the Misses Rimnier (Wilson Patten-screet), St. Barnabas', Orford, Miss (14egh-street), Miss Fieldhouse's, Heath Side, Miss Campbells, Hamilton-street and Fairfield, People's College, SAnkey, and St. Mary's. The Heat') Side girls were in charge of a flower stall, Miss Campbell a refreshment stall, and the Misses Williams (Bold-street) a Japanese stall. The other stalls were stockcd with a choice assortment of miscellaneous articles. In connec- tion with the Fairfield School stall there was a fish pond. The tea room was under the management of Mrs Po wis. Mr Gordon Roberts had the direction of a tlJeatre and some amusing attractions were here provided. Waehinz competitions,hat trimming competitions and comb play- ing contests were participated in and were immensely popular.</p>
        <p>inert) was a crowded attendance when the sale was opened. The MAYOR, in introducir g the Mayoras, said it was extremely gratifying to all of them to see so large an at- tendance, in spite of the inclement weather. Be did not desire to say anything about the war itself, but one thing was quite certain, and that was that however much they deplored the effects of the war, it, had brought out some of the noblest sentiments of which the English people were capable. (Applause.) They saw the sons at the aristocracy and the sons of working men fighting shoulder to shoulder in South Africa in defence of the rights of her Majesty the Queen, and on the ether hand every section ef the commueity had telt a desire to do something to render th condition of the soldiers and those dependent upon them as comfortable and as happy as was possible. And last, but not least, the school girls of Warrington had set an example which might very properly be followed in many other towns by an endeavour to do their share in providing the neces- sary means for making the homes of the soldiers who had left this district comfortable in their absence. Is that endeavour they bad been very ably supported by the mistresses of the various schools,to whom he wished to pay his personal acknowledgment for their very valuable services. The stalls were eloquent testimony Ito the industry of the school girls, and an evidence of the abundant success of their efferts. (Applause.) All he had new to do was to ask the Mayoress to open the sale of work, and to ask the ladies to all do their share in buying everything there was to be seen. (Applause.) The MAYORESS said it was very greatly her desire that the openine ceremony should have been performed by Isomeone more capable than she. Since, however, it was the unanimous wish of the school girls that she should do it, it was her pleasure to please them. She did so with the mere courage because she was deeply sensible if the great kindnesses she had received, and which had been shown in so many ways this last two months by the ladies of Warrington. She was delighted to see se many people there, and she thought the effort of their 'school girls was worthy of all praise, (Applause.) She felt that this was a lesson of self-denial, the girls having given up their winter holidays in order to prepare for the sale. Not only was it a lesson of self-denial but also else of kind thoughtfulness for the needs of others that would de the girls good, and she was quite sure they I would never regret it. Nay, rather she thought each would have a pride in her part of the success of the sale. She knew the visitors ware present with a desire to help them and she had areal pleasure in declaring the sale open. (Applause.) Miss Jennie Chantry, from the Misses Rimmer's school then steeped forward and presented a beautiful bouquet to the Mayoress. The proceeds of the first day's sale amounted to £197 18s 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-19_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Wednesday's Proceedings.</head>
        <p>Wednesday's Proceedings.</p>
        <p>The bazaar was again opened on Wednesday after- noon, but the attendance at the opening ceremony was small in comparison with that of the opening day. Miss Ridgway, daughter of Colonel Ridgway, performed the opening ceremony, and she was accompanied by the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Colonel Ridgway, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk) and Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). In introducing Miss Ridgway, the MAYOR said it afforded him the greatest possible pleasure to offer to Miss Ridgway on their behalf and his own a very hearty and sincere welcome to their proceedings, and to ask her at the same time to be good enough to open the sale of work. Not only did they honour her because of the name she bore which was associated with a family wbo had been so long and honourably connected with the district but also because her father had taken an honourable and useful part in connection with our Vol- unteer force—a force of which they were particularly proud at the present time and in regard to which they would have an opportunity before long of showing the high esteem in which they held them from the Colonel down to the humblest man in the ranks. (Applause.) Miss RIDGWAY, who was cordially received, said she was sorry that owing to a severe cold her mother wail unable to be present that day. She felt it a great com- pliment to be asked to open that sale of work associated as it was with the Mayor's Fund for the help our soldiers who had so gallantly reeponded to their country's Gall. It was their duty, and one they were willing to take, to help those whom their soldiers had left behind. (Hear. hear.) She had been a ache.' girl herself and she knew that the attention that their lessons required of them left them with very little time for outside work. She felt sure that everyone thoroughly appreciated the very nice things which were in that room and which had been there during the bazaar. She hoped that no one would go away without empty purees if only for the sake of helping and repaying the girls for the time the had spent. She had now very great plea- sure in declaring the sale open. Miss Ida Silcock then handed a beautiful bouquet to Miss Ridgway, who acknowledged the gift in suitable terms.</p>
