        <p>COUNTY OF CHESTER WEIGHTS AND MEASURES ACT. BYE-LAWS REGULATING THE SALE OF COAL. NoncE is HEREBY GIVEN, that the following BYE-LAWS regulating the Sale of Coal within the District under the jurisdiction of the County Council of the County of Cheater were adopted at a Quarterly Meeting of the said Council. held at Chester Castle, on Friday, the 10th day ofNOVEMBER,IB99, and approved by the Board of Trade on the 28th day of December 1899. and therefore now in operation, viz :- I.—Every persan upon whose behalf Coal, in quantities not exceeding two hundred-weight, is hawked, sold or exposed. for sale by retail, shall cause his name and place of business to be registered at the Head Office of the Weights and Measures Department of the County Council at Chester Castle, and on each and every occasion on which he shall, change his registered place of business he shall, within four- teen days thereafter, cause to be communicated to the said office particulars of such change. 2.—Every person who shall sell or offer for sale or de- liver to a purchaser or shall cause to be sold or offered for sale or delivered to a purchaser, from any vehicle, brie, or ether beast of burden, Coal in quantities not exceeding two hundred-weight shall carry, or cause to be carried, such Coal in sacks, bags or other receptacles containing either two hundred-weight, one bundred-weight, half a hundred-weight, or a quarter of a hundred- weight ; and the tep of every ouch sack, bag, or other receptacle, shall :have permanently fastened to it a metal label indicating the correct weight of Coal therein ; and no person shall sell or offer for sale, or deliver to a purchaser, or shall cause to be sold or offered for sale or delivered to a purchaser, Coal which is of leas weight than that specified on the label attached to such sack, bag, or other receptacle. 3.—Every person who shall sell or offer for sale, or de- liver to a purchaser or shall cause to be sold or offered for sale or delivered to a purchaser, Coal in sacks, bags, or other receptacles, from any vehicle, horse or ether beast of burden, in or on which Coal is carried for sale or delivery to more than one pur- chaser, shall carry or cause to be carried therewith, a correct weighing instrument of a form approved by the Local Authority, and duly stamped by az Inspector for this County, together with the neces- sary correctiweights,and the person in charge of such vehicle, horse, or other beast of burden, shall re- weigh the Coal upon being requested to do so by the purchaser, or by anyone on behalf of the purchaser, or by an Inspector of Weights and Measures. 4—Every person who shall sell or offer for sale, or de- liver to a purchaser, or cause to be sold or offered for sale or delivered to a purchaser, from any vehicle, Coal in quantities not exceeding two hundred-weight shall have clearly and legibly painted upon a conspicuous part of the right hand side, or of the front, of such vehicle, in letters not less than one hash in height and of proportionate breadth, the name and place of business of the person on whose behalf such Ceal is sold or offered for sale or delivered to a purchaser, and shall keep the same leeib'e and undefaced. s.—Every person who shall sell, or cause to be sold, at any wharf, yard, or other place, Coal in quantities not exceeding two hundred-weight, shall sell, or cause to be sold, such Coal in quantities of either two hundred-Weight, one hundred-weight, half a hundred-weight, or a quarter of a hundred-weight, and shall weigh, or cause to be weighed, such shoal by means of a correct weighing instrument duly verified and stamped by an Inspector of Weights and Measures for this County, and if required, shall re-weigh, or cause to be re-weighed, the same in the presence of the purchaser, or of any one on behalf of the purchaser, or of an Inspector of Weights and Measures. B.—Every person who shall sell or deliver to a purchaser, or shall cause to be sold or delivered to a purchaser, Coal from a vehicle, shill carry such Coal for the purpose of being weighed or re-weighed any dis- tance not exceeding half a mile, as may be required by an Inspector of Weights and Measures. 7.—The penalty of every breach, contravention, or non- observance of any one or any part of any one of the foregoing Byre-laws shall be any sum not exceeding five pounds. ll.—From and after the date of the confirmation of these Bye-laws the Bye-laws which were made by the County Council on the sth day of February, 1891, and confirmed by the Board of Trade on the 24th day of Aprll, 1891, stall be repealed. The County Council alsa at its Meeting on the said 11th day of November, 1899, pursuant to section 22 (1) of the Weights and Measures Act, 1869, approved of the following manner of marking the tare weight of a vehicle conveying for delivery on Sale any quantity of Coal ex- ceeding two hundred-weight, viz The tare weight shall be properly and legibly marked and fastened upon the right hand side of the vehicle by means of a metal plate or plates, each figure to be painted white upon a dark ground or black upon a white ground, and to be not less than one inch deep. REGINALD POTTS, Clerk to the Cheshire County Council. Chester, 10th January, 1900.</p>
